hadiths[664]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17641","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ described a king among those before you who reflected that his dominion would end and had distracted him from worshipping his Lord, so he slipped from his palace one night into another kingdom and settled on the seacoast making bricks, eating from his labour and giving the surplus in charity until his worship reached the local king, who summoned him twice and was refused, then rode out himself while the man fled until assured of safety, whereupon he revealed his identity and the second king abandoned his mount and joined him, and both worshipped Allāh until they prayed to die together and died, and Ibn Masʿūd said if he were at Rumaylat Miṣr he would show their graves by the description of the Prophet ﷺ. > **""I reflected upon my affair and knew that what I possessed would end and had distracted me from worshipping my Lord, so I left it and came here to worship my Lord.""**"
"17642","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the Children of Isrāʾīl appointed a successor after Mūsā, peace be upon him, who prayed one moonlit night above Bayt al-Maqdis, recalled his deeds, descended by a rope that remained hanging in the mosque by morning, journeyed to people on the seashore making bricks, joined them for wages, ate from his handiwork, prayed at prayer times until the workers reported him to their overseer, refused three summons, fled when the overseer rode out, then stopped at his plea, told him he had been a king fleeing awe of his Lord, so the overseer said he would join him, and they worshipped Allāh until they died at Rumaylat Miṣr, and Ibn Masʿūd said he could find their graves by the Prophet's ﷺ description. > **""I was a king, and I fled out of awe of my Lord.""**"
"17643","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported concerning the verse Alif Lām Rā, those are the verses of the clear Book, We sent it down as an Arabic Qurʾān, We relate to you the best of stories, that the Qurʾān came down upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and he recited it to them for a time, so they said O Messenger of Allāh, if only you would narrate stories to us, and Allāh sent down that opening, then they said if only you would speak to us, and Allāh sent down Allāh has sent down the best discourse, a Book resembling itself, and in each case they were commanded to hold to the Qurʾān, and Khallād added that others reported they said if only you would remind us, so Allāh sent down Has not the time come for those who believe that their hearts submit to the remembrance of Allāh and the truth sent down. > **""We relate to you the best of stories.""**"
"17644","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Jibrīl came to the Prophet ﷺ and addressed him as Muḥammad, commanding him to live as he wills for he shall die, to act as he wills for he shall be recompensed, to love whom he wills for he shall part from him, and to know that the honour of the believer lies in night prayer and his dignity in independence from people. > **""Live as you wish, for you shall die; act as you wish, for you shall be recompensed for it; love whom you wish, for you shall part from him; and know that the honour of the believer is his prayer by night, and his dignity is his independence from people.""**"
"17645","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Jibrīl, peace be upon him, told him to love whom he wills for he shall part, to act as he wills for he shall meet it, and to live as he wills for he shall die, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ added that Jibrīl had made the sermon concise for him. > **""Love whom you wish, for you shall part from him; act as you wish, for you shall meet it; and live as you wish, for you shall die.""**"
"17646","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ gave this nation glad tidings of splendour, eminence, religion, victory and establishment on earth, with doubt about the third item, and then warned that whoever among them performs a deed of the Hereafter for the world shall have no share in the Hereafter. > **""Give this nation glad tidings of splendour, eminence, religion, victory and establishment on earth; yet whoever among them performs a deed of the Hereafter for the world shall have no share in the Hereafter.""**"
"17647","Al-Jārūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever seeks the world through a deed of the Hereafter shall have his face effaced, his remembrance erased, and his name established in the Fire. > **""Whoever seeks the world through a deed of the Hereafter, his face shall be effaced, his remembrance erased, and his name established in the Fire.""**"
"17648","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say whoever beautifies himself with a deed of the Hereafter while neither intending it nor seeking it shall be cursed in the heavens and the earths. > **""Whoever adorns himself with a deed of the Hereafter while he neither intends it nor seeks it shall be cursed in the heavens and the earths.""**"
"17649","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that on the Day of Resurrection some people shall be ordered toward Paradise until they draw near, smell its fragrance, see its palaces and what Allāh prepared for its people, then shall be called to turn away for they have no share in it, and shall return with unmatched regret, saying O our Lord, had You entered us into the Fire before showing us Your reward it would have been easier, and Allāh shall say that was His intent for them, for in solitude they confronted Him with grave sins, met people humbly showing them what opposed their hearts, feared and honoured people rather than Him, abandoned for people rather than Him, so today He shall make them taste painful punishment along with deprivation of reward. > **""You would confront Me in solitude with grave sins, and meet people humbly, showing people what opposed what you gave Me from your hearts; you feared people and did not fear Me, honoured people and did not honour Me, abandoned for people and did not abandon for Me; so today I shall make you taste painful punishment along with what I have denied you of reward.""**"
"17650","ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr reported that he came to his father and told him he had found people unsurpassed in goodness who remembered Allāh until one trembled as if fainting from fear of Allāh, and had sat with them, so his father told him not to sit with them again, and seeing hesitation said he had seen the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and his Companions recite the Qurʾān without such seizures, asking whether those people were more fearful of Allāh than Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, whereupon ʿĀmir saw the point and left them. > **""I saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and his Companions recite the Qurʾān and this did not seize them; do you deem them more fearful of Allāh than Abū Bakr and ʿUmar?""**"
"17651","Ibn Ghannam reported that he and Abū al-Dardāʾ entered the mosque of al-Jābiya, met ʿUbāda ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with them, who walked between them holding their hands while they conversed privately, and warned that long life would show a man from the midst of the Muslims reciting the Qurʾān as upon the tongue of Muḥammad ﷺ, repeating it, declaring its lawful and unlawful and abiding by its stations, yet counting among them like the head of a dead donkey, then Shaddād ibn Aws and ʿAwf ibn Mālik arrived and Shaddād said his greatest fear for people was what he heard from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ about hidden lust and shirk, and when ʿUbāda and Abū al-Dardāʾ asked for pardon and recalled that Satan had despaired of worship in Arabia and that worldly lusts were known, Shaddād asked whether prayer, fasting or charity done for a man was shirk and they swore it was, so he reported the Prophet ﷺ said whoever prays, fasts or gives charity for show has committed shirk, and when ʿAwf asked whether Allāh would not accept the sincere portion, Shaddād reported the Prophet ﷺ said Allāh declares He is the best partner for one who associates with Him, leaving all his work, little and much, to the partner, being free of it. > **""Whoever prays for show has committed shirk, whoever fasts for show has committed shirk, and whoever gives charity for show has committed shirk; Allāh says: I am the best partner for whoever associates with Me — whoever associates anything with Me, his work, little and much, belongs to the partner he associated, and I am free of it.""**"
"17652","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Qays, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh, blessed and exalted, declares He is the best partner, so whoever associates a partner with Him, the deed belongs to His partner, then commanded people to purify their deeds for Allāh, for He accepts only what is pure for Him, and not to say this is for Allāh and for kinship, for it shall be for kinship with nothing for Allāh, nor this is for Allāh and for your faces, for it shall be for their faces with nothing for Allāh. > **""O people, purify your deeds for Allāh, for Allāh accepts of deeds only what is pure for Him; do not say: this is for Allāh and for kinship, for it belongs to kinship and nothing of it belongs to Allāh.""**"
"17653","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever perfects prayer where people see him and ruins it in solitude commits a belittling by which he belittles his Lord, blessed and exalted. > **""Whoever perfects prayer where people see him and ruins it in solitude, that is a belittling by which he belittles his Lord.""**"
"17654","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the son of Ādam shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection as if a bundle — or perhaps a load — and Allāh shall say O son of Ādam, I am the best divider, look at your work done for Me and I shall reward you for it, and look at your work done for other than Me and let him reward you for what you did for him. > **""O son of Ādam, I am the best divider; look at your work you did for Me and I shall reward you for it, and look at your work you did for other than Me and let him reward you for what you did for him.""**"
"17655","Shaddād ibn Aws, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ they counted showing off as the gravest shirk, while al-Bazzār's wording has it as the lesser shirk. > **""We used to count showing off in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ as shirk.""**"
"17656","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said when the last age comes his nation shall become three groups: a group worshipping Allāh purely, a group worshipping Allāh for show, and a group worshipping Allāh to devour people thereby, and the rest of the report is given in full in the book of resurrection under reckoning. > **""When the last age comes, my nation shall become three groups: a group worshipping Allāh purely, a group worshipping Allāh for show, and a group worshipping Allāh to devour people thereby.""**"
"17657","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the thing he fears most for them is the lesser shirk, and when asked what it was said it is showing off, and it shall be said to its people when men come with their deeds to go to those they showed off to and seek it with them. > **""The most fearful thing I fear for you is the lesser shirk — showing off. It shall be said to whoever does that when people come with their deeds: go to those you used to show off to, and seek it with them.""**"
"17658","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that in Hell there is a valley from which Hell itself seeks refuge four hundred times each day, prepared for the show-offs among the nation of Muḥammad ﷺ: the bearer of Allāh's Book, those giving charity for other than Allāh, the pilgrim to Allāh's House, and the one going out in Allāh's path. > **""In Hell there is a valley from which Hell seeks refuge four hundred times each day, prepared for the show-offs among the nation of Muḥammad ﷺ.""**"
"17659","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the world is cursed and what is in it is cursed, except what is sought thereby of the Face of Allāh, mighty and majestic. > **""The world is cursed, and what is in it is cursed, except what is sought thereby of the Face of Allāh.""**"
"17660","ʿAmr ibn Murra reported from an elder called Abū Yazīd that he sat with ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them, when Ibn ʿUmar said Satan flows through the son of Ādam like blood and spirit, so Ibn ʿAmr wept and said he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say whoever publicises his deed to people, Allāh shall publicise him before the hearing of His creation, and belittle and abase him. > **""Whoever publicises his deed to people, Allāh shall publicise him before the hearing of His creation, and belittle him and abase him.""**"
"17661","Abū Bakra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever seeks repute, Allāh shall make him reputed, and whoever shows off, Allāh shall show him. > **""Whoever seeks repute, Allāh shall make him reputed, and whoever shows off, Allāh shall show him.""**"
"17662","ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say whoever stands in a station of showing off, Allāh shall show him, and whoever stands in a station of seeking repute, Allāh shall make him reputed. > **""Whoever stands in a station of showing off, Allāh shall show him, and whoever stands in a station of seeking repute, Allāh shall make him reputed.""**"
"17663","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that no servant stands in the world in a station of repute and show except that Allāh shall make him reputed before the heads of creation on the Day of Resurrection. > **""No servant stands in the world in a station of repute and show except that Allāh shall make him reputed before the heads of creation on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17664","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays al-Khuzāʿī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever stands for show and repute remains under Allāh's wrath until he sits down. > **""Whoever stands for show and repute remains under Allāh's wrath until he sits down.""**"
"17665","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said as a stopped report that whoever seeks repute, Allāh shall make him reputed, and whoever shows off, Allāh shall show him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever humbles himself for Allāh in modesty, Allāh shall raise him on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever seeks repute, Allāh shall make him reputed; whoever shows off, Allāh shall show him on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever humbles himself for Allāh in modesty, Allāh shall raise him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17666","Abū Hind al-Dārī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say whoever stands in a station of show and repute, Allāh shall show him and make him reputed on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever stands in a station of show and repute, Allāh shall show him and make him reputed on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17667","Abū Hind al-Dārī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say whoever shows off with Allāh for other than Allāh, Allāh shall disown him. > **""Whoever shows off with Allāh for other than Allāh, Allāh shall disown him.""**"
"17668","ʿĀʾisha, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said shirk is more hidden in his nation than the crawling of an ant upon smooth rock. > **""Shirk is more hidden in my nation than the crawling of an ant upon smooth rock.""**"
"17669","Abū ʿAlī, a man of Banū Kāhil, reported that Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, addressed them saying O people, fear this shirk for it is more hidden than an ant's crawling, whereupon ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥazn and Qays ibn al-Muḍārib demanded proof or they would complain to ʿUmar, with or without permission, so Abū Mūsā cited the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ addressing them one day with the same warning, and when asked how to guard against what is more hidden than an ant's crawling, taught them to say O Allāh, we seek refuge in You from knowingly associating anything with You, and seek Your forgiveness for what we do not know. > **""O Allāh, we seek refuge in You from associating anything with You that we know, and we seek Your forgiveness for what we do not know.""**"
"17670","Ḥudhayfa reported from Abū Bakr, may Allāh be pleased with them — either Ḥudhayfa attended it from the Prophet ﷺ or Abū Bakr informed him — that the Prophet ﷺ said shirk among you is more hidden than an ant's crawling, and when they asked whether shirk is anything but worshipping or calling upon other than Allāh, he said may your mother mourn you, O Ṣiddīq, shirk among you is more hidden than an ant's crawling, then offered words removing its small and great: to say three times daily O Allāh, I seek refuge in You from knowingly associating anything with You, and seek forgiveness for what I do not know, and explained that shirk includes saying Allāh and so-and-so gave me, and a rival is one's saying had it not been for so-and-so, so-and-so would have killed me. > **""Say three times daily: O Allāh, I seek refuge in You from knowingly associating anything with You, and I seek Your forgiveness for what I do not know.""**"
"17671","Maʿqil ibn Yasār, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he attended the Prophet ﷺ with Abū Bakr — or Abū Bakr informed him from the Prophet ﷺ — that shirk among you is more hidden than an ant's crawling, then asked shall I not guide you to what removes its small and great: to say O Allāh, I seek refuge in You from associating with You while I know, and seek Your forgiveness for what I do not know. > **""Say: O Allāh, I seek refuge in You from associating with You while I know, and I seek Your forgiveness for what I do not know.""**"
"17672","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿIṣma ibn Fātik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever endears himself to people by what they love while defying Allāh shall meet Allāh on the Day of Resurrection while He is angry with him. > **""Whoever endears himself to people by what they love while defying Allāh shall meet Allāh on the Day of Resurrection while He is angry with him.""**"
"17673","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever endears himself to people by what they love while defying Allāh with what He hates shall meet Allāh while He is angry with him. > **""Whoever endears himself to people by what they love while defying Allāh with what He hates shall meet Allāh while He is angry with him.""**"
"17674","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever angers Allāh for people's pleasure, Allāh shall be angry with him and shall anger with him those he pleased in His anger, and whoever pleases Allāh amid people's anger, Allāh shall be pleased with him and shall please with him those angered in His pleasure, until He beautifies him and his word and deed in their eyes. > **""Whoever angers Allāh for people's pleasure, Allāh shall be angry with him and shall anger with him those he pleased; and whoever pleases Allāh amid people's anger, Allāh shall be pleased with him and shall please with him those he angered.""**"
"17675","ʿĀʾisha, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever seeks people's praise through disobedience to Allāh, his praiser shall turn into a blamer. > **""Whoever seeks people's praise through disobedience to Allāh, his praiser shall turn into a blamer.""**"
"17676","Jundub ibn Sufyān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said no servant conceals a hidden state except that Allāh clothes him with its cloak: if good then good, and if evil then evil. > **""No servant conceals a hidden state except that Allāh clothes him with its cloak: if good then good, and if evil then evil.""**"
"17677","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he said to a man come, let us devote this day of ours to Allāh, mighty and majestic, and it was as if the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ witnessed that day, for he addressed the people saying among them is one who says come, let us devote this day of ours to Allāh, and kept repeating it until Abū Mūsā wished the earth would swallow him. > **""Among them is one who says: come, let us devote this day of ours to Allāh.""**"
"17678","In another wording Abū Mūsā reported that the Prophet ﷺ kept repeating that phrase until he wished he could sink into the earth. > **""He kept repeating it until I wished I could sink into the earth.""**"
"17679","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that were one of you to act inside a deaf rock with neither door nor window, his deed would emerge to people, whatever it was. > **""Were one of you to act inside a deaf rock with neither door nor window, his deed would emerge to people, whatever it was.""**"
"17680","ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were a man to fall upon his face from the day he was born until the day he dies seeking Allāh's pleasure, he would deem it little on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Were a man to fall upon his face from the day he was born until the day he dies seeking Allāh's pleasure, he would deem it little on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17681","Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿUmayra, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, said as a stopped report that were a man to fall upon his face from birth until death in old age seeking Allāh's pleasure, he would deem it little on that Day and wish to be returned to the world to increase in reward. > **""He would deem it little on that Day and wish to be returned to the world to increase in reward.""**"
"17682","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to beware of pride, for pride may dwell in a man even when a mere cloak is upon him. > **""Beware of pride, for pride may dwell in a man even when a mere cloak is upon him.""**"
"17683","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that in Hell there is a valley called Habhab, and it is binding upon Allāh to lodge every tyrant in it. > **""In Hell there is a valley called Habhab; it is binding upon Allāh to lodge every tyrant in it.""**"
"17684","ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn Maʿqil reported that he heard Wahb addressing people on the pulpit saying remember three things from me: beware of followed desire, an evil companion, and a man's admiration of himself. > **""Beware of followed desire, an evil companion, and a man's admiration of himself.""**"
"17685","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said four things belong to wretchedness: a tearless eye, a hard heart, prolonged hope, and greed for the world. > **""Four belong to wretchedness: a tearless eye, a hard heart, prolonged hope, and greed for the world.""**"
"17686","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that it was asked O Messenger of Allāh, which of our companions are best, and he said the one whose sight reminds you of Allāh, whose speech increases you in deeds, and whose work reminds you of the Hereafter. > **""The one whose sight reminds you of Allāh, whose speech increases you in deeds, and whose work reminds you of the Hereafter.""**"
"17687","Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī reported from Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, with doubt whether raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that when you are reminded by Allāh, then desist. > **""When you are reminded by Allāh, then desist.""**"
"17688","Burayda, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there is nothing but that it is more obedient to Allāh, blessed and exalted, than the son of Ādam. > **""There is nothing but that it is more obedient to Allāh than the son of Ādam.""**"
"17689","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh's angels know the sons of Ādam and their deeds, so when they see a servant working in obedience to Allāh they mention him among themselves by name saying tonight so-and-so has prospered, tonight so-and-so is saved, and when they see a servant working in disobedience they mention him by name saying tonight so-and-so has perished. > **""When they see a servant working in obedience to Allāh they say: tonight so-and-so has prospered; and when they see a servant working in disobedience they say: tonight so-and-so has perished.""**"
"17690","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said slowly, for Allāh is severe in punishment, and were it not for nursing infants, bowing men and grazing beasts, punishment would be poured upon you — or sent down upon you — while al-Ṭabarānī's wording mentions humble youths, bowing elders, nursing children and grazing beasts, that punishment would be poured then crushed. > **""Slowly, for Allāh is severe in punishment; were it not for nursing infants, bowing men and grazing beasts, punishment would be poured upon you.""**"
"17691","Musāfiʿ al-Dīlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were it not for Allāh's bowing servants, nursing children and grazing beasts, punishment would be poured upon you, then crushed. > **""Were it not for Allāh's bowing servants, nursing children and grazing beasts, punishment would be poured upon you, then crushed.""**"
"17692","Al-Ḥakam ibn Mīnāʾ reported that the Prophet ﷺ told ʿUmar to gather those present of Quraysh, so he gathered them and asked whether he should go out or they enter, and he went out saying O company of Quraysh, is there any stranger among you, and they said none except sons of our sisters, so he said the son of a people's sister is of them, then said know that the most deserving of the Prophet are the pious, so take care lest people come with deeds on Resurrection Day while you come carrying the world and he turns his face from you, then recited that the most deserving of Ibrāhīm are those who followed him, this Prophet and those who believed, and Allāh is the Guardian of the believers. > **""O company of Quraysh, know that the most deserving of the Prophet are the pious; take care lest people come with deeds on the Day of Resurrection while you come carrying the world, and I turn my face from you.""**"
"17693","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that he said O sons of Quṣayy, O sons of ʿAbd Manāf, I am the warner, death is the raider, and the Hour is the appointment. > **""O sons of Quṣayy, O sons of ʿAbd Manāf, I am the warner, death is the raider, and the Hour is the appointment.""**"
"17694","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said O son of Ādam, act as if you see, count yourself among the dead, and beware the supplication of the oppressed. > **""O son of Ādam, act as if you see, count yourself among the dead, and beware the supplication of the oppressed.""**"
"17695","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said I am Allāh's messenger to you, know that the return is to Allāh, then to Paradise or to Fire, and it is a stay without departure and eternity without death, in bodies that do not die. > **""Know that the return is to Allāh, then to Paradise or to Fire; it is a stay without departure and eternity without death, in bodies that do not die.""**"
"17696","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said today is training and tomorrow the race, with the goal Paradise or Fire, and the ruined is whoever enters the Fire; I am first, Abū Bakr the next, ʿUmar third, and people after me by precedence, first then first. > **""Today is training and tomorrow the race, and the goal is Paradise or Fire; the ruined is whoever enters the Fire.""**"
"17697","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh has divided your characters among you as He divided your provisions, and gives the world to whom He loves and whom He does not love, but gives religion only to whom He loves, so whomsoever Allāh gives religion has loved him; and by Him in whose Hand is his soul, no servant submits until his heart and tongue submit to Allāh, nor believes until his neighbour is safe from his wrongs, and when asked what his wrongs are said his tyranny and injustice; and no servant earns wealth unlawfully then spends it but it is blessed, nor gives it in charity but it is accepted, nor leaves it behind except as provision to the Fire; for Allāh does not erase evil with evil but erases evil with good, and the impure does not erase the impure. > **""Allāh has divided your characters among you as He divided your provisions; Allāh does not erase evil with evil but erases evil with good, and the impure does not erase the impure.""**"
"17698","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allāh be pleased with him, as known to Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said beware of belittled sins, for their likeness is a people camping in a valley bottom, each bringing a stick until they bake their bread, and when their owner is seized for them they destroy him; and Abū Ḥāzim reported, through Abū Ḍamra from Sahl, that the Prophet ﷺ separated his middle finger from the one next to the thumb saying my likeness and the Hour are like these two, and like a man sent by his people as a scout who, fearing to be overtaken, waves his garment crying they have come to you, they have come to you, then said I am he. > **""Beware of belittled sins; when their owner is seized for them they destroy him. My likeness and the Hour are like these two — I am the warner.""**"
"17699","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said O Banū Hāshim, O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, O Ṣafiyya aunt of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, O Fāṭima daughter of Muḥammad ﷺ, let me not find that people come tomorrow carrying the Hereafter while you come carrying the world; my allies among you on Resurrection Day are the pious; my likeness and yours is a man kindling light among his people who cries O people, you have been come upon, morning danger, I am the warner, death the destroyer, the Hour the appointment. > **""Let me not find that people come tomorrow carrying the Hereafter while you come carrying the world; my allies among you on the Day of Resurrection are the pious.""**"
"17700","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ addressed them upon his she-camel al-ʿAḍbāʾ — not al-Jadʿāʾ — saying O people, it is as if death therein is decreed for others, truth therein obligatory upon others, as if the dead we escort are travellers soon returning to us, lodging them in graves and consuming their inheritance as if immortal after them, having forgotten every admonition and felt safe from every calamity; blessed is whoever is busied by his own fault from people's faults, humbles himself for Allāh without deficiency, spends from wealth gathered without disobedience, mixes with the learned, avoids doubters and innovators, keeps his outward sound, and isolates people from his evil. > **""It is as if death therein is decreed for others and truth therein obligatory upon others; blessed is whoever is busied by his own fault from people's faults.""**"
"17701","Rakb al-Miṣrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said blessed is whoever is humble without deficiency, abases himself without misery, spends wealth gathered without disobedience, shows mercy to the lowly and needy, and mixes with the learned and wise; blessed is whoever has pure earnings, sound inward, noble outward, and isolates people from his evil; blessed is whoever acts upon his knowledge, spends the surplus of his wealth, and withholds the surplus of his speech. > **""Blessed is whoever is humble without deficiency, has pure earnings, a sound inward and a noble outward, acts upon his knowledge, spends the surplus of his wealth, and withholds the surplus of his speech.""**"
"17702","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking for a brief report, so the Prophet ﷺ told him to pray the prayer of one departing, for though he does not see Him, He sees him; to despair of what is in people's hands and be rich; and to avoid what requires apology. > **""Pray the prayer of one departing, for though you do not see Him, He sees you; despair of what is in people's hands and you shall be rich; and beware what requires apology.""**"
"17703","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said what is with people that they honour the luxurious, belittle worshippers, act upon the Qurʾān where it suits their desires and abandon what opposes them, thus believing in part of the Book and disbelieving in part, striving for what is attained without striving — the decreed decree, written term and apportioned provision — while not striving for what is attained only by striving: the ample recompense, the thanked striving, and the trade that never perishes. > **""What is with people that they honour the luxurious and belittle worshippers, acting upon the Qurʾān where it suits their desires and abandoning what opposes them?""**"
"17704","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say the Fire has been stoked and Paradise brought near, O people of the chambers, if you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much. > **""The Fire has been stoked and Paradise brought near; O people of the chambers, if you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much.""**"
"17705","Ibn Umm Maktūm, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ came out one morning saying the Fire has been stoked for its people and trials come like pieces of dark night; if you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much. > **""The Fire has been stoked for its people and trials come like pieces of dark night; if you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much.""**"
"17706","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that if you knew what he knows you would laugh little and weep much, and would go out to the open roads crying out to Allāh, not knowing whether you shall be saved or not. > **""If you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much, and would go out to the open roads crying out to Allāh, not knowing whether you shall be saved or not.""**"
"17707","Samura ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say if you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much. > **""If you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much.""**"
"17708","Kulayb ibn Ḥazn, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say O people, seek Paradise and flee the Fire with your utmost effort, for the seeker of Paradise does not sleep nor does the fleer from the Fire; the Hereafter today is veiled in hardships and the world is veiled in lusts. > **""O people, seek Paradise and flee the Fire with your utmost effort; the Hereafter today is veiled in hardships and the world is veiled in lusts.""**"
"17709","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he never saw the like of Paradise whose seeker sleeps, nor the like of the Fire whose fleer sleeps. > **""I never saw the like of Paradise whose seeker sleeps, nor the like of the Fire whose fleer sleeps.""**"
"17710","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say to Kaʿb ibn ʿUjra that prayer is devotion, fasting a shield, and charity extinguishes sin as water extinguishes fire; O Kaʿb ibn ʿUjra, people go out in two mornings, people go out in two ways: one selling himself and destroying his neck, and one buying himself and freeing his neck. > **""O Kaʿb ibn ʿUjra, prayer is devotion, fasting is a shield, and charity extinguishes sin as water extinguishes fire; people go out in two ways: one selling himself and destroying his neck, and one buying himself and freeing his neck.""**"
"17711","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said O Kaʿb ibn ʿUjra, when rulers come, whoever enters upon them, confirms their lies and aids their injustice is not of him nor he of him, and shall not come to the Pond, while whoever enters without confirming lies or aiding injustice is of him and he of him; O Kaʿb, no flesh or blood grown from unlawful wealth enters Paradise, the Fire is worthier of it; O Kaʿb, people go out morning and evening: one freeing his neck and one destroying it; O Kaʿb, prayer is a proof and charity removes sin as ice melts upon smooth rock. > **""Whoever enters upon them and confirms their lies and aids their injustice is not of me nor am I of him; no flesh grown from unlawful wealth enters Paradise; prayer is a proof and charity removes sin.""**"
"17712","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was admonishing his Companions when three men passed: one came and sat with the Prophet ﷺ, the second went a little then sat, the third went on his way, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked shall he not inform them of the three: the one who came and sat repented and Allāh turned to him, the one who went a little then sat was shy and Allāh was shy of him, and the one who went on disdained and Allāh dispensed with him. > **""Shall I not inform you of these three? The one who came and sat repented and Allāh turned to him; the one who was shy, Allāh was shy of him; and the one who disdained, Allāh dispensed with him.""**"
"17713","Muḥammad ibn Maslama, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said your Lord has breezes in the days of your time, so expose yourselves to them, for perhaps one of you shall be struck by a breeze after which he never suffers misery. > **""Your Lord has breezes in the days of your time, so expose yourselves to them, for perhaps one of you shall be struck by a breeze after which he never suffers misery.""**"
"17714","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said do good throughout your time and expose yourselves to the breezes of Allāh's mercy, for Allāh has breezes from His mercy striking whom He wills among His servants, and ask Allāh to cover your faults and secure your fears. > **""Do good throughout your time and expose yourselves to the breezes of Allāh's mercy, and ask Allāh to cover your faults and secure your fears.""**"
"17715","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, said as a stopped report heard from your Prophet ﷺ that whatever you dislike in your time is due to what you changed in your deeds: if good, then wāhan wāhan in admiration, and if evil, then āhan āhan in grief. > **""Whatever you dislike in your time is due to what you changed in your deeds: if good, then wāhan wāhan; and if evil, then āhan āhan.""**"
"17716","Thawbān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that you perform deeds unknown, and soon the absent one shall return to his family, either joyful or grieved. > **""You perform deeds unknown, and soon the absent one shall return to his family, either joyful or grieved.""**"
"17717","Abū Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Allāh, mighty and majestic, does not look to your bodies, nor your lineages, nor your wealth, but looks to your hearts; whoever has a sound heart, Allāh shows him mercy; you are but sons of Ādam, and the most beloved of you to Him is the most pious. > **""Allāh does not look to your bodies, nor your lineages, nor your wealth, but looks to your hearts.""**"
"17718","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ set out with him giving counsel, then turned toward Madīna and said that these members of his household deem themselves most deserving of him, but it is not so; his allies among them are the pious, whoever and wherever they are; O Allāh, he does not permit them to corrupt what He set right; and by Allāh, his nation shall be overturned from its religion as a vessel is overturned in the open plain. > **""My allies among you are the pious, whoever they are and wherever they are.""**"
"17719","Abū al-Aḥwaṣ reported from his father, may Allāh be pleased with them, that he came to the Prophet ﷺ who asked him: had you two slaves, one obeying you, neither betraying nor lying to you, and another betraying and lying to you, which is dearer — the obedient truthful one or the treacherous liar? He replied the one who neither betrays nor lies but tells the truth is dearer, so he said: such are you with your Lord. > **""Such are you with your Lord.""**"
"17720","In another version with al-Ṭabarānī the Prophet ﷺ said to him O ʿAwf ibn Mālik, is your lad who obeys you and follows your command dearer to you, or your lad who neither obeys nor follows your command? He said the obedient one, so he said: such are you with your Lord. > **""Such are you with your Lord.""**"
"17721","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he has prospered who purifies his heart for faith, makes his heart sound, his tongue truthful, his soul tranquil, his character upright, his ear listening and his eye observing; the ear is a funnel and the eye affirms what the heart retains; and he has prospered who makes his heart retentive. > **""He has prospered who purifies his heart for faith, makes his heart sound, his tongue truthful, his soul tranquil, and his character upright.""**"
"17722","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said his brother Mūsā, peace be upon him, asked O Lord, show me the one You showed me at the ship, and Allāh revealed O Mūsā, you shall see him, and soon al-Khaḍir came to him in sweet fragrance and fine white garments, greeting him as Mūsā son of ʿImrān and conveying his Lord's peace and mercy; Mūsā replied He is Peace, to Him is peace, from Him is peace, and praise belongs to the Lord of the worlds whose blessings he cannot count nor thank except by His aid; then Mūsā asked for a counsel by which Allāh would benefit him after him, so al-Khaḍir said O seeker of knowledge, the speaker tires less than the listener, so do not bore your companions when you speak; know your heart is a vessel, so see with what you fill it; know the world and cast it behind you, for it is no home for you nor place of rest, but made as provision for servants to take from it to the return; O Mūsā, settle yourself upon patience and you shall meet forbearance, fill your heart with piety and you shall attain knowledge, train yourself upon patience and you shall escape sin; O Mūsā, devote yourself to knowledge if you want it, for knowledge belongs to whoever devotes himself to it; do not be loquacious and babbling, for much speech disgraces scholars and exposes hidden faults, but keep to moderation, for that is guidance and rightness; turn from the ignorant and forbear the foolish, for that is the merit of the wise and adornment of scholars; when the ignorant reviles you, remain silent in peace and avoid him firmly, for what remains of his ignorance against you and his reviling is greater; O son of ʿImrān, you see you were given but little knowledge, for plunging recklessly comes of presumption and affectation; do not open a door whose closing you do not know, nor close a door whose opening you do not know; how can one be a worshipper whose greed for the world never ends and whose desire never ceases; how can one be ascetic who belittles his state and accuses Allāh of what He decreed for him; can one restrain lusts when desire has overcome him, and can seeking knowledge benefit him when ignorance has turned him, while his journey is to the Hereafter yet he faces his world; O Mūsā, learn what you learn to act upon it, not to narrate it, lest its ruin be upon you and its light for another; O son of ʿImrān, make asceticism and piety your garment, knowledge and remembrance your speech, multiply good deeds for you shall meet evil deeds, shake your heart with fear for that pleases your Lord, and do good for you must do other than it; you have been admonished if you preserve it; then al-Khaḍir departed and Mūsā remained grieved and distressed. > **""Know your heart is a vessel, so see with what you fill it; know the world and cast it behind you; learn what you learn to act upon it, not to narrate it.""**"
"17723","Abū Madīna al-Dārimī, a Companion, reported that when two men among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ met, they would not part until one recited to the other: By time — indeed man is in loss. > **""When two men among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ met, they would not part until one recited to the other: By time — indeed man is in loss.""**"
"17724","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays, Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ led them in prayer then said remain fixed in your places, then came to the men saying Allāh has commanded him to command them to fear Allāh and speak upright words, then went among the women saying Allāh commands him to command them to fear Allāh and speak upright words. > **""Allāh has commanded me to command you to fear Allāh and to speak upright words.""**"
"17725","Nuʿaym ibn Hammār al-Ghaṭafānī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: evil is the servant who tyrannises, exalts himself and forgets the Great, the Exalted; evil is the servant who deceives the world through religion; evil is the servant who permits prohibitions through doubts; evil is the servant whose desire misguides him; evil is the servant whose craving abases him. > **""Evil is the servant who tyrannises and exalts himself and forgets the Great, the Exalted; evil is the servant who deceives the world through religion.""**"
"17726","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said today is training and tomorrow the race, with the goal Paradise or Fire; I am first, Abū Bakr next in prayer, ʿUmar third, and people after by precedence, first then first. > **""Today is training and tomorrow the race, and the goal is Paradise or Fire.""**"
"17727","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said what is with people that they honour the luxurious, belittle worshippers, act upon the Qurʾān where it suits their desires and abandon what opposes them, thus believing in part of the Book and disbelieving in part, striving for what is attained without striving — the decreed decree, written term and apportioned provision — while not striving for what is attained only by striving: the ample recompense, the thanked striving, and the trade that never perishes. > **""What is with people that they honour the luxurious and belittle worshippers, acting upon the Qurʾān where it suits their desires?""**"
"17728","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā reported that Dāwūd the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: be to the orphan as a merciful father, and know that as you sow you reap; the likeness of a righteous wife to her husband is a king crowned with a gold-inlaid crown, whenever he sees her his eyes delight, while the likeness of an evil wife is a heavy load upon an aged elder; know that a fool's speech in his people's gathering is like singing at a corpse's head; never promise your brother then fail to fulfil, lest you bequeath enmity between you; seek refuge in Allāh from a companion who does not aid you when you remember Allāh nor remind you when you forget — and he is Satan; and remember what you dislike mentioned of you in your people's gathering, and do not do it in solitude. > **""Be to the orphan as a merciful father, and know that as you sow you reap.""**"
"17729","Abū Kabsha, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that during the Tabūk expedition people hastened to al-Ḥijr to enter it, so it was announced that prayer gathers, and he came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ holding his camel, saying upon what do you enter upon peoples with whom Allāh is angry? A man called out expressing wonder at them, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked shall he not inform them of something more wondrous: your Prophet informs you of what was before you and what shall be after you — be straight and upright, for Allāh does not care for punishing you at all. > **""Your Prophet informs you of what was before you and what shall be after you — be straight and upright.""**"
"17730","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said whoever shows off, Allāh shall show him; whoever seeks repute, Allāh shall make him reputed; whoever towers in magnifying himself, Allāh shall lower him; whoever is humble in awe, Allāh shall raise him; people are expanded in the world and constricted in the Hereafter, or constricted in the world and expanded in the Hereafter; and there is one at rest and one rested from; asked O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who are they, he said the believer when he dies rests, while the one rested from is he who wrongs people and backbites them. > **""Whoever shows off, Allāh shall show him; whoever seeks repute, Allāh shall make him reputed; whoever towers in magnifying himself, Allāh shall lower him; and whoever is humble in awe, Allāh shall raise him.""**"
"17731","Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿUqba reported that ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said Paradise is veiled in hardships and the Fire veiled in lusts, so whoever lifts the veil falls into what lies beyond it. > **""Paradise is veiled in hardships and the Fire veiled in lusts, so whoever lifts the veil falls into what lies beyond it.""**"
"17732","Maʿn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported that a man asked ʿAbd Allāh for comprehensive beneficial words, so ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, said worship Allāh and associate nothing with Him, turn with the Qurʾān wherever it turns, accept truth from whoever brings it even if distant, and reject falsehood from whoever brings it even if a beloved near one. > **""Worship Allāh and associate nothing with Him, turn with the Qurʾān wherever it turns, accept truth from whoever brings it, and reject falsehood from whoever brings it.""**"
"17733","ʿAwn ibn ʿAbd Allāh reported that ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, said knowledge is not from much narration, but knowledge is from awe. > **""Knowledge is not from much narration, but knowledge is from awe.""**"
"17734","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said none of you is but a guest and a loan; the guest departs and the loan is returned to its people. > **""None of you is but a guest and a loan; the guest departs and the loan is returned to its people.""**"
"17735","Al-Ḥasan reported that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said were he to stand between Paradise and Fire and be told choose whichever you love to be in, or to be ash, he would love to be ash. > **""Were I to stand between Paradise and Fire and be told to choose, I would love to be ash.""**"
"17736","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said what is coming is near, and only what is not coming is distant; Allāh does not hasten for one's haste nor lighten for people's affair; what Allāh wills, not what people will; Allāh wills a matter and people will a matter, and what Allāh wills occurs though people distance it; none brings near what Allāh distances, none distances what Allāh brings near, and nothing occurs except by Allāh's permission; the truest speech is Allāh's Book, the best guidance Muḥammad's guidance, the worst matters their novelties, every novelty an innovation and every innovation misguidance; the best cast into the heart is certainty, the best wealth the wealth of the soul, the best knowledge what benefits, the best guidance what is followed; what is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts; each of you ends in a place of four cubits, so do not bore people nor surrender them, for every soul has vigour and advance, and lassitude and retreat; the worst narrations are lying narrations; lying leads to wickedness and wickedness to Fire, so hold to truthfulness, for truthfulness leads to righteousness and righteousness to Paradise — consider that they are two companions met; the truthful is called truthful until written with Allāh as ṣiddīq, and the liar keeps lying until written a liar; lying is unfit neither in earnest nor jest, nor for a man to promise his child then fail him; do not ask the People of the Book about anything, for long time passed over them, their hearts hardened and they innovated in religion; if you must ask them, take what agrees with your Book, and turn from what opposes you and remain silent. > **""The truest speech is the Book of Allāh, the best guidance the guidance of Muḥammad, and the worst of matters their novelties; hold to truthfulness, for truthfulness leads to righteousness and righteousness to Paradise.""**"
"17737","ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, recited: Nay, you prefer the life of this world, then asked do you know with what He began the worldly life and why we preferred it: the world was hastened for us, given its pleasure and splendour, while the Hereafter was hidden and folded from us, so we loved the immediate and left the deferred. > **""The world was hastened for us with its pleasure and splendour, while the Hereafter was hidden from us, so we loved the immediate and left the deferred.""**"
"17738","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said people are two goers: one selling himself and destroying it, and one opposing it and freeing it; charity is a proof, charity a shield, fasting a shield, prayer a light, tranquillity a gain and abandoning it a loss. > **""People are two goers: one selling himself and destroying it, and one opposing it and freeing it; charity is a proof, fasting a shield, and prayer a light.""**"
"17739","Saʿd ibn ʿUmāra of Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr, a Companion, reported that a man asked him to admonish him privately, may Allāh have mercy on him, so he said when you reach prayer, perfect ablution, for there is no prayer without ablution and no faith without prayer; then when you pray, pray the prayer of one departing; abandon seeking many needs, for that is present poverty; gather despair of what is with people, for that is wealth; and consider what you would apologise for in word and deed, and avoid it. > **""When you pray, pray the prayer of one departing; gather despair of what is with people, for that is wealth.""**"
"17740","Al-Walīd ibn Ayman al-Alhānī reported that he heard al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, may Allāh be pleased with him, preaching at Ḥimṣ saying ruin is to commit evil deeds in times of trial. > **""Ruin is to commit evil deeds in times of trial.""**"
"17741","Abū Sinān al-Duʾalī reported that he entered upon ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, with early Emigrants present, when ʿUmar sent for a basket brought from an Iraqi fortress containing a ring, which one of his sons put in his mouth, so ʿUmar seized it then wept; asked why he wept when Allāh had granted victory, he said he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the world is opened upon no one except that Allāh casts enmity and hatred among them until Resurrection Day, and he feared that. > **""The world is opened upon no one except that Allāh casts enmity and hatred among them until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17742","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he does not fear poverty for them but fears rivalry in plenty for them, nor fears error for them but fears deliberate sin. > **""I do not fear poverty for you, but I fear rivalry in plenty for you; nor do I fear error for you, but I fear deliberate sin for you.""**"
"17743","Al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Anṣār heard Abū ʿUbayda arrive with wealth from Baḥrayn, where the Prophet ﷺ had sent him, so they attended dawn prayer with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and after he turned they presented themselves; he smiled and asked had they heard Abū ʿUbayda arrived with wealth, they said yes, so he said receive glad tidings and hope for good; by Allāh, it is not poverty he fears for them, but that the world be poured upon them and they rival in it as those before them rivalled. > **""Receive glad tidings and hope for good; by Allāh, it is not poverty I fear for you, but that the world be poured upon you and you rival in it as those before you rivalled.""**"
"17744","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that while the Prophet ﷺ preached, a coarse Bedouin stood saying O Messenger of Allāh, the hyena has devoured us, so the Prophet ﷺ said other than that is more fearful to him for them: when the world is poured upon them abundantly — would that his nation not wear gold. > **""Other than that is more fearful to me for you: when the world is poured upon you abundantly.""**"
"17745","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said when his nation walks struttingly and Persia and Rome serve them, some of them shall overpower others. > **""When my nation walks struttingly and Persia and Rome serve them, some of them shall overpower others.""**"
"17746","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, used to distribute stipends when a man came and he gave him a thousand dirhams, saying take it, for he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say only the dīnār and dirham destroyed those before you, and they shall destroy you. > **""Only the dīnār and dirham destroyed those before you, and they shall destroy you.""**"
"17747","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked how they shall be when a bowl is brought morning and another evening; they asked O Messenger of Allāh, shall we then be well? He replied rather you today are better. > **""How shall you be when a bowl is brought to you morning and another evening? Rather you today are better.""**"
"17748","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said wealth is not from abundance of goods; wealth is but wealth of the soul. > **""Wealth is not from abundance of goods; wealth is but wealth of the soul.""**"
"17749","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked him three times O Abū Dharr, do you say abundance of wealth is wealth, and scarcity of wealth poverty? He said yes; then the Prophet ﷺ said wealth is in the heart and poverty in the heart; whoever has wealth in his heart, what he meets of the world does not harm him, and whoever has poverty in his heart, what is multiplied for him of the world does not enrich him; only his own stinginess harms him. > **""Wealth is in the heart and poverty is in the heart.""**"
"17750","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said an angel at a gate of heaven says whoever lends today shall be repaid tomorrow, and an angel at another gate says O Allāh, grant the spender replacement and grant the hoarder ruin. > **""O Allāh, grant the spender replacement and grant the hoarder ruin.""**"
"17751","Abū al-Bakhtarī reported from ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, that ʿUmar asked people what they thought of surplus wealth with them; they said O Commander of the Believers, you were busied from family, estate and trade, so it is yours; he asked ʿAlī, who first deferred then said why make your certainty doubt; ʿUmar demanded proof, so ʿAlī recalled when the Prophet ﷺ sent ʿUmar as collector and al-ʿAbbās withheld charity, then they found the Prophet ﷺ heavy-spirited one day and cheerful the next, and he explained that two charity dīnārs remained with him the first day, causing his heaviness, and he had directed them the next, causing his cheer; ʿUmar said by Allāh you spoke truth, and he would thank him in world and Hereafter. > **""You came to me the first day while two dīnārs of charity remained with me, and that was what you saw of my heaviness; you came today after I directed them, and that was what you saw of my cheer.""**"
"17752","Umm Salama, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ entered upon her with changed countenance, and when she asked whether from pain, he said it was due to the seven dīnārs brought yesterday, which evening found upon the bedding's edge. > **""It is due to the seven dīnārs brought yesterday, which evening found upon the bedding's edge.""**"
"17753","In another wording: evening came and we had not spent them. > **""Evening came and we had not spent them.""**"
"17754","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that wealth came to ʿUmar and he divided it among Muslims, leaving a surplus about which he consulted; they said leave it for emergencies; ʿAlī remained silent, and when pressed said Allāh had completed division of this wealth, recalling the Baḥrayn wealth that came to the Prophet ﷺ when night barred its division, so he prayed the prayers in the mosque with its trace upon his face until finished; ʿUmar said divide it without fail, so ʿAlī divided it and Ṭalḥa received eight hundred dirhams. > **""Allāh has completed the division of this wealth.""**"
"17755","Abū Saʿīd, meaning al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that he would not love Uḥud in gold remaining with him by the third morning with anything of it except what he reserves for debt. > **""I would not love Uḥud in gold remaining with me by the third morning with anything of it except what I reserve for debt.""**"
"17756","Samura, meaning Ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to say he would not love Uḥud entirely in gold for himself. > **""I would not love Uḥud entirely in gold for myself.""**"
"17757","ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās reported that Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, told him O son of my brother, he was with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ holding his hand when he said O Abū Dharr, he would not love Uḥud in gold and silver spent in Allāh's path while dying leaving a qīrāṭ of it; asked about a qinṭār, he said three times O Abū Dharr, he goes to the lesser and he to the greater — he wants the Hereafter while he wants the world — a qīrāṭ. > **""O Abū Dharr, I would not love Uḥud in gold and silver spent in Allāh's path while dying leaving a qīrāṭ of it.""**"
"17758","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he came to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, staff in hand, when ʿUthmān asked Kaʿb about ʿAbd al-Raḥmān who died leaving wealth; Kaʿb said if he discharged Allāh's right therein there is no harm upon him, so Abū Dharr struck Kaʿb with his staff saying he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say he would not love this mountain in gold for himself, spent and accepted, leaving six ounces behind, adjuring ʿUthmān thrice whether he heard it, and he said yes. > **""I would not love this mountain in gold for myself, spent and accepted, leaving six ounces behind me.""**"
"17759","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ turned to Uḥud and said by Him in whose Hand is his soul, it would not please him were Uḥud transformed into gold for Muḥammad's household, spent in Allāh's path, while dying leaving two dīnārs except two dīnārs reserved for debt if any. > **""By Him in whose Hand is my soul, it would not please me were Uḥud transformed into gold for Muḥammad's household, spent in Allāh's path, while dying leaving two dīnārs except two reserved for debt.""**"
"17760","ʿĀʾisha, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ commanded her in his illness to give gold in charity; when he awoke he asked what she did, she said his state busied her, so he said bring it, and she brought seven or nine — Abū Ḥāzim doubts — dīnārs; when she brought them he said what would Muḥammad think were he to meet Allāh with these with him, and what would these avail Muḥammad were he to meet Allāh with these with him. > **""What would Muḥammad think were he to meet Allāh with these with him, and what would these avail Muḥammad were he to meet Allāh with these with him?""**"
"17761","In another wording: between five and seven, or eight and nine — I shall spend them. > **""Between five and seven, or eight and nine — I shall spend them.""**"
"17762","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ṣāmit reported that he was with Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, when his stipend came with his slave-girl attending to his needs, leaving seven, which he ordered her to exchange for coppers; asked why not reserve it for need or guest, he said his close friend covenanted to him that any gold or silver one holds over is ember upon its owner until he pours it forth in Allāh's path. > **""My close friend covenanted to me that any gold or silver one holds over is ember upon its owner until he pours it forth in Allāh's path.""**"
"17763","Umm Salama, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the most the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ brought was a purse containing eight hundred dirhams. > **""The most the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ brought was a purse containing eight hundred dirhams.""**"
"17764","ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that a man of the Bench died leaving two dīnārs or two dirhams, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said two brands — pray for your companion. > **""Two brands — pray for your companion.""**"
"17765","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that a man of the Bench died and two dīnārs were found in his wrap, so they mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ who said two brands — pray for your companion. > **""Two brands — pray for your companion.""**"
"17766","Also from Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, that a black servant joined the Prophet ﷺ then died, so the Prophet ﷺ was informed and asked to see whether he left anything; they said two dīnārs, so the Prophet ﷺ said two brands. > **""Two brands.""**"
"17767","Salama, meaning Ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he sat with the Prophet ﷺ when one bier came, then another; he asked whether the deceased left debt, they said no; whether he left anything, they said yes, three dīnārs; so he gestured with his finger: three brands. > **""Three brands.""**"
"17768","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say whoever leaves a dīnār, it is a brand. > **""Whoever leaves a dīnār, it is a brand.""**"
"17769","Abū Umāma al-Ḥimṣī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that a man of the Bench died and a dīnār was found in his waist-wrap, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said a brand; then another died and two dīnārs were found, so he said a brand or two brands. > **""A brand — a brand or two brands.""**"
"17770","In another wording from him the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said concerning a man who died leaving one or two dīnārs: a brand or two brands. > **""A brand or two brands.""**"
"17771","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that a Bedouin raided with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at Khaybar and received two dīnārs from his share, which he sewed and wrapped in his cloak; when he died the two dīnārs were found and mentioned to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, who said two brands. > **""Two brands.""**"
"17772","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed over a man who left two or three dīnārs, and the Prophet ﷺ said two brands or three. > **""Two brands or three.""**"
"17773","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Allāh's Prophet ﷺ was brought news while they were with him that so-and-so died leaving two dīnārs or two dirhams, so he said two brands. > **""Two brands.""**"
"17774","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he gifted the Prophet ﷺ three birds, who fed his servant one bird; next day she brought them, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked had he not forbidden her to store anything for tomorrow, for Allāh brings the provision of each morrow. > **""Did I not forbid you to store anything for tomorrow, for Allāh brings the provision of each morrow?""**"
"17775","Bilāl, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ entered while he had dates, asking what they were; he said stored for their winter, so he said do you not fear seeing its steam in Hell. > **""Do you not fear seeing its steam in Hell?""**"
"17776","In another wording the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said feed us dates, O Bilāl, so he scooped handfuls, and he said more, O Bilāl, and he added three, saying nothing remained except what he stored for the Prophet ﷺ, so he said spend, O Bilāl, and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne. > **""Spend, O Bilāl, and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne.""**"
"17777","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon Bilāl, may Allāh be pleased with him, finding heaps of dates, asking what they were, O Bilāl; he said prepared for his guests, so he said do you not fear their smoke in Hell's fire — spend, O Bilāl, and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne. > **""Spend, O Bilāl, and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne.""**"
"17778","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon Bilāl finding heaps of dates, asking what they were; he said he stores them, so he said do you not fear seeing their steam in Hell's fire — spend, O Bilāl, and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne. > **""Spend, O Bilāl, and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne.""**"
"17779","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ saying he had nothing to give, but borrow until something comes and they give him; ʿUmar said Allāh did not burden him thus — give what you have, and when you have not, you are not burdened; the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ disliked ʿUmar's words until seen in his face, so the man said O Messenger of Allāh, may father and mother be ransom for you, give and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne; the Prophet ﷺ smiled and said by this he was commanded. > **""Give and fear no lessening from the Owner of the Throne — by this I was commanded.""**"
"17780","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, after each prayer sat for people, and one day Ibn ʿAbbās and ʿUthmān, may Allāh be pleased with them, entered to find heaps of wealth; ʿUmar said he saw them among Madīna's largest clans and told them to take and divide it, returning surplus; asked whether he would add if deficient, he said a trace from a rough one, recalling that Muḥammad and his household ate dried meat; Ibn ʿAbbās swore had Allāh opened this for Muḥammad he would have dealt otherwise, so ʿUmar grew angry until his ribs shook, asking how, and he said he would have eaten and fed them, whereupon his anger lifted. > **""Had Allāh opened this for Muḥammad, he would have eaten and fed us.""**"
"17781","Al-Ḥasan reported that when Qays ibn ʿĀṣim came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ he said this is the chief of the desert folk; Qays asked O Messenger of Allāh, what wealth bears no liability for guest or dependents however many? He said excellent wealth is forty, and if many then sixty, and woe to owners of hundreds — thrice — except whoever gives from its milk and aid, lends its back, offers its stallion, slaughters its fat, grants its rich-milked, and feeds the content and the petitioner; Qays admired such morals and was asked how he deals with gifts, lending, and studding, replying he grants a hundred yearly, lends neither young milch nor worn old, and lets camels go out with people so whoever wills takes a head; asked whether his wealth or his clients' is dearer, he said his own, so he was told one has of his wealth only what he eats and consumes, wears and wears out, or gives and sends forth; Qays asked is it so, he said yes, and Qays swore if he lived he would reduce their number. > **""One has of his wealth only what he eats and consumes, wears and wears out, or gives and sends forth.""**"
"17782","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said nothing erodes Islam with erosion like stinginess. > **""Nothing erodes Islam with erosion like stinginess.""**"
"17783","Abū al-Qayn reported that he passed the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with dates upon his mount when his uncle rose to take a handful to place before the Prophet ﷺ, but he sprawled over the dates, so the Prophet ﷺ said O Allāh, increase him in stinginess — and he became among the most miserly of people. > **""O Allāh, increase him in stinginess.""**"
"17784","Nāfiʿ reported that Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both, heard a man say the miser is more excusable than the wrongdoer, so Ibn ʿUmar said you lie; he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the miser shall not enter Paradise. > **""The miser shall not enter Paradise.""**"
"17785","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that his young goat among many sheep was suckled by its mother satisfying it, then broke loose one day and suckled the whole flock yet was not sated; so it was said the like of this are peoples coming after you, one man given what suffices a tribe or nation yet never sated. > **""The like of this are peoples coming after you: one man among them is given what suffices a tribe or a nation, yet he is never sated.""**"
"17786","Jābir, meaning Ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were the son of Ādam to have a valley of wealth he would wish for two valleys, and were he to have two he would wish for a third, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have a valley of wealth he would wish for two valleys, and were he to have two he would wish for a third, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust.""**"
"17787","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say were the son of Ādam to have a valley of palms he would wish for its like, then its like, until he wished for valleys, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have a valley of palms he would wish for its like, then its like, until he wished for valleys, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust.""**"
"17788","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allāh be pleased with him, said they used to recite in the Prophet's ﷺ time: were the son of Ādam to have two valleys of gold and silver he would seek another to them, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have two valleys of gold and silver he would seek another to them, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns.""**"
"17789","Masrūq reported that he asked ʿĀʾisha, may Allāh be pleased with her, whether the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said anything upon entering the house; she said upon entering he would cite verse saying: were the son of Ādam to have two valleys of wealth he would seek a third, and nothing fills his mouth except dust; wealth was made only for establishing prayer and giving zakāt, and Allāh turns to whoever turns. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have two valleys of wealth he would seek a third, and nothing fills his mouth except dust; wealth was made only for establishing prayer and giving zakāt, and Allāh turns to whoever turns.""**"
"17790","Burayda, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ recite in prayer: were the son of Ādam to have a valley of gold he would seek a second to it, and were he given a second he would seek a third to it, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have a valley of gold he would seek a second to it, and were he given a second he would seek a third to it, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns.""**"
"17791","Samura ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to say a man's soul is never filled with wealth until it is filled with dust; were one of you to have a valley filled from top to bottom he would love another valley to be filled for him, and were the other filled and he set out and found another valley he would say by Allāh, had he been able he would fill it. > **""A man's soul is never filled with wealth until it is filled with dust; were one of you to have a valley filled from top to bottom he would love another valley to be filled for him.""**"
"17792","Abū Saʿīd, meaning al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were the son of Ādam to have a valley of wealth he would seek a second to it, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have a valley of wealth he would seek a second to it, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust.""**"
"17793","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said were the son of Ādam to have two valleys of wealth he would wish for a third to them, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have two valleys of wealth he would wish for a third to them, and nothing fills the son of Ādam's belly except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns.""**"
"17794","Abū Umāma, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that were the son of Ādam to have two valleys he would wish for a third valley; wealth was made only for establishing prayer and giving zakāt; nothing satiates the son of Ādam except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns. > **""Were the son of Ādam to have two valleys he would wish for a third; wealth was made only for establishing prayer and giving zakāt; nothing satiates the son of Ādam except dust.""**"
"17795","Kaʿb ibn ʿIyāḍ al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from Allāh's Prophet ﷺ that were two valleys of wealth poured forth for the son of Ādam he would wish for a third to them, and nothing satiates the son of Ādam except dust, and Allāh turns to whoever turns. > **""Were two valleys of wealth poured forth for the son of Ādam he would wish for a third to them, and nothing satiates the son of Ādam except dust.""**"
"17796","ʿUqba ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that when you see Allāh giving the servant what he loves while he persists in His disobedience, that is but gradual seizure from Him; then he cited the verse: When they forgot what they were reminded of, We opened upon them the gates of everything until, when they rejoiced in what they were given, We seized them suddenly and they were in despair, and the root of the wrongdoing people was cut off, and praise belongs to Allāh, Lord of the worlds. > **""When you see Allāh giving the servant what he loves while he persists in His disobedience, that is but gradual seizure from Him.""**"
"17797","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Satan, may Allāh curse him, says the wealthy companion shall never be safe from him in one of three, with which he comes morning and evening: taking it from unlawful means, spending it in unlawful right, and making it beloved to him so he withholds its right. > **""The wealthy companion shall never be safe from me in one of three: taking it from unlawful means, spending it in unlawful right, and making it beloved to him so he withholds its right.""**"
"17798","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said Iblīs sends his fiercest and strongest companions against whoever does good with his wealth. > **""Iblīs sends his fiercest and strongest companions against whoever does good with his wealth.""**"
"17799","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said your enemy is not he who, if you kill him is light, and if he kills you you enter Paradise; rather your fiercest foe is your child who came from your loins, then your fiercest foe is your wealth which your right hand possesses. > **""Your fiercest foe is your child who came from your loins, then your fiercest foe is your wealth which your right hand possesses.""**"
"17800","Abū Mūsā, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said this dīnār and dirham destroyed those before you, and he does not see them but destroying you. > **""This dīnār and dirham destroyed those before you, and I do not see them but destroying you.""**"
"17801","ʿAwf ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ stood among his Companions saying: is it poverty or need you fear, or does the world concern you — for Allāh shall open Persia and Rome for you, and the world shall be poured upon you until nothing turns you after you have turned except it. > **""Allāh shall open Persia and Rome for you, and the world shall be poured upon you until nothing turns you after you have turned except it.""**"
"17802","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he fears the trial of ease for them more than the trial of hardship; they were tried with the trial of hardship and were patient, and the world is green and sweet. > **""I fear the trial of ease for you more than the trial of hardship; the world is green and sweet.""**"
"17803","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samura, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the world is sweet and green, and Allāh makes you stewards therein and sees how you act; so beware the world and beware women. > **""O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the world is sweet and green, and Allāh makes you stewards therein and sees how you act; so beware the world and beware women.""**"
"17804","Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said this wealth is sweet and green. > **""This wealth is sweet and green.""**"
"17805","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the world is sweet and green; whoever takes it by its right is blessed therein, while many a plonger into what his soul desires shall have nothing on Resurrection Day except the Fire. > **""The world is sweet and green; whoever takes it by its right is blessed therein, while many a plonger into what his soul desires shall have nothing on the Day of Resurrection except the Fire.""**"
"17806","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said to his Companions the world is sweet and green; Allāh makes you stewards therein and looks at how you act — so beware women and beware the world. > **""The world is sweet and green; Allāh makes you stewards therein and looks at how you act — so beware women and beware the world.""**"
"17807","Abū Bakra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ preached, praised Allāh and extolled Him, then said the world is sweet and green, Allāh makes you stewards therein and looks at how you act, so beware the world and beware women; every traitor shall have a banner on Resurrection Day at his back. > **""The world is sweet and green, and Allāh makes you stewards therein and looks at how you act; every traitor shall have a banner on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17808","Umm Salama, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the world is sweet and green; whoever takes it by its right is blessed therein, and whoever takes it without its right is like one who eats; woe to the plonger in Allāh's wealth and His Messenger's wealth from Hell's punishment on Resurrection Day. > **""Whoever takes it by its right is blessed therein; woe to the plonger in Allāh's wealth and His Messenger's wealth from Hell's punishment on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17809","ʿĀʾisha, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the world is sweet and green, so whoever is given something of it without cheerful soul, it shall not be blessed for him. > **""The world is sweet and green, so whoever is given something of it without cheerful soul, it shall not be blessed for him.""**"
"17810","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said this wealth is green and sweet; whoever takes it — Yaḥyā mentioned something he did not recall — is blessed therein, while many a plonger in Allāh's and His Messenger's wealth for what his soul desires shall have the Fire on Resurrection Day. > **""This wealth is green and sweet; whoever takes it rightfully is blessed therein, while many a plonger in Allāh's and His Messenger's wealth for what his soul desires shall have the Fire on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17811","Maymūna, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said the world is sweet, green and sweet; whoever fears therein and reforms is saved, while the other is like an eater who never satiates; distant people are as distant as two stars: one rising in the east and another setting in the west. > **""The world is sweet and green; whoever fears therein and reforms is saved, while the other is like an eater who never satiates.""**"
"17812","ʿAmra ibn al-Ḥārith, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the world is sweet and green; whoever takes it by its right, Allāh blesses him therein, while many a plonger in Allāh's and His Messenger's wealth shall have the Fire on Resurrection Day when he meets Him. > **""The world is sweet and green; whoever takes it by its right, Allāh blesses him therein, while many a plonger in Allāh's and His Messenger's wealth shall have the Fire on the Day he meets Him.""**"
"17813","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever has the Hereafter as his intention, Allāh, blessed and exalted, puts wealth in his heart, gathers his affairs, removes poverty from between his eyes, and the world comes to him humbled, so he mornings and evenings but rich; and whoever has the world as his intention, Allāh puts poverty between his eyes, so he mornings and evenings but poor. > **""Whoever has the Hereafter as his intention, Allāh puts wealth in his heart and the world comes to him humbled; and whoever has the world as his intention, Allāh puts poverty between his eyes.""**"
"17814","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever has the world as his concern and grief, looks to it and intends it, Allāh puts poverty between his eyes, scatters his estate, and he gets of it only what was written for him; and whoever has the Hereafter as his concern and grief, looks to it and intends it, Allāh, mighty and majestic, puts wealth in his heart, gathers his estate, and the world comes to him abased. > **""Whoever has the Hereafter as his concern, Allāh puts wealth in his heart and the world comes to him abased; and whoever has the world as his concern, Allāh puts poverty between his eyes.""**"
"17815","Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said may Allāh have mercy on whoever hears his saying and conveys it to another: three over which a Muslim's heart never betrays — sincerity of deed for Allāh, counsel to Muslim leaders, and adherence to their community, for their supplication encompasses them; whoever has the world as his intention, Allāh puts his poverty between his eyes, scatters his estate, and he gets of it only what was written; and whoever has the Hereafter as his intention, Allāh puts his wealth in his heart, suffices his estate, and the world comes to him humbled. > **""Sincerity of deed for Allāh, counsel to Muslim leaders, and adherence to their community; whoever has the Hereafter as his intention, Allāh puts his wealth in his heart and the world comes to him humbled.""**"
"17816","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said free yourselves from worldly worries as much as you can; whoever has the world as his greatest concern, Allāh scatters his estate and puts his poverty between his eyes; and whoever has the Hereafter as his greatest concern, Allāh gathers his affair and puts his wealth in his heart; no servant turns with his heart to Allāh except that Allāh makes believers' hearts come to him with love and mercy, and Allāh is swifter to him with every good. > **""Whoever has the Hereafter as his greatest concern, Allāh gathers his affair and puts his wealth in his heart.""**"
"17817","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ addressed them in the mosque of al-Khayf, praising Allāh as He deserves, then said whoever has the Hereafter as his concern, Allāh gathers his affairs, puts his wealth between his eyes, and the world comes to him humbled; and whoever has the world as his greatest concern, Allāh scatters his affairs, puts his poverty between his eyes, and he gets of the world only what was written for him. > **""Whoever has the Hereafter as his concern, Allāh gathers his affairs and the world comes to him humbled; and whoever has the world as his greatest concern, Allāh scatters his affairs.""**"
"17818","Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever mornings with the world as his concern has nothing of Allāh in him; whoever shows no concern for Muslims' affair is not of them; and whoever willingly, uncoerced, gives humiliation from himself is not of us. > **""Whoever mornings with the world as his concern has nothing of Allāh in him; and whoever shows no concern for Muslims' affair is not of them.""**"
"17819","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever mornings grieved over the world mornings angry with his Lord; whoever mornings complaining of a calamity befallen him but complains of Allāh; whoever abases himself before a rich man to attain what is in his hands angers Allāh, mighty and majestic; and whoever is given the Qurʾān then enters the Fire, Allāh distances him. > **""Whoever mornings grieved over the world mornings angry with his Lord; and whoever abases himself before a rich man to attain what is in his hands angers Allāh.""**"
"17820","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever fulfils his craving in the world shall be barred from his desire in the Hereafter; whoever fixes his eyes upon the luxury of the rich shall be abased in the kingdom of the heavens; and whoever bears severe scant provision with beautiful patience, Allāh shall house him in al-Firdaws wherever he wills. > **""Whoever fulfils his craving in the world shall be barred from his desire in the Hereafter; and whoever bears severe scant provision with beautiful patience, Allāh shall house him in al-Firdaws.""**"
"17821","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said wretched is the servant of the dīnār and the servant of the dirham, whose only concern is a dīnār or dirham he obtains and takes. > **""Wretched is the servant of the dīnār and the servant of the dirham.""**"
"17822","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever resents his provision and broadcasts his complaint, no deed of his ascends to Allāh, and he shall meet Allāh while He is angry with him. > **""Whoever resents his provision and broadcasts his complaint, no deed of his ascends to Allāh, and he shall meet Allāh while He is angry with him.""**"
"17823","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said beware of greed, for it is poverty; and beware what requires apology. > **""Beware of greed, for it is poverty; and beware what requires apology.""**"
"17824","Jubayr ibn Nufayr reported that ʿAwf ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, went out to people saying the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ commanded you to seek refuge from three: from greed where there is nothing to covet, from greed leading to miserliness, and from coveting what is not to be coveted. > **""Seek refuge from greed where there is nothing to covet, from greed leading to miserliness, and from coveting what is not to be coveted.""**"
"17825","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever loves his world harms his Hereafter, and whoever loves his Hereafter harms his world, so prefer what remains over what perishes. > **""Whoever loves his world harms his Hereafter, and whoever loves his Hereafter harms his world, so prefer what remains over what perishes.""**"
"17826","Shurayḥ ibn ʿUbayd al-Ḥaḍramī reported that Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī, may Allāh be pleased with him, at death said O hearers of the Ashʿarīs, let the witness convey to the absent: he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the sweetness of the world is the bitterness of the Hereafter, and the bitterness of the world is the sweetness of the Hereafter. > **""The sweetness of the world is the bitterness of the Hereafter, and the bitterness of the world is the sweetness of the Hereafter.""**"
"17827","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever's heart is imbued with love of the world is entangled in three: misery whose toil never ends, greed never reaching wealth, and hope never reaching its end; the world is seeker and sought — whoever seeks the world, the Hereafter seeks him until death overtakes him, and whoever seeks the Hereafter, the world seeks him until he fulfils his provision from it. > **""Whoever's heart is imbued with love of the world is entangled in misery whose toil never ends, greed never reaching wealth, and hope never reaching its end.""**"
"17828","Huzayl ibn Shuraḥbīl reported that ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said whoever desires the Hereafter harms the world, and whoever desires the world harms his Hereafter, and commanded them to harm the perishing for the remaining; and said you are in a time of many scholars, few preachers, many givers and few askers, so lengthen prayer and shorten sermons, for some eloquence is magic; and after you comes a time of many preachers, few scholars, many askers and few givers. > **""Whoever desires the Hereafter harms the world, and whoever desires the world harms his Hereafter, so harm the perishing for the remaining.""**"
"17829","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that two ravenous hungry wolves among sheep parted, one at its start and another at its end, are no faster in ruin than a man in his religion loving worldly honour and its wealth. > **""Two ravenous hungry wolves among parted sheep are no faster in ruin than a man in his religion loving worldly honour and its wealth.""**"
"17830","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said two ravenous wolves in an enclosure, eating and corrupting, are no more harmful therein than love of honour and love of wealth in a Muslim man's religion. > **""Two ravenous wolves in an enclosure, eating and corrupting, are no more harmful therein than love of honour and love of wealth in a Muslim man's religion.""**"
"17831","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said two ravenous wolves in a sheepfold are no faster in ruin therein than seeking wealth and honour in a Muslim man's religion. > **""Two ravenous wolves in a sheepfold are no faster in ruin therein than seeking wealth and honour in a Muslim man's religion.""**"
"17832","ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAdī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he and his brother bought a hundred shares of Khaybar, and when it reached the Prophet ﷺ he said two attacking wolves left in sheep whose owner neglected them are no worse than a Muslim's seeking wealth and honour to his religion. > **""Two attacking wolves left in sheep whose owner neglected them are no worse than a Muslim's seeking wealth and honour to his religion.""**"
"17833","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said two ravenous wolves spending the night among sheep are no more corrupting to them than the son of Ādam's love of honour and wealth. > **""Two ravenous wolves spending the night among sheep are no more corrupting to them than the son of Ādam's love of honour and wealth.""**"
"17834","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said two ravenous hungry wolves spending the night in a sheepfold whose owners neglected it, tearing and eating, are no faster in ruin therein than love of wealth and honour in a Muslim man's religion. > **""Two ravenous hungry wolves in a neglected sheepfold are no faster in ruin therein than love of wealth and honour in a Muslim man's religion.""**"
"17835","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sent him to Yemen he told him beware of luxury, for Allāh's servants are not luxurious. > **""Beware of luxury, for Allāh's servants are not luxurious.""**"
"17836","Abū Umāma, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there shall be men of his nation eating various foods, drinking various drinks, wearing various clothes, and boasting in speech — those are the worst of his nation. > **""There shall be men of my nation eating various foods, drinking various drinks, wearing various clothes, and boasting in speech — those are the worst of my nation.""**"
"17837","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said among the worst of his nation are those nourished in bliss, upon whom their bodies grew. > **""Among the worst of my nation are those nourished in bliss, upon whom their bodies grew.""**"
"17838","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the sated in this world are the hungry tomorrow in the Hereafter. > **""The sated in this world are the hungry tomorrow in the Hereafter.""**"
"17839","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, swore by Him besides whom there is no god that he never saw anyone harsher against the extremists than the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, nor anyone harsher against them after him than Abū Bakr, and he thought ʿUmar was the most fearful of earth's people against them — or for them. > **""I never saw anyone harsher against the extremists than the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, nor after him than Abū Bakr.""**"
"17840","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ: perished are the extremists. > **""Perished are the extremists.""**"
"17841","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said a man's nobility is his religion, his manhood his intellect, and his lineage his character; while al-Bazzār's wording has a man's lineage is his wealth and his nobility his piety — lineage is wealth and nobility is piety. > **""A man's nobility is his religion, his manhood his intellect, and his lineage his character.""**"
"17842","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, said the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ forbade them from amassing in family and wealth; Abū Ḥamza, seated with him, said yes, Akhram al-Ṭāʾī narrated from his father, from ʿAbd Allāh, from the Prophet ﷺ; ʿAbd Allāh then asked what of the people of Barādhān, Madīna and elsewhere; Shuʿba asked Abū al-Tayyāḥ what amassing is, and he said abundance. > **""The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ forbade us from amassing in family and wealth.""**"
"17843","Shaqīq reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allāh be pleased with him, entered upon Umm Salama, may Allāh be pleased with her, saying O Mother of the Believers, he fears he has perished, for he is among Quraysh's wealthiest, having sold land of his for forty thousand dīnārs; she said O son, spend — and mentioned the report. > **""O Mother of the Believers, I fear I have perished, for I am among Quraysh's wealthiest.""**"
"17844","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that no servant or maid but has three friends: a friend saying I am with you, take what you will and leave what you will — that is his wealth; a friend saying I am with you, but when you reach the king's gate I leave you — those are his servants and family; and a friend saying I am with you wherever you enter and wherever you exit — that is his deed; and in al-Ṭabarānī's Middle wording the parable of the man and death is like a man with three friends: the first says this is my wealth, take and give and leave as you will; the second says I serve you, but when you die I leave you; the third says I am with you, entering and exiting with you, dead or alive — the first is his wealth, the second his kin, the third his deed entering and exiting with him wherever he is. > **""No servant but has three friends: wealth saying take what you will, family leaving you at the king's gate, and deeds with you wherever you enter and exit.""**"
"17845","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said no servant but has three friends: a friend saying what you spend is yours and what you withhold is not yours — that is his wealth; a friend saying I am with you, but when you reach the king's gate I leave you and return — that is his family; and a friend saying I am with you wherever you enter and wherever you exit — that is his deed, saying you were the lightest of the three to me. > **""What you spend is yours and what you withhold is not yours; I am with you wherever you enter and wherever you exit — you were the lightest of the three to me.""**"
"17846","Samura ibn Jundub, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said each of you on the day he dies has three friends: one withholding what he asks — that is his wealth; a friend going with him until he enters the grave, giving and withholding nothing — those are his kin; and a friend saying I am with you wherever you go, never parting from you — that is his deed, whether good or evil. > **""Each of you on the day he dies has three friends: wealth withholding what he asks, kin going with him to the grave, and deeds with him wherever he goes, never parting from him.""**"
"17847","Abū Hurayra, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the parable of the son of Ādam with his wealth, family and deeds is a man with three brothers or companions: one says I am with you in your life, but when you die I am neither of you nor you of me; another says I am with you, but when you reach that tree I am neither of you nor you of me; and the third says I am with you alive and dead. > **""I am with you alive and dead.""**"
"17848","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said whoever economises never falls into poverty. > **""Whoever economises never falls into poverty.""**"
"17849","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said an economiser never falls into poverty. > **""An economiser never falls into poverty.""**"
"17850","Ḥudhayfa, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said how excellent is moderation in wealth, how excellent is moderation in poverty, and excellent is moderation in worship. > **""How excellent is moderation in wealth, how excellent is moderation in poverty, and excellent is moderation in worship.""**"
"17851","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ walked with them at Mecca while fasting, and fasting exhausted him, so they milked a camel of theirs into a bowl and poured honey over it to honour the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at his breaking; at sunset they handed him the bowl, and upon tasting he gestured as if saying what is this; they said milk and honey to honour him — honour may Allāh honour him, or a supplication to that meaning — then he said whoever economises, Allāh enriches him; whoever squanders, Allāh impoverishes him; whoever is humble, Allāh raises him; and whoever tyrannises, Allāh crushes him. > **""Whoever economises, Allāh enriches him; whoever squanders, Allāh impoverishes him; whoever is humble, Allāh raises him; and whoever tyrannises, Allāh crushes him.""**"
"17852","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that peoples used to come to the Prophet ﷺ without acquaintances, so a man would take a man's hand, another two men's, another three men's, according to capacity; his brother-in-law took two men, so he drew aside and blamed him saying you take two men with what you have; he said they had provision from Allāh — come and see — showing wheat bought from yesterday's caravan, dates like a camel's calf, and a jar of fat for oil and condiment; then he took them morning or evening to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, having fed and oiled them, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he saw his two companions in good state — how many meals do you feed them daily? He said two meals; he said two meals — why not one? > **""Two meals? Why not one?""**"
"17853","Abū Ḥasaba Muslim ibn Akays, freedman of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir, reported from Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ, may Allāh be pleased with him, that one who entered upon him found him weeping and asked what makes you weep, O Abū ʿUbayda; he said he wept because the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ once mentioned what Allāh would open for Muslims and bestow upon them, mentioning Syria, saying were your term prolonged, O Abū ʿUbayda, three servants suffice you — a servant serving you, a servant travelling with you, and a servant serving your family and returning to them — and three mounts suffice you — a mount for your baggage, a mount for your transport, and a mount for your lad; and now he looked at his house filled with slaves and his stable filled with mounts and horses — so how shall he meet the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ after this, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ enjoined that the dearest and nearest to him are those meeting him upon the state they parted from him. > **""Three servants suffice you and three mounts suffice you; the dearest and nearest to me are those meeting me upon the state they parted from me.""**"
"17854","Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿda reported that some Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ visited Khabbāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, saying rejoice, O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, you shall meet Muḥammad ﷺ at the Pond; he asked how, pointing to his house's top and bottom, when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said only a rider's provision of the world suffices one of you. > **""Only a rider's provision of the world suffices one of you.""**"
"17855","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he entered upon Salmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, and saw his house bare, so he spoke of it, and Salmān said the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ covenanted to him that his provision of the world be as a rider's provision. > **""Let your provision of the world be as a rider's provision.""**"
"17856","Thawbān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he asked O Messenger of Allāh, what suffices him of the world; he said what fills your hunger and covers your nakedness; if you have a house shading you, then that, and if you have a mount, then excellent. > **""What fills your hunger and covers your nakedness suffices you; if you have a house shading you, then that.""**"
"17857","ʿAlī ibn Badhīma reported that Salmān's goods were sold and reached fourteen dirhams. > **""Salmān's goods were sold and reached fourteen dirhams.""**"
"17858","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that they were with Abū Bakr, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he asked for drink and was brought water and honey; placing it upon his hand he wept and sobbed until they thought something ailed him, asking nothing; when finished they asked O Caliph of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, what made you weep; he said once with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ he saw him warding something from himself though he saw nothing, so he asked O Messenger of Allāh, what do I see you warding though I see nothing; he said the world extended to him, so he said away from me, and it said to him you shall never attain me; Abū Bakr said it distressed him, fearing he had opposed the Messenger's ﷺ command and the world had reached him. > **""The world extended to me, so I said: away from me; and it said to me: you shall never attain me.""**"
"17859","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, raised to the Prophet ﷺ that a herald calls: leave the world to its people, leave the world to its people, leave the world to its people; whoever takes of the world more than suffices him takes carrion unknowingly. > **""Leave the world to its people; whoever takes of the world more than suffices him takes carrion unknowingly.""**"
"17860","Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab reported that the Messenger's ﷺ camel al-ʿAḍbāʾ was unbeaten, until a Bedouin on a young camel outran her, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said it is binding upon Allāh that nothing of the world rises except He lowers it; Maʿn ibn ʿĪsā said Mālik would not chain it, until one day he came out brisk and narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayra. > **""It is binding upon Allāh that nothing of the world rises except He lowers it.""**"
"17861","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ planted a peg before him, then another beside it, then a third far off, asking do you know what this is; they said Allāh and His Messenger know best; he said this is man, this his term, and this his hope — he pursues hope while his term snatches him before it. > **""This is man, this his term, and this his hope — he pursues hope while his term snatches him before it.""**"
"17862","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them both, raised to the Prophet ﷺ as far as he knew that the righteousness of this nation's first is by asceticism and certainty, and its ruin by miserliness and hope. > **""The righteousness of this nation's first is by asceticism and certainty, and its ruin is by miserliness and hope.""**"
"17863","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the Hour has drawn near while they only grow more distant from it. > **""The Hour has drawn near while they only grow more distant from it.""**"
"17864","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the nearness of the Hour is that the year be as the month, the month as the week, the week as the day, the day as the hour, and the hour as a blaze of fire; and one of you shall sleep with his term between his eyes. > **""The nearness of the Hour is that the year be as the month, the month as the week, the week as the day, the day as the hour, and the hour as a blaze of fire.""**"
"17865","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said no sun ever rises except that two angels are sent beside it calling, heard by earth's people except the two heavy ones: O people, come to your Lord — what is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts; and no sun ever sets except that two angels are sent beside it calling: O Allāh, grant the spender replacement and grant the hoarder ruin — while al-Ṭabarānī's Middle wording has O Allāh, whoever spends grant him replacement, and whoever withholds grant him ruin. > **""O people, come to your Lord — what is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts; O Allāh, grant the spender replacement and grant the hoarder ruin.""**"
"17866","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Saʿīd — with doubt whether he reported it from his father Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه — said that he heard the Prophet ﷺ upon the pulpit saying that sufficiency outweighs distracting abundance. > **""What is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts.""**"
"17867","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called the people to their Lord, contrasting sufficiency with distraction and the path of good with the path of evil. > **""O people, come to your Lord. What is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts. O people, there are only two paths: a path of good and a path of evil, yet the path of evil has not been made dearer to you than the path of good.""**"
"17868","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the one whose wealth is little, whose dependents are many, whose prayer is excellent, and who does not backbite the Muslims, indicating his closeness to him on the Day of Resurrection like two fingers. > **""Whoever has little wealth, many dependents, excellent prayer, and does not backbite the Muslims, will come on the Day of Resurrection with me like these two.""**"
"17869","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined contentment as imperishable wealth. > **""Hold fast to contentment, for contentment is wealth that never perishes.""**"
"17870","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised a year of lawful sustenance to whoever endures hunger or need without complaining to people and entrusts his affair to Allah. > **""Whoever hungers or falls into need, conceals it from people, and entrusts it to Allah, it becomes binding upon Allah to open for him a year's lawful sustenance.""**"
"17871","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no household endures severe hardship with patience except that Allah brings them provision. > **""No household endures hardship with patience except that Allah brings them provision.""**"
"17872","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness to whoever is afflicted in wealth or self, conceals it, and does not complain of it to people. > **""Whoever is afflicted by calamity in his wealth or in himself, conceals it, and does not complain of it to people, it becomes binding upon Allah to forgive him.""**"
"17873","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that a needy man left his house for the open land, whereupon his wife set the millstone, kindled the oven, and supplicated for provision, and Allah filled the bowl and the oven; when her husband lifted the millstone, the matter was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, who said it would have continued turning until the Day of Resurrection had he not lifted it. > **""Had he not lifted it, it would have continued turning until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17874","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that a man of earlier times returned from travel hungry and severely famished, his wife delayed him with hope in Allah's mercy, then found her oven filled with roasted lamb and her millstone grinding; she stopped the stone and removed the meat, and the Prophet ﷺ said that had she left what was in her millstone without breaking it, it would have ground until the Day of Resurrection. > **""Had she taken what was in her millstone and not broken it, it would have ground until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17875","Abū al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Shikhkhīr narrated from a man of Banū Sulaym, whom he believed had seen the Prophet ﷺ, that Allah tests His servant through what He has given him, blessing and expanding it for whoever is content with his portion and withholding blessing from whoever is discontent. > **""Allah tests His servant through what He has given him. Whoever is content with what has been apportioned for him, Allah blesses it for him and expands it, and whoever is not content is not blessed therein.""**"
"17876","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah tests the servant to manifest his conduct, blessing him if he is content and withholding blessing if he is not. > **""Allah indeed tests the servant to see how he acts. If he is content, he is blessed therein, and if he is not content, he is not blessed therein.""**"
"17877","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said how excellent are the disliked things, death and poverty, for he would not care by which of richness or poverty he is tested, since Allah's due stands in each: kindness in richness and patience in poverty. How excellent are the disliked things: death and poverty. By Allah, it is only richness and poverty, and I do not care by which of the two I am tested, for the due of Allah is binding in each one of them: in richness there is kindness, and in poverty there is patience."
"17878","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه also said that no harm befalls a Muslim in whatever state he begins the morning or evening, so long as there is no rancour in his soul. No harm befalls a Muslim person in whatever state he begins the morning or evening, so long as there is no rancour in his soul."
"17879","Maḥmūd ibn Labīd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said that the son of Ādam dislikes two things, though death is better than tribulation and little wealth entails less reckoning. > **""There are two things the son of Ādam dislikes: death, though death is better than tribulation; and he dislikes scarcity of wealth, though scarcity of wealth entails less reckoning.""**"
"17880","Abū Asmāʾ narrated that he visited Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه at al-Rabadhah, where Abū Dharr said his wife urged him toward Iraq while its people incline toward the world, whereas his beloved ﷺ had covenanted with him that before the bridge of Hell lies a slippery and treacherous path, and crossing it light-laden offers greater hope of salvation than crossing it heavily burdened. Before the bridge of Hell there is a path full of slipping and stumbling, and that we come upon it while our loads allow sufficiency or leanness gives greater hope of salvation than coming upon it while heavily laden."
"17881","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him to look at the most eminent man in the mosque, wearing a fine ḥullah, then at the lowliest man, wearing worn garments, and said the latter is better before Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the earth full of the former. > **""This one is better before Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the earth full of the like of that one.""**"
"17882","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that he walked with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, hand in hand, until they came upon a man of wretched appearance who complained of sickness and hardship; the Prophet ﷺ offered him words that remove such affliction, the man preferred them over witnessing Badr and Uḥud, and the Prophet ﷺ taught Abū Hurayrah those words of reliance and glorification, after which Abū Hurayrah's condition improved. > **""I place my trust in the Living who never dies. Praise belongs to Allah who has taken no son, has no partner in dominion, has no helper out of humiliation, and magnify Him with great magnification.""**"
"17883","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever asks about him or wishes to behold him should look to the dishevelled, pale, diligent one who lays no brick upon brick nor reed upon reed, for whom the banner is raised and who hastens toward it, for today is the training ground, tomorrow the race, and the goal is Paradise or Hell. > **""Whoever asks about me, or is pleased to look upon me, let him look to the dishevelled, pale one, girded for striving, who has laid no brick upon brick nor reed upon reed; a banner is raised for him and he hastens toward it. Today is the training ground, tomorrow the race, and the goal is Paradise or Hell.""**"
"17884","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after sunrise, the Prophet ﷺ said a people will come on the Day of Resurrection with light like the sun; when Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه asked if they were they, he said they have abundant good but those are the poor emigrants gathered from the ends of the earth. > **""A people will come on the Day of Resurrection whose light is like the light of the sun. They are the poor emigrants who will be gathered from the ends of the earth.""**"
"17885","In another narration of the preceding report, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما asked whether they were those radiant poor emigrants. In one narration, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar asked: Are we they?"
"17886","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked who first enters Paradise, then described the poor emigrants by whom frontiers are secured and hardships averted, who die with their needs unfulfilled in their breasts, whom Allah commands the angels to greet, and whom the angels will enter upon from every gate with salutations for their patience. > **""Do you know the first of Allah's creation to enter Paradise? They are the poor emigrants by whom the frontiers are secured, by whom hardships are averted, and one of them dies while his need remains in his breast, unable to fulfil it. Allah will say to whomever He wills among His angels: Go to them and greet them. The angels will come to them and enter upon them from every gate: Peace be upon you for what you endured with patience, and excellent is the final abode.""**"
"17887","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the first company to enter Paradise are the poor emigrants who ward off hardships, hear and obey when commanded, and die with their needs before rulers unfulfilled, whom Allah will call into an adorned Paradise without reckoning while the angels glorify Allah and greet them from every gate. > **""The first company to enter Paradise are the poor emigrants by whom hardships are averted. When they are commanded they hear and obey, and when one of them has a need before authority it remains unfulfilled until he dies with it in his breast. Allah will call Paradise on the Day of Resurrection, it will come with its splendour and adornment, and He will say: My servants who fought in My path, were killed and harmed in My path and strove in My path, enter Paradise. So they will enter it without reckoning.""**"
"17888","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the poor Muslims enter before their rich by five hundred years, describing them as those sent forth at destruction, kept back at spoils, and turned away at the doors of rulers. > **""The poor Muslims will enter before their rich by five hundred years. They are those who, when there is destruction, are sent forth, and when there is booty, others are sent; those who are turned back at the doors of rulers.""**"
"17889","Thawbān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described his Ḥawḍ from ʿAdan to ʿUmān, its cups as numerous as stars, its water whiter than snow and sweeter than honey, to which the poor emigrants will come first, dishevelled, dusty-clothed, withheld from luxurious marriages and closed doors, fulfilling their duties without receiving their dues. > **""My Ḥawḍ extends from ʿAdan to ʿUmān. Its cups are as numerous as the stars, its water whiter than snow and sweeter than honey. The first to reach it are the poor emigrants: dishevelled heads, soiled garments, who do not marry women of luxury, for whom doors are not opened, who render what is due upon them and are not given what is due to them.""**"
"17890","In another narration from the same narrator, instead of the first to reach the Ḥawḍ, the wording is that most of those who come to it are the poor emigrants. Most of those who come to it are the poor emigrants."
"17891","Abū al-Ṣiddīq narrated from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, from the Prophet ﷺ, that the poor believers enter Paradise four hundred years before the rich, until the rich man wishes he had been destitute, and described them as those sent forth at hardship, set aside at comfort, and veiled from doors. > **""The poor believers will enter Paradise four hundred years before the rich, until the rich man says: Would that I had been destitute. They are those who, when there is hardship, are sent forth to it, and when there is blessing, others are sent for it, and they are those who are veiled from doors.""**"
"17892","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say the poor of his Ummah enter Paradise forty autumns before the rich, describing them as soiled in garments, dishevelled in heads, denied entry at gates, unable to marry women of luxury, through whom the east and west are sustained, rendering all dues upon them while not receiving all dues owed to them. > **""The poor of my Ummah will enter Paradise forty autumns before their rich: soiled in garments, dishevelled in heads, who are not admitted at gates, who do not marry women of luxury, through whom the east and west of the earth are sustained, who render all that is upon them and are not given all that is theirs.""**"
"17893","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the poor of his Ummah enter before their rich by forty autumns, or forty years. > **""The poor of my Ummah will enter before their rich by forty autumns, or by forty years.""**"
"17894","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the poor Muslims enter half a day — a day with one's Lord like a thousand years — before the rich, and that they all enter in the image of Ādam, whose stature is twelve cubits tall and six wide by the cubit of a tall man. > **""The poor Muslims will enter before their rich by half a day. And they will all enter in the image of Ādam: twelve cubits tall in the sky and six wide, by the cubit of a tall man.""**"
"17895","ʿIrbaḍ ibn Sāriyah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ came out to the people of al-Ṣuffah wearing a small turban and said that had they known what is stored for them they would not grieve over what is withheld, and that Persia and Rome would be opened. > **""Had you known what is stored for you, you would not have grieved over what has been withheld from you, and Persia and Rome will surely be opened.""**"
"17896","Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه said he would never remain behind from the first expedition after hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the poor Muslims will come on the Day of Resurrection upon their saddles, be told to halt for reckoning, reply that they were given nothing to be reckoned for, and enter Paradise forty years before people. > **""The poor Muslims will come on the Day of Resurrection upon their saddles. It will be said to them: Halt for reckoning. They will say: You gave us nothing to reckon us for. So they will enter Paradise forty years before people.""**"
"17897","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sābiṭ narrated that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه appointed Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir over an expedition toward enemy land, whereupon Saʿīd recalled hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the poor Muslims will be herded like doves, told to halt for reckoning, swear by Allah they left nothing to be reckoned for, be declared truthful by Allah, and enter Paradise seventy years before people. > **""The poor Muslims will be herded like doves are herded. It will be said to them: Halt for reckoning. They will say: By Allah, we left nothing to be reckoned for. Allah will say: My servants have spoken truthfully. So they will enter Paradise seventy years before people.""**"
"17898","After the preceding report, the like of it was also narrated from Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir, from the Prophet ﷺ, in similar wording. The like of it was narrated from Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir, from the Prophet ﷺ."
"17899","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that he was among the people of al-Ṣuffah, none with a complete garment and sweat streaked with dust and grime, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and announced glad tidings for the poor emigrants; then a well-adorned man came who vied to outdo the Prophet's speech, and after he left the Prophet ﷺ said Allah dislikes such as him who twist their tongues like cattle twist theirs upon pasture, and Allah will likewise twist their tongues and faces in Hell. > **""Let the poor emigrants receive glad tidings. Allah does not love this one and his like, who twist their tongues as cattle twist their tongues upon pasture; likewise Allah will twist their tongues and faces in the Fire.""**"
"17900","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked into Paradise and saw most of its people were the poor, and looked into Hell and saw most of its people were the rich and women. > **""I looked into Paradise and saw most of its people were the poor, and I looked into Hell and saw most of its people were the rich and women.""**"
"17901","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ looked into Paradise and saw most of its people were the weak and poor, and looked into Hell and saw most of its people were women. > **""I looked into Paradise and saw most of its people were the weak and the poor, and I looked into Hell and saw most of its people were women.""**"
"17902","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Paradise, heard footsteps before him and was told they were Bilāl's رضي الله عنه, saw most of its people were emigrants and offspring of Muslims and fewest were the rich and women, the rich being detained for reckoning and sifting while women were distracted by gold and silk; then at a gate of Paradise he outweighed his Ummah in the balance, as did Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما after him, while ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه came late, saying his abundant wealth had long detained him in reckoning and sifting. > **""I entered Paradise and heard footsteps before me. I asked: What is this? He said: Bilāl. I passed on, and most of the people of Paradise were the emigrants and the offspring of the Muslims, and I saw therein none fewer than the rich and women. As for the rich, they are here at the gate, being reckoned and sifted, and as for women, the two red things distracted them: gold and silk.""**"
"17903","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghunm narrated that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه appointed Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه over Syria, commanding him to pay stipends and campaign, and as Muʿādh distributed late in the day a villager asked precedence before nightfall, but Muʿādh prioritised townspeople, citing that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say all prophets enter Paradise two thousand years before Dāwūd and Sulaymān عليهما السلام, the poor Muslims forty years before the rich, and townspeople forty years before villagers by virtue of Friday, congregations, circles of remembrance, and their exclusive exposure to tribulation. > **""All the prophets will enter Paradise two thousand years before Dāwūd and Sulaymān, and the poor Muslims will enter Paradise forty years before their rich, and the people of towns will enter Paradise forty years before the people of villages, for towns excel through Friday, congregations, and circles of remembrance, and when tribulation comes they are singled out by it apart from them.""**"
"17904","Umayyah ibn Khālid ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Usayd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to seek opening through the poor Muslims. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to seek opening through the poor Muslims."
"17905","In another narration, the wording is that he used to seek aid through the poor Muslims. He used to seek aid through the poor Muslims."
"17906","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated to live as a poor man, die as a poor man, and be gathered among the poor. > **""O Allah, keep me alive as a poor man, cause me to die as a poor man, and gather me among the company of the poor.""**"
"17907","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه narrated that his beloved ﷺ commanded him with seven: love of the poor and nearness to them, looking to those below and not above, maintaining kinship though it turns away, asking nothing of anyone, speaking truth though bitter, fearing no blame in Allah's cause, and abundant utterance of lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh, for these are from a treasure beneath the Throne. > **""My beloved commanded me with seven: love of the poor and nearness to them; to look to one below me and not to one above me; to maintain kinship though it turns away; to ask no one for anything; to speak the truth though it be bitter; to fear in Allah's cause no blame of any blamer; and to say abundantly: There is no power nor strength except through Allah, for they are from a treasure beneath the Throne.""**"
"17908","In another narration of Abū Dharr's report, the wording includes the command to be merciful to the poor and to sit with them. He commanded me to be merciful to the poor and to sit with them."
"17909","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to teach five: love of the poor and nearness to them, looking to those below and not above, maintaining kinship though it turns away, speaking truth though bitter, and abundant utterance of lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh. > **""Shall I not teach you five: love of the poor and nearness to them; look to one below you and do not look to one above you; maintain kinship though it turns away; speak the truth though it be bitter; and say abundantly: There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"17910","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, in marfūʿ form, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said the food of a household may be little while their houses shine with light. > **""The food of a household may be little, yet their houses shine with light.""**"
"17911","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that before them lies a formidable pass from which none is saved except everyone lightly laden. > **""Before you lies a formidable pass from which none is saved except everyone lightly laden.""**"
"17912","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out holding Abū Dharr's hand and said before them lies a formidable pass ascended only by the lightly laden; when a man asked whether he was lightly or heavily laden, the Prophet ﷺ asked about food for one day, for tomorrow, and for the day after, and said possession of three days' food would place him among the heavily laden. > **""O Abū Dharr, do you know that before us lies a formidable pass which none ascends except the lightly laden? Do you have food for a day? And food for tomorrow? And food for the day after? Had you food for three days, you would be among the heavily laden.""**"
"17913","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said two believers, rich and poor, who lived in the world will meet at the gate of Paradise; the poor enters while the rich is detained as Allah wills, then enters and is met by the poor asking what detained him, and he replies he was terribly detained until sweat poured from him enough to quench a thousand camels feeding on salty herbage. > **""Two believers will meet at the gate of Paradise: a rich believer and a poor believer who lived in the world. The poor will be admitted to Paradise, and the rich detained as long as Allah wills to detain him. Then he will be admitted, and the poor will meet him and say: O my brother, what detained you? By Allah, you were detained until I feared for you. He will say: O my brother, after you I was detained in a dreadful, hateful detention, and before reaching you sweat poured from me such that had a thousand camels, all feeding on salty herbage, come to it, they would have left quenched.""**"
"17914","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said most of the people of Paradise are the simple-hearted, whose nature inclines to good and who are heedless of evil. > **""Most of the people of Paradise are the simple-hearted.""**"
"17915","The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that many a weak, lowly man, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it. > **""Many a weak, lowly man, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it.""**"
"17916","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه narrated that while with the Prophet ﷺ at a funeral, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether to inform them of Allah's worst servants, the harsh and arrogant, and of Allah's best servants, the weak, lowly one in two worn garments, disregarded, whose oath Allah would fulfil. > **""Shall I not inform you of the worst servants of Allah? The harsh, the arrogant. Shall I not inform you of the best servants of Allah? The weak, lowly one in two worn garments, disregarded, who, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it.""**"
"17917","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ offered to inform them of the people of Hell and Paradise: the people of Paradise are every weak, lowly one in two worn garments whose oath Allah fulfils, while the people of Hell are every coarse, insolent hoarder who withholds and is followed by retinues. > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Hell and the people of Paradise? As for the people of Paradise, they are every weak, lowly one in two worn garments who, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it. As for the people of Hell, they are every coarse, insolent one, a hoarder, a withholder, with followers.""**"
"17918","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that many a dishevelled, dusty one in two worn garments, turned away from people's doors, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it. > **""Many a dishevelled, dusty one in two worn garments, turned away from people's doors, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it.""**"
"17919","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, in marfūʿ form, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said that many a man in two worn garments, disregarded, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it. > **""Many a man in two worn garments, disregarded, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it.""**"
"17920","Thawbān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that among his Ummah are those to whom none would give a dīnār, dirham, or even a fals if asked, yet were they to ask Allah for Paradise He would grant it: one in two worn garments, disregarded, whose oath Allah fulfils. > **""Among my Ummah are those who, were one of you to come asking one of them for a dīnār, he would not give it; were he to ask for a dirham, he would not give it; were he to ask for a fals, he would not give it; yet were he to ask Allah for Paradise, He would grant it to him: one in two worn garments, disregarded, who, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it.""**"
"17921","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said wretched is the slave of the dīnār, the dirham, and fine garments, pleased when given and angry when withheld, miserable and overturned, with no relief when pierced by a thorn; and blessed is the servant holding his horse's bridle in Allah's path, dishevelled and dusty-footed, steadfast in guard or rearguard, whose intercession is unaccepted and whose permission is denied. > **""Wretched is the slave of the dīnār, wretched is the slave of the dirham, wretched is the slave of fine garments: if given he is pleased, if withheld he is angry; wretched and overturned, and when pierced by a thorn may he find no relief. Blessed is a servant holding the bridle of his horse in the path of Allah, dishevelled in head, dusty in feet: whether stationed in guard or in rearguard he remains there; if he intercedes he is not granted intercession, and if he seeks permission he is not permitted.""**"
"17922","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform them of the people of Paradise, every weak, lowly one in two worn garments, disregarded, whose oath Allah fulfils, and of the people of Hell, every jazza, jaʿẓa, and arrogant one; and when asked, he explained jazza as the bulky and jaʿẓa as the self-magnifying. > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? Every weak, lowly one in two worn garments, disregarded, who, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it. Shall I not inform you of the people of Hell? Every jazza, jaʿẓa, arrogant one. Jazza is the bulky, and jaʿẓa is the one mighty in his own self.""**"
"17923","Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform him of the people of Paradise and Hell: the people of Hell are every coarse, insolent, arrogant one, while the people of Paradise are the weak and overpowered. > **""O Surāqah, shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise and the people of Hell? As for the people of Hell, they are every coarse, insolent, arrogant one. As for the people of Paradise, they are the weak and overpowered.""**"
"17924","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform him of the people of Paradise, and said they are the weak and overpowered. > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? They are the weak and overpowered.""**"
"17925","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform him of the people of Hell, every coarse, insolent, arrogant hoarder who withholds, and of the people of Paradise, every poor one whose oath Allah fulfils. > **""Shall I not inform you, O Abū al-Dardāʾ, of the people of Hell? Every coarse, insolent, arrogant one, a hoarder, a withholder. Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? Every poor one who, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it.""**"
"17926","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform them of the people of Paradise, every weak, lowly one whose oath Allah fulfils, and of the people of Hell, every cruel, insolent one. > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? Every weak, lowly one who, were he to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it. Shall I not inform you of the people of Hell? Every cruel, insolent one.""**"
"17927","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him to look at the highest man in the mosque, adorned in a ḥullah, then at the lowest man, in worn garments, and said the latter is better before Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the earth full of the former. > **""Look at the highest man in the mosque. Look at the lowest man in the mosque. This one is better before Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the earth full of the like of that one.""**"
"17928","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said Allah has treasured ones among His creation whom He keeps alive in well-being and takes unto His Paradise at death, over whom tribulations pass like portions of dark night while they remain in well-being therein. > **""Allah has treasured ones among His creation. He keeps them alive in well-being, and when He takes them He takes them unto His Paradise. Tribulations pass over them like portions of the dark night, while they remain therein in well-being.""**"
"17929","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, in marfūʿ form, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ asked whether to inform them of the people of Paradise, the weak and wronged; of the people of Hell, every coarse one; of their best, those finest in character; and of their worst, the garrulous, the mouth-stretching, and the grandiloquent. > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? The weak and wronged. Shall I not inform you of the people of Hell? Every coarse one. Shall I not inform you of the best among you? Those finest among you in character. Shall I not inform you of the worst among you? The garrulous, the mouth-stretching, the grandiloquent.""**"
"17930","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Mūsā ibn ʿImrān عليه السلام passed a trembling man and stood supplicating for his healing, whereupon he was told this was not possession by Satan but hunger of the soul for Allah, that Allah beholds him many times daily, marvels at his obedience, and grants him a daily answered supplication, so Mūsā should bid him supplicate for him. > **""O Mūsā, what befalls him is not possession by Satan, but hunger of his soul for Me, as you see. I behold him many times each day, marvelling at his obedience. Bid him supplicate for you, for he has with Me an answered supplication each day.""**"
"17931","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed through a lane of Madīnah, saw a deceased Black man cast upon the road, asked whose slave he was and whether he was seen praying, commanded washing and shrouding, led his funeral prayer, and exclaimed glory be to Allah, explaining afterward that the angels had nearly intervened between them for their multitude praying over him. > **""Rise, wash him and shroud him. Glory be to Allah, glory be to Allah. The angels nearly intervened between me and him for their multitude praying over him.""**"
"17932","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while people were praying and supplicating, told them to remain in their state and gave glad tidings to the company of emigrants of triumph over the rich on the Day of Resurrection by five hundred years, until the rich will wish he had been a beggar. > **""Remain in what you are engaged in. Receive glad tidings, O company of emigrants, of triumph on the Day of Resurrection over the rich by five hundred years, until the rich will wish he had been a beggar.""**"
"17933","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said Mūsā عليه السلام asked about the straitened believing servant and the eased disbelieving servant, whereupon Allah opened for each a door to Paradise and Hell respectively, and Mūsā swore by Allah's might that lifelong dragging upon the face would be as nothing beside such Paradise, and lifelong worldly possession as nothing beside such Hell. > **""O Mūsā, this is what I have prepared for him. By Your might and majesty, had he been severed in hands and feet and dragged upon his face since the day You created him until the Day of Resurrection, and this were his end, he would never have seen misery. O Mūsā, this is what I have prepared for him. By Your might and majesty, had the world been his since the day You created him until the Day of Resurrection, and this were his end, he would never have seen good.""**"
"17934","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the most wretched of the wretched is he upon whom the poverty of this world and the punishment of the Hereafter gather. > **""The most wretched of the wretched is he upon whom gather the poverty of this world and the punishment of the Hereafter.""**"
"17935","Abū ʿUsayb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out at night, called him, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهم, entered a garden of an Anṣārī, ate unripe dates with them and drank cold water, then said they will be asked about this on the Day of Resurrection; when ʿUmar struck the cluster to the ground and asked, he said yes, except for a rag covering nakedness, a morsel repelling hunger, or a shelter guarding against heat and cold. > **""You will surely be asked about this on the Day of Resurrection. Yes, except for three: a rag with which one covers his nakedness, a morsel with which one repels his hunger, or a shelter wherein one withdraws from heat and cold.""**"
"17936","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that what exceeds the waist-wrapper, the shade of a wall, and drawn water is surplus for which the servant will be reckoned or questioned on the Day of Resurrection. > **""What exceeds the waist-wrapper, the shade of a wall, and drawn water is surplus for which the servant will be reckoned on the Day of Resurrection, or questioned about.""**"
"17937","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه narrated that some Companions came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked whether they earn reward when passing markets, desiring fruits without money to buy them, and he replied that reward is only in such restraint. > **""And is reward except in that?""**"
"17938","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade humbling oneself before the rich. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade humbling oneself before the rich."
"17939","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah has servants who recognise people by discernment of marks. > **""Allah has servants who recognise people by discernment.""**"
"17940","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said to beware the insight of the believer, for he sees by the light of Allah. > **""Beware the insight of the believer, for he sees by the light of Allah.""**"
"17941","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that the most discerning of people were three, citing the conduct of Mūsā عليه السلام at the well, the conduct of Yūsuf's عليه السلام master, and the succession of ʿUmar رضي الله عنه. The most discerning of people were three: the companion of Mūsā who said hire him for he is strong and trustworthy, for she saw him lift a rock requiring many men and place her behind him rather than before him; the companion of Yūsuf who said honour his abode that he may benefit us or we take him as a son; and Abū Bakr when he appointed ʿUmar as successor."
"17942","In another narration of the preceding report the wording is that among the most discerning of people are three. Among the most discerning of people are three."
"17943","ʿAlī ibn Zayd narrated that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه was asked to describe the people of the cities and characterised the people of Ḥijāz, Iraq, Syria, and Egypt. The people of Ḥijāz are the most eager of people for tribulation and the most incapable regarding it; the people of Iraq are the most eager for knowledge and the farthest from it; the people of Syria are the most obedient of people to the creature in disobedience of the Creator; and the people of Egypt are the shrewdest of people when young and the most foolish when old."
"17944","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the mine of taqwā. > **""Everything has a mine, and the mine of taqwā is the hearts of those who know Allah.""**"
"17945","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned three matters necessitating Allah's friendship and then described the wali of Allah while forming thirty with his hand. > **""Among what necessitates the friendship of Allah are three: when one sees a right from the rights of Allah he does not defer it to days he may not reach; that one performs righteous deeds openly upon the same standing as one's deeds in secret; and that one joins with what is hastened the welfare of what is hoped for. Such is the wali of Allah.""**"
"17946","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked who the family of Muḥammad are and answered with piety and recitation of a verse. > **""Every pious one. His allies are none but the pious.""**"
"17947","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ no share of nobility increased or decreased a person except through taqwā. No share of nobility with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ increased or decreased anyone except through taqwā."
"17948","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that freedom from sin could expose them to something greater. > **""Had you not committed sins, I would have feared for you what is greater than that: self-conceit.""**"
"17949","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the divine warning against enmity toward His walī. > **""Allah says: Whoever shows enmity to a walī of Mine has made war with Me lawful.""**"
"17950","Maymūnah, wife of the Prophet رضي الله عنها, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed Allah's declaration concerning harm to His walī, nearness through obligations and voluntary deeds, and the divine hesitation over the death of the beloved servant. > **""Allah says: Whoever harms a walī of Mine has made war with Me lawful. My servant draws near to Me with nothing like performance of My obligation, and he continues drawing near to Me through voluntary deeds until I love him. When I love him I become his leg with which he walks, his hand with which he grasps, his tongue with which he speaks, and his heart with which he understands. If he asks Me I give him, if he supplicates Me I answer him, and I do not hesitate over anything I do as I hesitate over his death, for he dislikes death and I dislike displeasing him.""**"
"17951","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ, from Jibrīl عليه السلام, from Allah the Exalted, a warning against demeaning His walī. > **""Whoever demeans a walī of Mine has openly challenged Me to war.""**"
"17952","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed Allah's declaration against enmity toward His walī. > **""Allah says: Whoever shows enmity to a walī of Mine has set himself in combat with Me.""**"
"17953","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed Allah's declaration concerning enmity toward His walī, hesitation over the believer's death, diversion between richness and poverty according to benefit, and the reward for preferring divine desire. > **""Allah says: Whoever shows enmity to a walī of Mine has set himself in combat with Me. I do not hesitate over anything I do as I hesitate over the death of the believer, for he dislikes death and I dislike displeasing him. Sometimes My believing walī asks Me for richness and I divert him from richness to poverty, for had I diverted him to richness it would have been evil for him; and sometimes My believing walī asks Me for poverty and I divert him to richness, for had I diverted him to poverty it would have been evil for him. By My might, majesty, exaltedness, splendour, beauty, and loftiness of station: no servant prefers My desire over his own desire except that I fix his term before his aid, heaven and earth guarantee his provision, and I stand for him beyond the commerce of every merchant.""**"
"17954","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah's marvel at the youth without youthful inclination. > **""Allah marvels at a youth in whom there is no youthful folly.""**"
"17955","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the reward of ninety-nine ṣiddīqs to the young one raised in worship until death overtakes him. > **""There is no young one raised in worship until death overtakes him except that Allah grants him the reward of ninety-nine ṣiddīqs.""**"
"17956","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted youth resembling elders with elders resembling youth. > **""The best of your youth are those who resemble your elders, and the worst of your elders are those who resemble your youth.""**"
"17957","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted youth resembling the middle-aged with the middle-aged resembling youth. > **""The best of your youth are those who resemble your elders, and the worst of your elders are those who resemble your youth.""**"
"17958","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah's dislike for a man of seventy in the gait and appearance of twenty. > **""Allah dislikes a son of seventy in the guise of a son of twenty in his gait and appearance.""**"
"17959","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the ruling for imitation. > **""Whoever imitates a people is among them.""**"
"17960","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described affection from Allah and repute from heaven, and the proclamation of Jibrīl عليه السلام in love and hatred. > **""Affection is from Allah and repute is from heaven. When Allah loves a servant He says to Jibrīl: I love so-and-so, so love him. Then Jibrīl proclaims that Allah loves so-and-so, so love him. Then love for him descends upon earth. And when He hates a servant He says to Jibrīl: I hate so-and-so, so hate him. Then Jibrīl proclaims that your Lord hates so-and-so, so hate him. Then hatred for him proceeds upon earth.""**"
"17961","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said every servant has repute in heaven which is then placed upon earth. > **""There is no servant except that he has repute in heaven. If his repute is good it is placed upon earth, and if his repute in heaven is evil it is placed upon earth.""**"
"17962","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Nabāwah foretell distinction between the people of Paradise and Hell through praise. > **""It is imminent that the people of Paradise be distinguished from the people of Hell through good praise and evil praise.""**"
"17963","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ how to know his good and evil, and was told by the testimony of neighbours. > **""When you hear your neighbours saying: You have done well, then you have done well. And when you hear them saying: You have done evil, then you have done evil.""**"
"17964","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Qays al-Fihrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ linked people's greeting of welcome or rejection to the Day of Resurrection. > **""When a man comes to a people and they say: Welcome, then welcome to him on the day he meets his Lord. And when a man comes to a people and they say: Away, then away to him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17965","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ was asked who the people of Paradise and Hell are and answered by what fills their hearing before death. > **""Who is of the people of Paradise? One who does not die until Allah fills his hearing with what he loves. Who is of the people of Hell? One who does not die until Allah fills his hearing with what he hates.""**"
"17966","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ identified the most beloved and most hateful to Allah with those most beloved and hateful to people. > **""Shall I not inform you of the most beloved among you to Allah? The most beloved among you to Allah are the most beloved among you to people. Shall I not inform you of the most hateful among you to Allah? The most hateful among you to Allah are the most hateful among you to people.""**"
"17967","Thawbān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ described the servant seeking Allah's pleasure until Jibrīl and the heavens proclaim mercy for him, reciting the verse of affection, and the servant seeking Allah's anger until wrath descends. > **""The servant seeks the pleasure of Allah and continues thus until Allah says: O Jibrīl, My servant so-and-so seeks to please Me, and My pleasure is upon him. Then Jibrīl says: May the mercy of Allah be upon so-and-so, and the bearers of the Throne say it, and those next to them, until the inhabitants of the seven heavens say it, then it descends to earth. And this is the verse Allah sent down: Those who believe and do righteous deeds, the Most Merciful will appoint affection for them. And the servant seeks the anger of Allah until Allah says: O Jibrīl, so-and-so incurs My anger, and My wrath is upon him. Then Jibrīl says: Allah is wrathful with so-and-so, and the bearers of the Throne say it, and those beneath them, until the inhabitants of the seven heavens say it, then it descends to earth.""**"
"17968","ʿAmr ibn Mālik al-Ruʾāsī رضي الله عنه narrated that he asked the Prophet ﷺ for his pleasure, was turned from thrice, then reminded him that the Lord is entreated and accepts, whereupon he was pleased with him. > **""I came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, be pleased with me. He turned from me thrice. I said: O Messenger of Allah, the Lord is entreated and He accepts. So he was pleased with me.""**"
"17969","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say Allah multiplies praise of the servant sevenfold beyond his deeds according to divine pleasure or anger. > **""When Allah is pleased with a servant He praises him sevenfold of good he did not do, and when He is wrathful with a servant He censures him sevenfold of evil he did not do.""**"
"17970","In another narration the wording is when He loves and when He hates, with ninefold instead of sevenfold. When He loves and when He hates, He praises ninefold."
"17971","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated that he was informed that members of a household follow one another into Hell until none remain, and likewise into Paradise until none remain. Members of a household follow one another into Hell until no free person, slave, nor maid remains among them, and members of a household follow one another into Paradise until no free person, slave, nor maid remains among them."
"17972","Al-Ḥārith ibn ʿUmayrah narrated that he went to al-Madāʾin, met Salmān رضي الله عنه in worn garments with red leather, was greeted by name though never seen before, and Salmān explained that his soul recognised his soul and cited the Prophet ﷺ on assembled souls. > **""Souls are assembled troops. Those among them that recognised one another in Allah harmonise, and those among them that were unacquainted in Allah differ.""**"
"17973","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said souls are assembled troops in recognition and estrangement. > **""Souls are assembled troops. Those among them that recognised one another harmonise, and those among them that were unacquainted differ.""**"
"17974","ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated that a jesting woman of Makkah lodged with a woman like her, and when this reached ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها she declared her beloved ﷺ truthful and cited his saying on assembled souls. > **""Souls are assembled troops. Those among them that recognised one another harmonise, and those among them that were unacquainted differ.""**"
"17975","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who does neither. > **""The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended.""**"
"17976","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who does neither. > **""The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended.""**"
"17977","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who does neither. > **""The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended.""**"
"17978","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who does neither. The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended."
"17979","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the souls of believers meet across a day's journey though none has ever seen his companion. > **""The souls of believers meet across a day's journey, though none among them has ever seen his companion.""**"
"17980","In another narration the wording is that they meet across a day and a night's journey. They meet across a day and a night's journey."
"17981","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved none but the pious. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved none but one possessed of taqwā."
"17982","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said part of faith is loving a man only for Allah without wealth received. > **""Part of faith is that a man loves another man whom he loves only for Allah, without wealth given to him, for that is faith.""**"
"17983","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said loving a servant for Allah honours one's Lord. > **""No servant loves another servant for Allah except that he honours his Lord.""**"
"17984","Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd narrated that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه complained of need to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who commanded patience and likened the haste of poverty toward his lovers to a torrent descending a valley or mountain. > **""Be patient, O Abū Saʿīd, for poverty hastens to whoever loves me among you swifter than a torrent from the top of a valley and from the top of a mountain to its foot.""**"
"17985","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ declaring love for him, and was told to prepare for destitution. > **""Prepare for destitution.""**"
"17986","Mujāhid narrated that a man passed by Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, who said this man loves him, and when asked how he knew, replied because he loves him. This man loves me, for I love him."
"17987","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said no two servants loving in Allah meet, shake hands, and send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ except that they part forgiven for past and future sins. > **""No two servants love one another in Allah, one meets his companion and shakes his hand, and they send blessings upon the Prophet, except that they do not part until their sins are forgiven, past and future.""**"
"17988","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said whoever looks at his brother with affection without rancour is forgiven past sins before his eyelid moves. > **""Whoever looks at his brother with a glance of affection, with no malice in his heart against him, does not blink before he is forgiven for his past sins.""**"
"17989","A man of Banū Salīṭ narrated that he came to the Prophet ﷺ among a crowd and heard him declare the Muslim the brother of the Muslim, point to his chest for taqwā, and warn that two loving in Allah part only through a new offence committed by one, and the offender is evil thrice over. > **""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he neither wrongs him nor forsakes him. Taqwā is here. No two men love one another in Allah but they are separated only by an offence committed by one of them, and the offender is evil, the offender is evil, the offender is evil.""**"
"17990","ʿUmar رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ declared the Muslim the brother of the Muslim and swore that two loving ones are separated only by a sin committed by one of them. > **""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he neither wrongs him nor forsakes him. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, no two love one another but they are separated only by a sin committed by one of them.""**"
"17991","Abū al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنه narrated that a Companion's newborn was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who stroked his forehead and supplicated, whereupon hair like a horse's mane grew; when the boy loved the Khawārij and joined them the hair fell, his father bound and confined him, and after admonition he renounced them and the hair regrew. A Companion's boy was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who stroked his forehead and supplicated so that hair grew like a horse's mane. He then loved the Khawārij and the hair fell from his forehead, so his father bound him until he renounced their view and hated them, whereupon the hair grew again."
"17992","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every soul is gathered upon its desire, so one desiring disbelief is with the disbelievers and his knowledge avails nothing. > **""Every soul is gathered upon its desire. Whoever desires disbelief is with the disbelievers, and his knowledge avails him nothing.""**"
"17993","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ met him, took his hand, and said among the believers are those for whom his heart softens. > **""O Abū Umāmah, among the believers are those for whom my heart softens.""**"
"17994","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said of two loving in Allah, the dearer to Allah loves his companion more intensely. > **""No two men love one another in Allah except that the dearer of them to Allah is the one who loves his companion more intensely.""**"
"17995","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه, in marfūʿ form, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said of two loving in Allah behind absence, the dearer to Allah loves his companion more intensely. > **""No two men love one another in Allah behind absence except that the dearer of them to Allah is the one who loves his companion more intensely.""**"
"17996","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated that he gathered his people, and after prayer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed them concerning servants neither prophets nor martyrs envied for their stations and nearness; a bedouin asked for their description, the Prophet's ﷺ face rejoiced, and he described them as diverse people without kinship loving purely in Allah, seated on pulpits of light with luminous faces and garments, fearless while people fear, and they are Allah's allies without fear or grief. > **""O people, hear, understand, and know that Allah has servants who are neither prophets nor martyrs, envied by prophets and martyrs for their stations and nearness to Allah. They are people from diverse regions and distant tribes, joined by no close kinship; they loved one another in Allah and were pure. Allah will place for them pulpits of light on the Day of Resurrection, seat them upon them, make their faces light and their garments light. People will fear on the Day of Resurrection while they do not fear, and they are the allies of Allah upon whom there is no fear nor do they grieve.""**"
"17997","In another narration the reporter was with the Prophet ﷺ when the verse forbidding questions was revealed, then asked and was told of servants neither prophets nor martyrs envied for their seat and nearness, with addition of pulpits of light of pearl before the Most Merciful. > **""Allah has servants who are neither prophets nor martyrs, envied by prophets and martyrs for their seat and nearness to Allah, upon pulpits of light of pearl before the Most Merciful.""**"
"17998","Shahr ibn Ḥawshab narrated that a Companion named Mālik taught them ablution and brief complete prayer as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed, then conveyed his saying about people neither prophets nor martyrs envied for their place; a bedouin asked for their names, the Prophet's ﷺ face shone, and he described them as diverse tribes loving in Allah with luminous faces upon light, fearless and griefless. > **""I know people who are neither prophets nor martyrs, envied by prophets and martyrs for their place with Allah. They are people from diverse tribes loving one another in Allah. Their faces are light and they are upon light. They do not fear when people fear, nor grieve when people grieve.""**"
"17999","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah has companions on the Day of Resurrection at the right of the Throne, though both His Hands are right, upon pulpits of light with faces of light, neither prophets, martyrs, nor truthful ones: those loving through Allah's majesty. > **""Allah has companions on the Day of Resurrection at the right of the Throne upon pulpits of light, their faces of light, neither prophets, martyrs, nor truthful ones. They are those loving one another through the majesty of Allah.""**"
"18000","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah has servants neither prophets nor martyrs envied by prophets and martyrs on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Allah has servants who are neither prophets nor martyrs, envied by prophets and martyrs on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18001","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say those loving in Allah stand in Allah's shade upon pulpits of light, fearless while people fear, and through their mention Allah averts punishment intended for earth's inhabitants. > **""Those loving one another in Allah are in Allah's shade on the Day of Resurrection, when there is no shade but His, upon pulpits of light. People will fear while they do not fear. When Allah intends punishment for earth's inhabitants, He remembers them and averts punishment from them through mentioning them.""**"
"18002","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah seats servants on pulpits of light with light covering their faces until reckoning of creation ends. > **""Allah has servants whom He seats on the Day of Resurrection upon pulpits of light, with light covering their faces, until He finishes reckoning creation.""**"
"18003","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said those loving in Allah stand upon chairs of ruby around the Throne. > **""Those loving one another in Allah stand upon chairs of ruby around the Throne.""**"
"18004","Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no two love in Allah except two chairs are placed and they are seated until Allah finishes reckoning, whereupon Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه confirmed him. > **""No two love one another in Allah except two chairs are placed for them and they are seated upon them until Allah finishes reckoning.""**"
"18005","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say those loving in Allah stand upon a pillar of ruby with a hollow ruby tent sixty miles into heaven, with spouses in every direction unknown to others, and one overlooking fills Paradise with light. > **""Those loving one another in Allah stand upon a pillar of ruby with a tent of hollow ruby sixty miles into heaven, with spouses in every direction unknown to others. One of them overlooks the people of Paradise and fills them with light, until they ask what light has appeared, and it is said: A man from among those loving one another has overlooked you.""**"
"18006","Abū Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said Paradise contains chambers whose outer is seen from inner and inner from outer, prepared for those loving, visiting, and spending in Allah. > **""In Paradise are chambers whose outer is seen from their inner and whose inner is seen from their outer, prepared by Allah for those loving in Him, visiting in Him, and spending in Him.""**"
"18007","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said Paradise contains ruby pillars bearing peridot chambers with open doors shining like a brilliant star, inhabited by those loving, spending, and meeting in Allah. > **""In Paradise are pillars of ruby bearing chambers of peridot with open doors, shining as the brilliant star shines. Who dwells in them? Those loving in Allah, spending in Allah, and meeting in Allah.""**"
"18008","Abū Muslim al-Khawlānī narrated that he entered the mosque of Ḥimṣ finding thirty-two Companions around the youthful Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه, declared love for Allah alone, was given glad tidings with the Prophet's ﷺ saying on lovers in Allah's shade envied by prophets and martyrs, then conveyed it to ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه who added the divine saying that His love is due for those loving, advising, visiting, and spending in Him upon pulpits of light envied by prophets and truthful ones. > **""Those loving one another in Allah are in Allah's shade on a day with no shade but His, envied by prophets and martyrs for their place. My love is due for those loving one another in Me, advising one another in Me, visiting one another in Me, and spending one another in Me, upon pulpits of light envied by prophets and truthful ones for their place.""**"
"18009","Aḥmad narrated abridged through Abū Idrīs, who sat in a gathering of twenty Companions of the Prophet ﷺ among them a young handsome youth, then mentioned the like abridged. Abū Idrīs sat in a gathering with twenty Companions of the Prophet ﷺ among them a young handsome youth, concerning those loving in Allah."
"18010","In another narration with him the addition is those sitting together for Me. And those sitting together in Me."
"18011","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said there are men neither prophets nor martyrs granted pulpits and faces of light on the Day of Resurrection, secured from the greatest terror: tribal outcasts loving in Allah. > **""There are men who are neither prophets nor martyrs, for whom pulpits of light are placed on the Day of Resurrection, their faces of light, secured from the greatest terror. They are outcasts of tribes loving one another in Allah.""**"
"18012","Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah said those loving for His majesty stand in His Throne's shade when no shade but His exists. > **""Allah says: Those loving one another for My majesty stand in My Throne's shade on a day with no shade but Mine.""**"
"18013","Shuraḥbīl ibn al-Simṭ narrated that he asked ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah رضي الله عنه for a hadith heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without forgetfulness or falsehood, and was told the divine saying that His love is due for those loving, visiting, spending, and acting sincerely for His sake, and that no believing man or woman loses three prepubescent children of his loins except Allah admits him to Paradise by mercy toward them. > **""Allah says: My love is due for those loving one another for My sake, My love is due for those visiting one another for My sake, My love is due for those spending one another for My sake, and My love is due for those acting sincerely one another for My sake. No believing man or woman whose three children of his loins, not reaching puberty, precede him except Allah admits him to Paradise by His mercy toward them.""**"
"18014","In another narration the wording affirms His love is due for those acting sincerely for His sake and those aiding for His sake. His love is due for those acting sincerely for His sake, and His love is due for those aiding one another for His sake."
"18015","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever loves a man for Allah is loved by Allah and both enter Paradise, with the lover higher in rank and the beloved joined to him. > **""Whoever loves a man for Allah, Allah loves him, and both enter Paradise together. The one who loved for Allah is higher in station, and the one loved for Allah is joined to him.""**"
"18016","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described visitations among the people of Paradise upon camels with cushions, the higher visiting the lower, except those loving in Allah who visit wherever they will. > **""The people of Paradise visit one another upon camels bearing cushions. The people of ʿIlliyyīn visit those below them, while those below do not visit the people of ʿIlliyyīn, except those loving one another in Allah, for they visit wherever they will.""**"
"18017","With the same chain, Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned three sheltered in Allah's shade when no shade but His exists. > **""Three are in the shade of Allah on the Day of Resurrection, when there is no shade but His: a man who, wherever he turns, knows Allah is with him; a man whom a woman invites to herself and he leaves her from fear of Allah; and a man who loves for the majesty of Allah.""**"
"18018","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described enduring affection. > **""Affection is what is inherited among the people of Islām.""**"
"18019","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say companionship follows love. > **""The servant is with whom he loves.""**"
"18020","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said companionship follows love. > **""A man is with whom he loves.""**"
"18021","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed three truths concerning share in Islām, allegiance, and gathering with the beloved. > **""Three are truth: Allah does not make one with a share in Islām like one with no share; no servant takes Allah as guardian and Allah entrusts him to another; and no man loves a people except he is gathered with them.""**"
"18022","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that an aged bedouin asked the Prophet ﷺ when the Hour is, was asked what he prepared, answered love of Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, was told he is with whom he loves, then rose and urinated in the mosque, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ ordered him left alone in hope he is among Paradise, and water was poured over it. > **""What have you prepared for it? You are with whom you love. Leave him, perhaps he is among the people of Paradise.""**"
"18023","ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه also narrated that a bedouin thrice swore love for the Prophet ﷺ, was asked who swears thus, answered himself, and was told to proceed with recompense according to earning and expectation. > **""Proceed, for you are with whom you love. Upon you is what you earned, and for you is what you expected.""**"
"18024","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about the Hour, was asked what he prepared, answered love of Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, and was told he is with whom he loves. > **""What have you prepared for it? Love of Allah and His Messenger. Then you are with whom you love.""**"
"18025","Abū Sarīḥah رضي الله عنه narrated that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the Hour, confessed little preparation except love of Allah and His Messenger, and was told he is with whom he loves. > **""I have prepared little for it, except that I love Allah and His Messenger. Then you are with whom you love.""**"
"18026","ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Khaṭmī رضي الله عنه narrated that a bedouin asked the Prophet ﷺ when the Hour is, received no answer until prayer, was found in a house of the Anṣār, questioned why he asked, confessed little prayer fasting or charity except love of Allah and His Messenger, and was told he is with whom he loves. > **""Why did you ask about the Hour? What have you prepared for it? Then you are with whom you love.""**"
"18027","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ṣafwān ibn Qudāmah narrated that his father Ṣafwān رضي الله عنه emigrated to the Prophet ﷺ, pledged upon Islām, extended his hand which the Prophet ﷺ stroked, declared love, and was told a man is with whom he loves. > **""I love you, O Messenger of Allah. A man is with whom he loves.""**"
"18028","ʿUrwah ibn Muḍarris al-Ṭāʾī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said companionship follows love. > **""A man is with whom he loves.""**"
"18029","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said companionship follows love. > **""A man is with whom he loves.""**"
"18030","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say a man receives what he expects, bears what he earns, accompanies whom he loves, and whoever dies upon the middle of the path belongs to its people. > **""A man receives what he expects, bears what he earns, a man is with whom he loves, and whoever dies upon the middle of the path is among its people.""**"
"18031","Abū Qirṣāfah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said love determines gathering. > **""Whoever loves a people, Allah gathers him among their company.""**"
"18032","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما said that whoever loves them for this world is loved alike by righteous and wicked, but whoever loves them for Allah will be with them on the Day of Resurrection like two fingers, indicating with forefinger and middle. Whoever loves us for this world, the companion of this world is loved by righteous and wicked alike; whoever loves us for Allah, we and he on the Day of Resurrection will be like these two. > **""Whoever loves us for Allah, we and he on the Day of Resurrection will be like these two.""**"
"18033","Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated that Abū Sālim al-Jayshānī came to Abū Umayyah's house reporting he heard Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command one loving his companion to visit him at home and inform him, declaring he came for that purpose. > **""When one of you loves his companion, let him come to him at his house and inform him that he loves him for Allah.""**"
"18034","ʿAbdullāh ibn Sarjis رضي الله عنه narrated that he told the Prophet ﷺ he loves Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه, was asked whether he informed him, ordered to inform him, did so and received reciprocal supplication, then reported back and was told mention carries reward. > **""Have you informed him of that? Inform him. Indeed that carries reward for whoever mentions it.""**"
"18035","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that while seated with the Prophet ﷺ a man greeted and departed, he declared love for him, was asked whether he informed him, ordered to inform his brother, then greeted him, seized his shoulder, swore love for Allah, received likewise, and explained he acted only by the Prophet's ﷺ command. > **""Have you informed him? Inform your brother of that. By Allah, I love you in Allah.""**"
"18036","Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb رضي الله عنه narrated that while seated with the Prophet ﷺ a passerby was declared beloved for Allah, and the speaker was asked whether he informed him, then commanded to rise and inform him. > **""Have you informed him of that? Rise and inform him.""**"
"18037","Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ command expression of affection to strengthen it. > **""Express affection to one you befriend, for it is more firmly established.""**"
"18038","Al-Ḥārith narrated that ʿAlī رضي الله عنه questioned al-Ḥasan رضي الله عنه concerning manliness: uprightness is repelling wrong through right; nobility is benefiting kin, bearing liabilities, agreeing with brothers, guarding neighbours; manliness is chastity and tending wealth; precision is regard for little and withholding contemptible; vileness is sparing oneself and offering one's family; generosity is spending in hardship and ease; avarice is deeming spent wealth lost; brotherhood is consolation in hardship and ease; cowardice is boldness toward friends and retreat from enemies; spoil is desire for taqwā and detachment, the cool spoil; forbearance is swallowing anger and mastering self; wealth is contentment with apportionment though little, for wealth is soul's wealth; poverty is soul's greed in everything; strength is might and contesting the mighty; humiliation is panic at truth; incapacity is frivolity and spitting when addressed; courage is meeting peers; burden is speech in what concerns not; glory is giving under liability and pardoning offence; intellect is guarding what the heart is entrusted; folly is abandoning one's leader and raising speech above him; good praise is doing beautiful and leaving ugly; prudence is prolonged deliberation and gentleness with rulers; foolishness is following the base and accompanying the errant; heedlessness is abandoning the mosque and obeying the corrupter; deprivation is abandoning one's offered share; the fool is foolish in wealth and negligent in honour. Then ʿAlī said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say no poverty is severer than ignorance, no wealth more profitable than intellect, no solitude drearier than conceit, no support more availing than consultation, no intellect like planning, no lineage like good character, no scrupulousness like restraint, no worship like reflection, no faith like modesty and patience, while the bane of speech is lying, of knowledge forgetting, of forbearance folly, of worship lassitude, of wit insolence, of courage transgression, of generosity reminding, of beauty vanity, of lineage boasting. Then he counselled his son never to belittle any man seen, deeming the better as father, the equal as brother, the younger as son. > **""No poverty is severer than ignorance, no wealth more profitable than intellect, no solitude drearier than conceit, no support more availing than consultation, no intellect like planning, no lineage like good character, no scrupulousness like restraint, no worship like reflection, and no faith like modesty and patience.""**"
"18039","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say whoever lacks one of three should count no deed: taqwā restraining from prohibitions, forbearance repelling the fool, or character for living among people. > **""Whoever lacks one of three should count no deed: taqwā restraining him from prohibitions, forbearance with which he repels the fool, or character with which he lives among people.""**"
"18040","Maʿqil ibn Yasār رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed his Lord's address commanding devotion to worship with fullness of heart and hands, warning distance brings poverty of heart and occupation of hands. > **""Your Lord says: Son of Ādam, devote yourself to My worship and I fill your heart with richness and your hands with provision. Son of Ādam, do not distance yourself from Me, lest I fill your heart with poverty and your hands with occupation.""**"
"18041","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed people around the pulpit commanding true modesty, and when a man claimed modesty, explained it as keeping the term before eyes nightly, guarding belly and head with what they contain, remembering death and decay, and abandoning adornment. > **""O people, be modest before Allah with true modesty. Whoever among you is modest should not spend a night except with his term before his eyes; let him guard the belly and what it holds, the head and what it contains; let him remember death and decay; and let him abandon the adornment of this world.""**"
"18042","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUmayr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded true modesty through guarding head and belly and remembering death and decay, promising the Garden of Refuge. > **""Be modest before Allah with true modesty. Guard the head and what it holds, the belly and what it contains, remember death and decay. Whoever does that, his reward is the Garden of Refuge.""**"
"18043","Umm al-Walīd bint ʿUmar رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked out one evening asking people whether they feel no shame in gathering uneaten, building uninhabited, and hoping unattained. > **""O people, do you feel no shame? You gather what you do not eat, build what you do not inhabit, and hope for what you do not attain. Do you feel no shame at that?""**"
"18044","Saʿīd ibn Yazīd al-Azdī رضي الله عنه narrated that he asked the Prophet ﷺ for counsel and was counselled to modesty before Allah as before a righteous man of his people. > **""I counsel you to be modest before Allah as you are modest before a righteous man of your people.""**"
"18045","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه narrated that one of them would refrain from a thing, though he and it were under one garment, fearing a Qurʾānic ruling descend concerning it. One of us would refrain from a thing, though he and it were in one garment, fearing that something of the Qurʾān descend concerning it."
"18046","Abū al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ reported from the earliest prophetic speech concerning shamelessness. > **""It was said: Among what people retained from the earliest prophetic speech is: If you feel no shame, do as you wish.""**"
"18047","Shuwayfiʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever feels no shame at what he says or is said lacks sound conduct and birth purity. > **""Whoever feels no shame at what he says or what is said is not upon sound conduct; his mother bore him without purity.""**"
"18048","Sakhbarah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described one given who thanks, tried who endures, wronging who seeks forgiveness, wronged who pardons, then fell silent, and when asked what is his, declared security and guidance. > **""Whoever is given and thanks, is tried and endures, wrongs and seeks forgiveness, is wronged and pardons. Those have security, and they are guided.""**"
"18049","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUmayr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said patience and expectation emancipate necks and admit their people to Paradise without reckoning. > **""Patience and expectation are emancipation of necks, and Allah admits their people to Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"18050","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned four attained only through wonder: patience which is the beginning of worship, humility, remembrance of Allah, and scarcity. > **""Four are attained only through wonder: patience, which is the beginning of worship; humility; remembrance of Allah; and scarcity.""**"
"18051","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ conditioned detachment upon humility. > **""You are not detached until you are humble.""**"
"18052","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that a time passed when none deemed himself more deserving of dīnār and dirham than his Muslim brother, then a time came when wealth grew dearer than the brother, and the rest of the report was mentioned. A time passed over us when none among us deemed himself more deserving of the dīnār and dirham than his Muslim brother. A time has passed over us when the dīnār and dirham are dearer to us than our Muslim brother."
"18053","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened Allah's shielding of the beloved servant from this world to shielding the sick from water. > **""When Allah loves a servant He shields him from this world as one of you shields his sick from water.""**"
"18054","ʿUqbah ibn Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened Allah's shielding of the beloved from this world to shielding the sick from water for healing. > **""When Allah loves a servant He shields him from this world as one of you shields his sick from water that he may heal.""**"
"18055","Sāʿidah ibn Saʿd ibn Ḥudhayfah narrated that Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said no day delighted his eyes and soul more than returning home to find no food great or small, and reported hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say Allah shields the believer from this world more jealously than a patient's family from food, and tends him through trial more than a father his child in good. > **""Allah shields the believer from this world more jealously than a patient's family shields him from food, and Allah tends the believer through trial more than a father tends his child in good.""**"
"18056","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated that whoever believes him and knows his truth receive little wealth and offspring and hastened seizure, and whoever disbelieves though knowing receive abundance and long life. > **""O Allah, whoever believes in me, confirms me, and knows that what I brought is truth from You, lessen his wealth and offspring and hasten his seizure. O Allah, whoever disbelieves in me, denies me, though knowing that what I brought is truth from You, increase his wealth and offspring and lengthen his life.""**"
"18057","Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated that believers witnessing his messengership receive love of meeting Allah, ease of decree, and little world, and disbelievers the opposite with abundance. > **""O Allah, whoever believes in You and testifies I am Your Messenger, endear meeting You to him, ease Your decree for him, and lessen this world for him. Whoever disbelieves in You and does not testify I am Your Messenger, do not endear meeting You to him, do not ease Your decree for him, and increase this world for him.""**"
"18058","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said detachment rests heart and body. > **""Detachment in this world rests the heart and body.""**"
"18059","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the righteous are adorned with nothing like detachment. > **""The righteous are not adorned in this world with anything like detachment in this world.""**"
"18060","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded nearness to the detached for they receive wisdom. > **""When you see one detached in this world, draw near to him, for he receives wisdom.""**"
"18061","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told of a merchant among Banū Isrāʾīl who gave short and excess by turns, deemed commerce void of good, sought better commerce, built a cell, adopted monastic life, and was called Jurayj. > **""Among Banū Isrāʾīl was a merchant who gave short at times and excess at others. He said: There is no good in this commerce; I shall seek commerce better than it. So he built a cell and adopted monastic life therein.""**"
"18062","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ explained detachment is not prohibiting lawful nor wasting wealth, but trusting what is with Allah over what is in hand and preferring reward of calamity over its continuance. > **""Detachment in this world is not prohibiting the lawful nor wasting wealth. Rather detachment is that you trust what is in Allah's Hands more than what is in your hands, and that amid calamity you desire its reward more than its continuance for you.""**"
"18063","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنه narrated, in apparently marfūʿ form, that the soundness of this Ummah's first is through detachment and certainty, and its ruin through miserliness and hope. > **""The soundness of the first of this Ummah is through detachment and certainty, and its ruin is through miserliness and hope.""**"
"18064","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked what richness is and answered despair of people's possessions. > **""He was asked: What is richness? He said: Despair of what is in people's hands.""**"
"18065","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed a dead ewe cast by its owners and swore this world is lighter to Allah than it to them. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, this world is lighter to Allah than this is to its owners.""**"
"18066","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed a mangy kid expelled by its owners, asked whether they deem it trifling, was answered yes, and declared this world lighter to Allah than it to them. > **""Do you deem this trifling to its owners? This world is lighter to Allah than this is to its owners.""**"
"18067","ʿAbdullāh ibn Rabīʿah al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ travelled, heard a caller to prayer, repeated both testimonies after him, remarked the caller will be found a shepherd or estranged from family, then descended a valley, passed a discarded kid, and asked whether they deem it trifling, declaring this world lighter to Allah than it to them. > **""I testify there is no deity but Allah. I testify Muḥammad is Allah's Messenger. You will find him a shepherd or estranged from his family. Do you deem this trifling to its owners? This world is lighter to Allah than this is to its owners.""**"
"18068","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed a people's dunghill with a dead kid, asked whether its owners need it, was told had they needed it they would not discard it, then swore this world is lighter to Allah than it to them and warned against being ruined by it. > **""By Allah, this world is lighter to Allah than this kid is to its owners, so let me never find it has ruined anyone among you.""**"
"18069","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed a dead ewe and declared this world lighter to Allah than it to its owners. > **""This world is lighter to Allah than this is to its owners.""**"
"18070","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, upon a kid he came to, asked whether they deem it trifling when they cast it, was answered yes, and declared this world lighter to Allah than it to them when they cast it. > **""Do you deem this trifling to its owners when they cast it? This world is lighter to Allah than this is to its owners when they cast it.""**"
"18071","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left his house with companions, took a Madīnan path, passed a courtyard with a dead kid cast therein, stood beholding it, asked whether they deem it trifling when they cast it, was answered yes, and swore this world is lighter to Allah than it to them when they cast it. > **""Do you deem this kid trifling to its owners when they cast it? By Allah, this world is lighter to Allah than this kid is to its owners when they cast it.""**"
"18072","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swear this world is lighter to Allah than this kid to its owners, and had it equalled a mustard grain He would have given it only to His allies and beloved creation. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, this world is lighter to Allah than this kid is to its owners. Had this world equalled with Allah a mustard grain, He would have given it only to His allies and beloved among His creation.""**"
"18073","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said had this world equalled a gnat's wing with Allah, no disbeliever would be given aught thereof. > **""Had this world equalled with Allah a gnat's wing, He would not have given a disbeliever aught thereof.""**"
"18074","Shaddād ibn Aws al-Fihrī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swear this world from first to last beside the Hereafter is like one placing a finger in the sea and observing what returns. > **""By Allah, this world from its first to its last beside the Hereafter is only like one of you placing his finger in the sea, so let him observe what it returns with.""**"
"18075","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said human food, however seasoned and salted, is made a parable of this world, so observe what it becomes. > **""The food of the son of Ādam is made a parable of this world, however he seasons and salts it, so observe what it becomes.""**"
"18076","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Sufyān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him what his food is, was told meat and milk, asked what it becomes, was told what he knows, then declared Allah made human excretion a parable of this world. > **""O Ḍaḥḥāk, what is your food? Then what does it become? Allah has made what exits from the son of Ādam a parable of this world.""**"
"18077","Salmān رضي الله عنه narrated that people came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, were asked whether they eat, drink, refine, and cool, answered yes, and were told the outcome of both is like this world: one rises behind his house holding his nose from its stench. > **""Have you food? Have you drink? Do you refine it? Do you cool it? Their outcome is like the outcome of this world: one of you rises behind his house holding his nose from its stench.""**"
"18078","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said this world is the abode of the homeless and hoarded by the mindless. > **""This world is the abode of one with no abode, and for it hoards one with no mind.""**"
"18079","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said this world is the believer's prison and drought, and leaving it is leaving both. > **""This world is the prison of the believer and his drought. When he leaves this world he leaves prison and drought.""**"
"18080","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said this world is the believer's prison and the disbeliever's paradise. > **""This world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever.""**"
"18081","Salmān رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say this world is the believer's prison and the disbeliever's paradise. > **""This world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever.""**"
"18082","Qatādah ibn Nuʿmān ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Jibrīl عليه السلام descended in fairest form conveying peace and Allah's revelation to this world to embitter, distress, straiten, and harden toward His allies that they love meeting Him, and to ease, widen, and sweeten toward His enemies that they hate meeting Him, for He made it a prison for allies and paradise for enemies. > **""Peace conveys peace to you, O Muḥammad, saying: I revealed to this world to embitter, distress, straiten, and harden toward My allies that they love meeting Me, and to widen, ease, and sweeten toward My enemies that they hate meeting Me. I made it a prison for My allies and a paradise for My enemies.""**"
"18083","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever mornings sound in body, secure in self, with daily sustenance has the world gathered for him, then addressed son of Ādam concerning sufficiency of a bowl, garment, house, mount, and reckoning for bread, water, and surplus garment. > **""Whoever mornings sound in his body, secure in his self, with his day's sustenance, it is as though the world were gathered for him. O son of Ādam, a bowl suffices you with what repels your hunger and covers your nakedness; if a house shelters you, that suffices; if a mount carries you, excellent; but a morsel of bread, water of the jar, and what exceeds the waist-wrapper is reckoning upon you.""**"
"18084","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said security and well-being are two blessings in which many are cheated. > **""Security and well-being are two blessings in which many people are cheated.""**"
"18085","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said whoever mornings sound in body, secure in self, with daily sustenance has the world gathered for him. > **""Whoever mornings sound in his body, secure in his self, with his day's sustenance, it is as though the world were gathered for him.""**"
"18086","ʿUmar رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed son of Ādam possessing sufficiency yet seeking transgression, content with neither little nor sated with much, and declared if mornings secure, sound, with daily sustenance, then away with this world. > **""Son of Ādam, with you is what suffices you, yet you seek what makes you transgress. Neither with little are you content, nor with much are you sated. Son of Ādam, when you morning secure in yourself, sound in your body, with your day's sustenance, then away with this world.""**"
"18087","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the Giver of well-being as the end of hopes. > **""To You end hopes, O Giver of well-being.""**"
"18088","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said many are cheated in health and leisure. > **""Two blessings in which many people are cheated: health and leisure.""**"
"18089","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what perfects righteousness and was told to act in secret as in open. > **""What is the perfection of righteousness? That you act in secret as you act openly.""**"
"18090","Abū ʿĀmir al-Sakūnī رضي الله عنه narrated that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what perfects righteousness and was told to act in secret as in open. > **""What is the perfection of righteousness? That you act in secret as you act openly.""**"
"18091","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said that he performs a deed which becomes known to others and this pleases him. The Prophet ﷺ told him that he receives two rewards, the reward of secrecy and the reward of openness. > **""You shall have two rewards, the reward of secrecy and the reward of openness.""**"
"18092","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say that when they pass by a land whose people have perished, they should quicken their pace. > **""When you pass by a land whose people have perished, then hasten your pace.""**"
"18093","Samurah ibn Jundab رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came to Ḥijr of Thamūd, he forbade them from drinking from a well near the settlement and from giving its water to their animals, and he forbade them from entering their houses. He also informed them that the young camel ascended a hillock called Kabābah and that the trace of the young camel was clear on its front. > **""Do not drink from it nor give drink from it, and do not enter their houses.""**"
"18094","Sabrah رضي الله عنه reported that when the Prophet ﷺ halted at Al-Ḥijr, he asked his Companions who had kneaded dough with its water, meaning their well, and commanded that whatever was made with it be thrown away. So the one with dough threw away his dough and the one with ḥays threw away his ḥays. > **""Whoever has kneaded anything with this water, let him throw it away.""**"
"18095","It is reported in a narration that the Prophet ﷺ addressed his Companions when he departed from Al-Ḥijr, commanding them concerning the ruins and their water. > **""The Prophet ﷺ spoke to his Companions when he set out from Al-Ḥijr.""**"
"18096","Abū Kabshah رضي الله عنه reported that during the expedition of Tabūk the people hastened toward Al-Ḥijr to enter it, so it was announced that prayer is gathering. He came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ while he was holding his camel and saying why they would enter upon a people with whom Allāh is angry. A man called out in wonder, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of something more wondrous, that their Prophet informs them of what was before them and what shall be after them, so be upright and correct, for Allāh does not care for punishing them. > **""Upon what are you entering, upon a people with whom Allāh is angry? Your Prophet informs you of what was before you and what shall be after you, so be upright and correct.""**"
"18097","ʿAmr ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ whether he should release his mount and trust in Allāh. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told him rather to tie it and then trust. > **""Rather tie it and trust.""**"
"18098","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that seeking the lawful is an obligation after the obligation. > **""Seeking the lawful is an obligation after the obligation.""**"
"18099","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that seeking the lawful is incumbent upon every Muslim. > **""Seeking the lawful is incumbent upon every Muslim.""**"
"18100","Umm ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنها, the sister of Shaddād ibn Aws, reported that she sent a cup of milk to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at his breaking of the fast while he was fasting on a long and intensely hot day. He sent back her messenger asking whence the milk came, and she said from her ewe. He sent back asking whence the ewe came, and she said she had bought it with her own wealth, so he accepted it. The next day she came and asked why he had questioned her messenger though she sent it out of pity for the long hot day, and he told her that thus were the messengers commanded, to eat only what is pure and to do only what is righteous. > **""Thus were the messengers commanded, to eat only what is pure and to do only what is righteous.""**"
"18101","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the verse, O people, eat of what is in the earth lawful and pure, was recited before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه stood and asked him to supplicate that Allāh make him one whose supplication is answered. The Prophet ﷺ told him to make his food pure and he would be one whose supplication is answered, and swore by Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad that a servant casts an unlawful morsel into his belly and no deed is accepted from him for forty days, and whichever servant whose flesh grows from illicit gain, the Fire is worthier of him. > **""O Saʿd, make your food pure and you shall be one whose supplication is answered. The servant who casts an unlawful morsel into his belly shall have no deed accepted for forty days, and whatever flesh grows from illicit gain, the Fire is worthier of it.""**"
"18102","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that they were sitting with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when a man from the people of Al-ʿĀliyah came and asked him to inform him of the hardest and the softest thing in this religion. He said its softest is the testimony that there is no god but Allāh and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger, and its hardest, O brother of Al-ʿĀliyah, is trust, for there is no religion for one without trust, nor prayer nor zakāh for him. He added that whoever obtains wealth unlawfully and wears a shirt with it, his prayer is not accepted until he removes that shirt, for Allāh the Blessed and Exalted is too Generous and Sublime to accept the deed or prayer of a man wearing unlawful clothing. > **""Its softest is the testimony that there is no god but Allāh and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger, and its hardest is trust. Allāh is too Generous to accept the deed of a man wearing an unlawful garment.""**"
"18103","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when one goes out for ḥajj with pure provision and puts his foot in the stirrup and calls, Labbayk Allāhumma Labbayk, a caller from heaven calls to him, Labbayk and Saʿdayk, your provision is lawful and your mount is lawful and your ḥajj is righteous without sin. But when he goes out with foul provision and puts his foot in the stirrup and calls Labbayk, a caller from heaven calls, No Labbayk nor Saʿdayk, your provision is unlawful and your spending is unlawful and your ḥajj is not righteous. > **""Your provision is lawful, your mount is lawful, and your ḥajj is righteous without sin. Your provision is unlawful, your spending is unlawful, and your ḥajj is not righteous.""**"
"18104","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh the Blessed and Exalted gives this world to whom He loves and whom He does not love, but He gives religion only to whom He loves. By Him in Whose Hand is his soul, no servant submits until his heart submits, and no servant believes until his neighbour is safe from his harms. They asked what his harms are, and he said his oppression and injustice. No servant earns unlawful wealth and gives charity with it but it is accepted, nor spends it but it is blessed, nor leaves it behind him except it becomes his provision to the Fire. Allāh does not efface evil with evil but effaces evil with good, and the foul does not efface the foul. Whoever earns wealth unlawfully and places it unlawfully, that is the incurable disease, and whoever earns wealth and places it rightly, his likeness is like rain that descends. Then the narrator mentioned a word which he forgot. > **""Allāh gives this world to whom He loves and whom He does not love, but He gives religion only to whom He loves. Allāh does not efface evil with evil, but effaces evil with good.""**"
"18105","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that whoever buys a garment for ten dirhams in which one dirham is unlawful, Allāh the Mighty and Majestic shall not accept prayer from him while he wears it. He then put his two fingers into his ears and said may they be deaf if he had not heard the Prophet ﷺ say it. > **""Whoever buys a garment for ten dirhams in which one dirham is unlawful, Allāh shall not accept prayer from him while he wears it.""**"
"18106","Abū al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever earns wealth unlawfully and then frees a slave with it or joins kinship with it, that shall be a burden upon him. > **""Whoever earns wealth unlawfully, then frees a slave with it or joins kinship with it, that shall be a burden.""**"
"18107","Maymūnah bint Saʿd رضي الله عنها reported that she asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to give them a ruling about theft. He said that whoever eats it while knowing it is stolen has shared in the sin of its theft. > **""Whoever eats it while knowing it is stolen has shared in the sin of its theft.""**"
"18108","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that for one of them to take his rope and go to the mountain and gather firewood, then bring it carried upon his back and sell it and eat therefrom, is better for him than to ask people. And for him to take dust and place it in his mouth is better for him than to place in his mouth what Allāh has forbidden him. > **""For one of you to gather firewood and eat therefrom is better than to ask people, and for him to place dust in his mouth is better than to place in it what Allāh has forbidden.""**"
"18109","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that no body nourished by the unlawful shall enter Paradise. > **""No body nourished by the unlawful shall enter Paradise.""**"
"18110","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no flesh grown from illicit gain shall enter Paradise, and the Fire is worthier of it. > **""No flesh grown from illicit gain shall enter Paradise, the Fire is worthier of it.""**"
"18111","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that no flesh grown from illicit gain shall enter Paradise. > **""No flesh grown from illicit gain shall enter Paradise.""**"
"18112","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say to place between themselves and the unlawful a veil of the lawful, and then he narrated the ḥadīth. > **""Place between yourselves and the unlawful a veil of the lawful.""**"
"18113","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Whoever avoids them, they become a protection for his religion, and whoever falls into them shall soon fall into major sins, like one who grazes around a sanctuary and soon enters it, for every king has a sanctuary. > **""The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Whoever avoids them protects his religion, and whoever falls into them shall soon fall into major sins.""**"
"18114","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Whoever is ensnared by them is liable to sin, and whoever avoids them is more preserving of his religion, like one grazing beside a sanctuary who is about to fall into it. Every king has a sanctuary, and the sanctuary of Allāh is what He has forbidden. > **""The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters. Every king has a sanctuary, and the sanctuary of Allāh is what He has forbidden.""**"
"18115","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that he presented himself to the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque of Al-Khayf, and his Companions told him to move away from the face of the Prophet ﷺ, but the Prophet ﷺ said to leave him for he had come to ask. He drew near and asked him by his father and mother to give them a ruling to hold to after him. He told him to let his own soul give him ruling. When he asked how, he told him to leave what causes doubt for what does not cause doubt, even if the muftīs give fatwā. When he asked how to know, he told him to place his hand upon his heart, for the heart finds rest in the lawful and does not find rest in the unlawful, and the Godfearing Muslim leaves the minor out of fear of falling into the major. He asked about tribalism, greed, piety, faith, Islām, and the best jihād, and he answered that tribalism is helping one's people in injustice, the greedy is one who seeks gain unlawfully, the pious is one who stops at doubt, the believer is one whom people trust with their wealth and blood, the Muslim is one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe, and the best jihād is a just word before a tyrannical ruler. > **""Leave what causes you doubt for what does not cause you doubt. The heart finds rest in the lawful and does not find rest in the unlawful. The best jihād is a just word before a tyrannical ruler.""**"
"18116","Wāthilah رضي الله عنه reported that he said O Prophet of Allāh, inform me. He offered to inform him of what he came to ask or to let him ask, and he chose to be informed as it is more pleasing to his soul. He told him he had come to ask about certainty and doubt, which he confirmed. He told him certainty is what settles in the breast and the heart is tranquil with it, even if the muftīs give fatwā, and to leave what causes doubt for what does not, for good is tranquillity and doubt is suspicion, and when in doubt, leave it. Then he narrated similarly. > **""Certainty is what settles in the breast and the heart is tranquil with it. Leave what causes you doubt for what does not cause you doubt, for good is tranquillity and doubt is suspicion.""**"
"18117","Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad al-Asadī رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ intending to ask about every matter of righteousness and sin. He found him among a group of Muslims and stepped over them to draw near, and some rebuked him to stay back. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to leave Wābiṣah and to draw near, until he stood before him. He asked whether he should ask or be informed, and he chose to be informed. He told him he had come to ask about righteousness and sin, which he confirmed. Then he joined his fingers and tapped his breast with them, saying righteousness is what the soul and heart are tranquil with, and sin is what pricks the soul and wavers, even if people give fatwā after fatwā. > **""Righteousness is what the soul and the heart are tranquil with, and sin is what pricks the soul and wavers, even if people give you fatwā.""**"
"18118","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that a man asked what is sin, O Messenger of Allāh. He said what pricks in your breast, so leave it. He asked what is faith, and he said whoever is displeased by his evil deed and pleased by his good deed, he is a believer. > **""What pricks in your breast, leave it. Whoever is displeased by his evil deed and pleased by his good deed, he is a believer.""**"
"18119","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to leave what causes doubt for what does not cause doubt. > **""Leave what causes you doubt for what does not cause you doubt.""**"
"18120","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه reported that one day he entered upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when a pot was boiling with meat. A piece of fat pleased him, so he took it and swallowed it, and suffered from it for a year. When he mentioned it to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he said it had contained the portion of seven people. Then he wiped his belly and he cast it out green, and by Him Who sent him with the truth, he suffered no complaint until this hour. > **""It contained the portion of seven people.""**"
"18121","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ swear by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that the likeness of the believer is like the bee, which eats what is pure and produces what is pure, and when it alights it neither breaks nor spoils. > **""The likeness of the believer is like the bee, which eats what is pure and produces what is pure, and when it alights it neither breaks nor spoils.""**"
"18122","Abū Razīn al-ʿUqaylī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the likeness of the believer is like the bee, which eats only what is pure and produces only what is pure. > **""The likeness of the believer is like the bee, which eats only what is pure and produces only what is pure.""**"
"18123","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that there are four traits, when they are in a person then whatever he misses of this world does not harm him: guarding trust, truthful speech, good character, and chastity in food. > **""Guarding trust, truthful speech, good character, and chastity in food.""**"
"18124","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that there are three traits, whoever has them renders reward due and completes faith: character by which he lives among people, piety which restrains him from the prohibitions of Allāh, and forbearance by which he repels the ignorance of the ignorant. > **""Character by which he lives among people, piety which restrains him from the prohibitions of Allāh, and forbearance by which he repels the ignorance of the ignorant.""**"
"18125","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh the Exalted intimately addressed Mūsā عليه السلام with one hundred and forty thousand words in three days, all of them counsels. When Mūsā heard the speech of men he found them distasteful because of what had fallen upon his hearing of the speech of the Lord. Among what He confided was, O Mūsā, those who strive for Me have not striven with the like of renunciation in this world, nor have seekers drawn near with the like of piety from what I forbade them, nor have worshippers worshipped Me with the like of weeping from fear of Me. Mūsā asked, O God of all creation and Master of the Day of Judgement and Possessor of Majesty and Honour, what have You prepared for them and what is their recompense. He said, O Mūsā, as for the renunciants in this world, I have permitted them My Paradise to dwell wherever they wish. As for the pious from what I forbade them, there is no servant who meets Me on the Day of Resurrection but I examine and search what was in his hands, except the pious, for I honour them and admit them to Paradise without reckoning. As for those who weep from fear of Me, theirs is the highest companionship in which none share. > **""Those who strive have not striven with the like of renunciation in this world, nor have seekers drawn near with the like of piety from what I forbade, nor have worshippers worshipped Me with the like of weeping from fear of Me.""**"
"18126","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that nothing of this world nor what is in it pleased the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ except piety. > **""Nothing of this world pleased the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ except piety.""**"
"18127","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said, O Abā Hurayrah, be pleased with what is apportioned for you and you shall be rich, be pious and you shall be the most worshipful of people, love for people what you love for yourself and you shall be a believer, do good to your neighbour and you shall be a Muslim, and beware of much laughter, for it deadens the heart. Loud guffaw is from Satan and smiling is from Allāh the Mighty and Majestic. > **""Be pleased with what is apportioned for you and you shall be rich, be pious and you shall be the most worshipful of people, and beware of much laughter, for it deadens the heart.""**"
"18128","Abū Qatādah and Abū al-Dahmāʾ رضي الله عنهما reported that they came to a man of the desert and asked whether he had heard anything from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. He said yes, he heard him say that a person never leaves a thing for Allāh the Mighty and Majestic but Allāh replaces it for him with what is better for him than it. > **""You never leave a thing for Allāh but Allāh replaces it for you with what is better for you than it.""**"
"18129","In a narration, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ took his hand and began teaching him of what Allāh the Blessed and Exalted had taught him, saying that a person never leaves a thing out of fear of Allāh the Mighty and Majestic but Allāh gives him better than it. > **""You never leave a thing out of fear of Allāh but Allāh gives you better than it.""**"
"18130","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever has power over a worldly temptation and fulfils it faithfully, though had he wished he would not have fulfilled it, Allāh the Mighty and Majestic shall marry him to the maidens of Paradise wherever he wishes. > **""Whoever has power over worldly temptation and fulfils it faithfully, Allāh shall marry him to the maidens of Paradise wherever he wishes.""**"
"18131","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that it suffices a man as evil that he be pointed at with fingers in religion or world, except whom Allāh protects. > **""It suffices a man as evil that he be pointed at with fingers in religion or world, except whom Allāh protects.""**"
"18132","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that it suffices a man as sin that he be pointed at with fingers. It was asked, O Messenger of Allāh, even if it be for good. He said even if it be for good, it is evil for him, except whom Allāh has mercy upon, and if it be for evil, then it is evil for him. > **""It suffices a man as sin that he be pointed at with fingers, even if it be for good, except whom Allāh has mercy upon.""**"
"18133","Ibn Muḥayrīz reported that he accompanied Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and asked him to counsel him. He told him to remember three traits by which Allāh shall benefit him: if he can know without being known, let him do so; if he can listen without speaking, let him do so; and if he can sit without others sitting to him, let him do so. > **""If you can know without being known, do so; if you can listen without speaking, do so; and if you can sit without others sitting to you, do so.""**"
"18134","Shutayr ibn Shakl, Zufar, Ṣilah ibn Zufar, and Sulayk ibn Misḥal reported that Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه came out to them while they were conversing and said that they utter speech which in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ they used to count as hypocrisy. > **""You utter speech which in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ we used to count as hypocrisy.""**"
"18135","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that a man in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would utter a word and thereby become a hypocrite, whereas now he hears such from one of them ten times in a single sitting. > **""A man would utter a word in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and thereby become a hypocrite, whereas I hear it ten times in one sitting.""**"
"18136","In a narration, Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said four times, meaning he hears such speech four times from one of them in a day in a sitting. > **""I hear it from one of you four times in a sitting.""**"
"18137","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that a man utters a word by which he falls into the Fire for so many autumns. > **""A man utters a word by which he falls into the Fire for so many autumns.""**"
"18138","Abū Saʿīd, meaning al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه, reported in a raised narration that a man utters a word intending no harm thereby except to make people laugh, yet he falls thereby farther than the sky. > **""A man utters a word only to make people laugh, yet he falls thereby farther than the sky.""**"
"18139","Amatu, daughter of Abū al-Ḥakam al-Ghifārīyah رضي الله عنها, reported that she heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say that a man draws near to Paradise until there remains between him and it only a cubit, then he utters a word and distances himself from it farther than Ṣanʿāʾ. > **""A man draws near to Paradise until there remains but a cubit between him and it, then he utters a word and distances himself farther than Ṣanʿāʾ.""**"
"18140","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that a man utters a word to make his companions laugh, returning to his family with nothing from it, yet he descends thereby farther than from the sky to the earth. > **""A man utters a word to make his companions laugh, yet he descends thereby farther than from the sky to the earth.""**"
"18141","Simāk reported that he asked Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه whether he used to sit with the Prophet ﷺ, and he said yes, and that he was long in silence. > **""He was long in silence.""**"
"18142","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever is pleased to remain safe, let him keep to silence. > **""Whoever is pleased to remain safe, let him keep to silence.""**"
"18143","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever guards his tongue, Allāh shall veil his faults; whoever restrains his anger, Allāh shall withhold His punishment from him; and whoever offers excuse to Allāh, Allāh shall accept his excuse. > **""Whoever guards his tongue, Allāh shall veil his faults; whoever restrains his anger, Allāh shall withhold His punishment; and whoever offers excuse to Allāh, Allāh shall accept his excuse.""**"
"18144","Tamīm ibn Yazīd, freedman of Banū Zamʿah, reported from a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ addressed them one day saying, O people, there are two things, whoever is protected by Allāh from their evil shall enter Paradise. A man of the Anṣār stood and asked whether he would inform them, then he repeated it, until at the third time the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ restrained him, saying he saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ intending to give glad tidings while he prevented him. He replied that he feared people would rely upon it. Then he said there are two things, whoever is protected by Allāh from their evil shall enter Paradise: what is between his jaws and what is between his legs. > **""Two things, whoever is protected by Allāh from their evil shall enter Paradise: what is between his jaws and what is between his legs.""**"
"18145","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever guards what is between his jaws and his private part shall enter Paradise. > **""Whoever guards what is between his jaws and his private part shall enter Paradise.""**"
"18146","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether he should inform of two deeds, whoever does them shall enter Paradise. They said yes, O Messenger of Allāh. He said that a man guards what is between his jaws and what is between his legs. The narrator and his companion withdrew saying by Allāh this is hard, how can a man guard between his jaws and speak only good. They returned and said the two traits are hard and who can control his tongue. He then said there are six, whoever does them shall enter Paradise. They asked what they are, and he said whoever does not associate anything with Allāh, nor commits adultery, nor brings a slander which he fabricates, then he completed the verse, and this was harder than the first. > **""Let a man guard what is between his jaws and what is between his legs. Whoever does not associate with Allāh, nor commits adultery, nor brings a fabricated slander, shall enter Paradise.""**"
"18147","Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported from his father that they used to sit with the Prophet ﷺ while they were young lads, and he never saw a man longer in silence than the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and when his Companions spoke and spoke much, he would smile. > **""I never saw a man longer in silence than the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and when his Companions spoke much, he would smile.""**"
"18148","Al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to inform him of a matter to hold fast to. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to control this, pointing to his tongue. > **""Control this.""**"
"18149","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ went out one day riding his mount with his Companions, none advancing before him. Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه asked Allāh to make their day before his, and asked what deeds to do after him if something happened. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ remained silent. He suggested jihād in the path of Allāh, and he said what a good thing jihād is, but people control more than that. He suggested fasting and charity, and he said what a good thing they are, but people control more than that. Muʿādh mentioned every good he knew, and each time the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said people control more than that. When he asked what that control is, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ pointed to his mouth and said silence except from good. He asked whether they are taken to account for what their tongues utter, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ struck the thigh of Muʿādh and said may his mother bereave him, and what throws people upon their nostrils in Hell except what their tongues utter. Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent from evil, say good and gain, and remain silent from evil and be safe. > **""Silence except from good. What throws people upon their nostrils in Hell except what their tongues utter? Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""**"
"18150","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day and testifies that he is the Messenger of Allāh, let his house suffice him and let him weep over his sin. And whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day and testifies that he is the Messenger of Allāh, let him speak good to gain or remain silent from evil to be safe. > **""Let him speak good to gain, or remain silent from evil to be safe.""**"
"18151","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to let his house suffice him, to weep over remembrance of his sin, and to control his tongue. > **""Let your house suffice you, weep over remembrance of your sin, and control your tongue.""**"
"18152","Thawbān, freedman of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that glad tidings are for whoever controls his tongue and whose house suffices him and who weeps over his sin. > **""Glad tidings for whoever controls his tongue, whose house suffices him, and who weeps over his sin.""**"
"18153","Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه counselled his son Abū ʿUbaydah with three words: O my son, I counsel you with fear of Allāh, let your house suffice you, weep over your sin and restrain your tongue. > **""I counsel you with fear of Allāh, let your house suffice you, weep over your sin and restrain your tongue.""**"
"18154","Abū Wāʾil reported from ʿAbdullāh that he ascended Al-Ṣafā, seized his tongue and said, O tongue, speak good and gain, remain silent from evil and be safe, before you regret, before you regret. Then he said he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say that most errors of the son of Ādam are in his tongue. > **""Most errors of the son of Ādam are in his tongue. O tongue, speak good and gain, remain silent from evil and be safe.""**"
"18155","Aswad ibn Aṣram رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to counsel him. He asked whether he controls his hand, and he replied what would he control if not his hand. He asked whether he controls his tongue, and he replied what would he control if not his tongue. He then said not to stretch the hand except to good, nor to speak with the tongue except what is right. > **""Do not stretch your hand except to good, nor speak with your tongue except what is right.""**"
"18156","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that he asked whether all they speak is written against them. He said may his mother bereave him, and what throws people upon their nostrils in the Fire except the harvests of their tongues. A person remains safe while silent, but when he speaks it is written for him or against him. > **""What throws people upon their nostrils in the Fire except the harvests of their tongues? You remain safe while silent, but when you speak it is written for you or against you.""**"
"18157","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the most fortunate and the most unlucky part of a man is what is between his jaws. > **""The most fortunate and the most unlucky part of a man is what is between his jaws.""**"
"18158","Abū al-Yusr رضي الله عنه reported that a man asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to guide him to a deed that admits him to Paradise. He said to restrain this, pointing to his tongue. The man repeated his question, and he said may his mother bereave him, and what throws people upon their nostrils in the Fire except the harvests of their tongues. > **""Restrain this. What throws people upon their nostrils in the Fire except the harvests of their tongues?""**"
"18159","Muʿādh رضي الله عنه reported in a narration that he asked for a deed that admits him to Paradise. He said to believe in Allāh, to speak good so it is written for him, and not to speak evil lest it be written against him. When he asked whether they are taken to account for what they speak, he narrated similarly about the harvests of tongues. > **""Believe in Allāh, speak good so it is written for you, and do not speak evil lest it be written against you.""**"
"18160","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ while addressing the people say that their place in Paradise is assured, meaning whoever guards what is between his jaws and guards what is between his legs. > **""Your place is in Paradise, whoever guards what is between his jaws and guards what is between his legs.""**"
"18161","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever guards what is between his jaws and his thighs shall enter Paradise. > **""Whoever guards what is between his jaws and his thighs shall enter Paradise.""**"
"18162","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever guarantees him what is between his jaws and his legs, he guarantees him Paradise. > **""Whoever guarantees me what is between his jaws and his legs, I guarantee him Paradise.""**"
"18163","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent. > **""Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""**"
"18164","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbour; whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent; and whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him honour his guest. > **""Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbour, let him speak good or remain silent, and let him honour his guest.""**"
"18165","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ met Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه and asked whether he should guide him to two traits which are light upon the back and heavier in the scale than others. He said yes, O Messenger of Allāh. He said to hold to good character and long silence, for by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, no creatures have acted with the like of them. > **""Hold to good character and long silence, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, no creatures have acted with the like of them.""**"
"18166","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ which deeds are most excellent. He told him prayer at its appointed time, then kindness to parents, then that people be safe from his tongue. Then he fell silent, and had he asked more he would have been given more. > **""Prayer at its appointed time, then kindness to parents, then that people be safe from your tongue.""**"
"18167","Al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to inform him of a matter to hold fast to. He told him to control this, pointing to his tongue. > **""Control this.""**"
"18168","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to those around him of his nation to guarantee him six traits and he would guarantee them Paradise. When asked what they are, he said prayer, zakāh, trust, the private part, the belly, and the tongue. > **""Guarantee me six traits and I shall guarantee you Paradise: prayer, zakāh, trust, the private part, the belly, and the tongue.""**"
"18169","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to accept six from him and he would accept Paradise for them. When asked what they are, he said when one of them speaks let him not lie, when he promises let him not break it, when entrusted let him not betray, and to lower the gaze, restrain the hands, and guard the private parts. > **""When one of you speaks, let him not lie; when he promises, let him not break it; when entrusted, let him not betray; lower your gaze, restrain your hands, and guard your private parts.""**"
"18170","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to guarantee him six and he would guarantee them Paradise: when one of them speaks let him not lie, when he promises let him not break it, when entrusted let him not betray, and to lower the gaze, guard the private parts, and restrain the hands. > **""Guarantee me six and I shall guarantee you Paradise: do not lie when you speak, do not break when you promise, do not betray when entrusted, lower your gaze, guard your private parts, and restrain your hands.""**"
"18171","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asking for counsel. He told him to hold to fear of Allāh for it gathers all good, to hold to jihād in the path of Allāh for it is the monasticism of the Muslims, to hold to remembrance of Allāh and recitation of His Book for it is light on earth and remembrance in heaven, and to guard the tongue except from good, for thereby he overcomes Satan. > **""Hold to fear of Allāh, for it gathers all good; hold to jihād, to remembrance of Allāh and His Book; and guard your tongue except from good, for thereby you overcome Satan.""**"
"18172","Abū Hurayrah رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever laughs much is lightened in his right, whoever jests much loses his majesty, whoever jokes much loses his dignity, whoever drinks water on an empty stomach weakens his strength, whoever speaks much errs much, whoever errs much sins much, and whoever sins much, the Fire is worthier of him. > **""Whoever speaks much errs much, whoever errs much sins much, and whoever sins much, the Fire is worthier of him.""**"
"18173","Al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه told him that whoever laughs much loses awe, whoever jokes is deemed light, whoever speaks much errs much, whoever errs much loses modesty, whoever loses modesty loses piety, and whoever loses piety, his heart dies. > **""Whoever speaks much errs much, whoever errs much loses modesty, whoever loses modesty loses piety, and whoever loses piety, his heart dies.""**"
"18174","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when they see a servant given renunciation in this world and fewness of speech, to draw near him, for he is given wisdom. > **""When you see a servant given renunciation in this world and fewness of speech, draw near him, for he is given wisdom.""**"
"18175","Aslam reported that ʿUmar saw Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه pulling his tongue and asked what he was doing, O successor of the Messenger of Allāh. He replied that it had brought him into perils, for the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said there is nothing of the body but complains of the sharpness of the tongue. > **""There is nothing of the body but complains of the sharpness of the tongue.""**"
"18176","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever speaks much errs much, whoever errs much sins much, and whoever sins much, the Fire is worthier of him. So whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent. > **""Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""**"
"18177","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the servant does not attain the reality of faith until he guards his tongue. > **""The servant does not attain the reality of faith until he guards his tongue.""**"
"18178","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking for counsel, and he told him to leave gossip, excessive questioning, and wasting wealth. > **""Leave gossip, excessive questioning, and wasting wealth.""**"
"18179","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh does not love wasting wealth, nor excessive questioning, nor gossip. > **""Allāh does not love wasting wealth, nor excessive questioning, nor gossip.""**"
"18180","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a man was killed in the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and a wailing woman wept over him saying, O martyr. The Prophet ﷺ said be quiet, what makes her know he is a martyr, for perhaps he spoke of what did not concern him and was miserly with what would not diminish him. > **""What makes you know he is a martyr? Perhaps he spoke of what did not concern him and was miserly with what would not diminish him.""**"
"18181","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that a man of them was martyred on the day of Uḥud and found with a stone tied upon his belly from hunger. His mother wiped dust from his face and said congratulations to you, O my son, Paradise. The Prophet ﷺ asked what made her know, for perhaps he spoke of what did not concern him and withheld what would not harm him. > **""What makes you know? Perhaps he spoke of what did not concern him and withheld what would not harm him.""**"
"18182","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, said that he warns them against excess speech, and it suffices one of them to convey his need. > **""I warn you against excess speech; it suffices one of you to convey his need.""**"
"18183","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that the people with most sins on the Day of Resurrection are those most indulgent in falsehood. > **""The people with most sins on the Day of Resurrection are those most indulgent in falsehood.""**"
"18184","Ibn Masʿūd رضi الله عنه swore by Him besides Whom there is no god that there is nothing upon the face of the earth more in need of long imprisonment than the tongue. > **""There is nothing upon the earth more in need of long imprisonment than the tongue.""**"
"18185","Umm ʿAṭiyyah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to command them to guard their private parts and their tongues, and said that these two bring them in with no bringing out. > **""They bring you in with no bringing out.""**"
"18186","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say to beware of difficult speech. > **""Beware of difficult speech.""**"
"18187","ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī رضي الله عنه reported that he asked whether to release his mount and trust. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told him rather to tie it and trust. > **""Rather tie it and trust.""**"
"18188","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that three birds were gifted to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so he fed his servant one bird. The next day she brought it to him, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether he had not forbidden her to store anything for tomorrow, for Allāh brings the provision of every morrow. > **""Did I not forbid you to store anything for tomorrow? For Allāh brings the provision of every morrow.""**"
"18189","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever cuts himself off to Allāh, Allāh suffices him every need and provides him whence he does not reckon, and whoever cuts himself off to this world, Allāh entrusts him to it. > **""Whoever cuts himself off to Allāh, Allāh suffices him every need and provides him whence he does not reckon; and whoever cuts himself off to this world, Allāh entrusts him to it.""**"
"18190","Umm Maysarah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of the best of people. They said yes, so he gestured toward the east and said a man holding the rein of his horse in the path of Allāh waiting to raid or be raided. Then he asked whether to inform of the next best, and gesturing toward the Ḥijāz said a man among his flock who establishes prayer, gives zakāh, knows the right of Allāh in his wealth, and has withdrawn from people. > **""The best of people is a man holding the rein of his horse in the path of Allāh. Then a man among his flock who establishes prayer, gives zakāh, and has withdrawn from people.""**"
"18191","ʿAdasah al-Ṭāʾī reported that he was at Sharāf when ʿAbdullāh came and his family sent him with things, and their lads brought game birds from four nights journey. When he brought them, ʿAbdullāh asked whence he brought the birds, and he told him. ʿAbdullāh then wished he were where it was hunted, speaking to none and none speaking to him, until he meets Allāh the Mighty and Majestic. > **""Would that I were where it was hunted, speaking to none and none speaking to me, until I meet Allāh.""**"
"18192","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that he passed by Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه standing at his door gesturing as though speaking to himself. When asked, Muʿādh said the enemy of Allāh wishes to divert him from what he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say: to strive throughout life to remain in the house and not go out to gatherings. He also heard him say that whoever goes out in the path of Allāh is guaranteed by Allāh, whoever visits the sick is guaranteed by Allāh, whoever goes to the mosque morning or evening is guaranteed by Allāh, whoever enters to a ruler to aid him is guaranteed by Allāh, and whoever sits in his house backbiting none is guaranteed by Allāh. So the enemy of Allāh wishes to draw him from his house to the gathering. > **""Strive throughout your life to remain in your house. Whoever sits in his house backbiting none is guaranteed by Allāh.""**"
"18193","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that among the nations before them was a man called Mawraq who was devoted in worship. While standing in prayer he thought of women and desired them until he broke his prayer. In anger he took his bow, cut its string, tied it to his testicles and pulled them to his heel, then bound his legs and tore them away. He took his two garments and sandals and came to a land with neither human nor beast, built a shelter, and stood praying. Each morning the earth split and one emerged with a vessel of food which he ate to satiety, then returned with a vessel of drink which he drank to quenching, then entered and the earth closed, and likewise in the evening. People passed nearby, and two men came at nightfall and asked him their way, which he indicated by hand. One wondered what keeps a man in a land with neither human nor beast, so they returned and pressed him. He told them he would inform them on condition that whoever conceals him Allāh honours in this world and the Hereafter, and whoever exposes him Allāh humiliates in this world and the Hereafter. They agreed and stayed. The next morning the provision came doubled, and they ate and drank to satiety, stayed till evening and till morning, then departed mounted. One kept to the king's door until he became his intimate, the other returned to trade. That king crucified whoever was known to lie. One night among his companions one told the marvel he had seen. The king demanded proof or crucifixion, and he named his fellow as witness. The fellow denied it as impossible lest he be crucified, so the king believed him and admitted the concealer to his intimates and crucified the revealer. Then the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said as for the one who concealed him, Allāh honoured him in this world and the Hereafter, and as for the one who exposed him, Allāh humiliated him in this world and shall humiliate him in the Hereafter. Then Bakr ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī asked Abū al-Muthannā whether he heard his grandfather narrate this from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and he said yes. > **""Whoever of you conceals me, Allāh shall honour him in this world and the Hereafter; and whoever exposes me, Allāh shall humiliate him in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"18194","Aslam reported that ʿUmar performed ḥajj in the year of al-Ramādah, year sixteen, and between al-Suqyā and al-ʿAraj in the night a rider called out asking whether the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was among the company. ʿUmar asked whether he was sane, and he said reason brought him, asking whether the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had died. They said he had died, so he wept and the people wept. He asked who took charge after him, and they said the son of Abū Quḥāfah. He asked about the frail man of Banū Taym, and they said yes, then whether he was among them, and they said he had died, so he supplicated and the people supplicated. He asked who took charge after him, and they said ʿUmar, the red man of Banū ʿAdī, who was speaking to him. He asked why they had passed over the fair man of Banū Umayyah or the bald man of Banū Hāshim, and they asked his need. He said he is Abū ʿUqayl al-Juʿaylī, who met the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at the hollow of Juʿayl, accepted Islām, pledged allegiance, and drank with him a drink of sawīq of which the Prophet drank first and gave him the last. By Allāh he still finds its satiety when hungry, its coolness when thirsty, and its quenching to this day. Then he settled on the mountain al-Abʿar with his wife and daughters, praying five prayers day and night, fasting a month each year, and sacrificing on the tenth of Dhū al-Ḥijjah, as the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ taught him. This year only one ewe remained, which a wolf attacked last night, eating part and they eating part, so help, help. ʿUmar told him help had come and to stay with them at the water. ʿUmar went to the water and delayed departure for him, but he did not come. He told the keeper of the water that Abū ʿUqayl al-Juʿaylī with three daughters and his wife would come, to spend upon him and his family until he returns. When ʿUmar completed ḥajj and returned, he asked after Abū ʿUqayl. He said he came the next day feverish, sickened some nights, then died, and that is his grave. ʿUmar turned to his companions saying Allāh did not approve for him their trial, then prayed over him among the people and took his daughters and wife under his care and spent upon them. > **""I met the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, accepted Islām, pledged allegiance, and drank with him a drink whose satiety I still find. So help, help.""**"
"18195","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came out to the people of al-Ṣuffah whose voices were raised in excessive laughter, and it angered him. He said they were not created for laughter and reproached them. Then Jibrīl عليه السلام came from Allāh commanding him to make ease and not hardship, to give glad tidings and not repel. So the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ went out to them, gave them glad tidings, eased them, and was cheerful with them. > **""You were not created for laughter. Make ease and do not make hardship, give glad tidings and do not repel.""**"
"18196","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed by people laughing and asked whether they laugh while the remembrance of Paradise and Hell is among them. None of them was seen laughing thereafter till death. Then was revealed: Inform My servants that I am the Forgiving, the Merciful, and that My punishment is the painful punishment. > **""Do you laugh while the remembrance of Paradise and Hell is among you? Inform My servants that I am the Forgiving, the Merciful, and that My punishment is the painful punishment.""**"
"18197","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī reported that he entered the mosque while ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, Commander of the Faithful, was upon the minbar saying the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh revealed to a prophet among the prophets of Banū Isrāʾīl to tell the obedient of his nation not to rely upon their deeds, for He does not settle accounts on the Day of Resurrection and then wills to punish but punishes. And to tell the disobedient not to despair, for He forgives grave sins and does not care. There is no people of a village, city, land, nor a particular man or woman who is toward Him as He loves and He is toward them as they love, then turns from what He loves to what He hates, but He turns toward them from what they love to what they hate. And there is no people of a city, land, nor a particular man or woman who is toward Him as He hates, then turns toward Him from what He hates to what He loves, but He turns toward them from what they hate to what they love. None of Him is who takes omens or has omens taken, who soothsayes or has soothsaying done, who works magic or has magic worked. He is, and His creation, and all His creation belongs to Him. > **""Tell the obedient not to rely upon their deeds, and tell the disobedient not to despair, for He forgives grave sins. There is no one who turns toward Him but He turns toward him.""**"
"18198","Abū Madīnah al-Dārimī رضي الله عنه, who had companionship, reported that when two men among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ met, they did not part until one recited to the other: By time, indeed man is in loss. > **""By time, indeed man is in loss.""**"
"18199","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه and Ibn Sīrīn reported that the Prophet ﷺ said there was a man among those before them who never did any good except tawḥīd. When death approached he told his family to burn him when dead until coals, then grind him, then scatter him on a windy day. When they did so he stood in the grasp of Allāh, and Allāh the Mighty and Majestic asked what compelled him to what he did. He said fear of Thee, O Lord. So He forgave him for it, though he never did any good except tawḥīd. > **""O son of Ādam, what compelled you to what you did? Fear of Thee, O Lord. So He forgave him.""**"
"18200","Al-Ḥasan reported in a raised narration that the Prophet ﷺ said Allāh says He does not combine upon His servant two fears and two securities: if He frightens him in this world He grants him safety in the Hereafter, and if He grants him safety in this world He frightens him in the Hereafter. > **""I do not combine upon My servant two fears and two securities: if I frighten him in this world I grant him safety in the Hereafter, and if I grant him safety in this world I frighten him in the Hereafter.""**"
"18201","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported a similar narration from the Prophet ﷺ concerning two fears and two securities. > **""I do not combine upon My servant two fears and two securities.""**"
"18202","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when they are with the Prophet ﷺ they find in themselves what they love, but when they return to their families and mix with them they find themselves changed. When they mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ, he said had they remained as they are with him even in solitude, the angels would shake their hands with their wings, but it is an hour and an hour. > **""Had you remained as you are with me even in solitude, the angels would shake your hands with their wings, but it is an hour and an hour.""**"
"18203","Al-Ḥasan reported that ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ had a house set apart for discourse, and they would come to him therein. He used to say an hour for this world and an hour for the Hereafter, and Allāh knows which of the two hours prevails. > **""An hour for this world and an hour for the Hereafter.""**"
"18204","ʿAmmār رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said death suffices as an admonisher and certainty suffices as wealth. > **""Death suffices as an admonisher and certainty suffices as wealth.""**"
"18205","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ passed by a gathering laughing and said to remember much the destroyer of pleasures. He thought he added that none remembers it in straitened livelihood but it expands him, nor in plenty but it straitens it upon him. > **""Remember much the destroyer of pleasures, for none remembers it in straitness but it expands him, nor in plenty but it straitens it upon him.""**"
"18206","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه reported that a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ died, and the Companions praised him and mentioned his worship while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ remained silent. When they fell silent, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether he used to remember death much. They said no. He asked whether he used to leave much of what he desired. They said no. He said their companion did not attain much of what they ascribe. > **""Did he remember death much? Did he leave much of what he desired? Your companion did not attain much of what you ascribe.""**"
"18207","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that a man was mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ for worship and striving, and he asked how is their companion's remembrance of death. They said they do not hear him mention it. He said their companion is not there. > **""How is your companion's remembrance of death? Your companion is not there.""**"
"18208","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said O Allāh, make death beloved to whoever knows that he is Thy Messenger. > **""O Allāh, make death beloved to whoever knows that I am Thy Messenger.""**"
"18209","Ṭāriq ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muḥāribī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said O Ṭāriq, prepare for death before death. > **""O Ṭāriq, prepare for death before death.""**"
"18210","Abū Juḥayfah reported that ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, came out to them gloomy. When asked, he said the pure part of this world has gone and only the murk remains, and death today is a gift for every Muslim. > **""The pure part of this world has gone and only the murk remains, and death today is a gift for every Muslim.""**"
"18211","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, swore by Him besides Whom there is no god that there is no living soul but death is better for it if righteous, for Allāh the Mighty and Majestic says what is with Allāh is better for the righteous. And if wicked, for Allāh says let not those who disbelieve think that Our respite is better for them; We only respire them to increase in sin. > **""There is no living soul but death is better for it: what is with Allāh is better for the righteous, and respite only increases the wicked in sin.""**"
"18212","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to remember much the severer of pleasures, for none remembers it in straitness but it expands him, nor remembers it in plenty but it straitens it upon him. > **""Remember much the severer of pleasures, for none remembers it in straitness but it expands him, nor in plenty but it straitens it upon him.""**"
"18213","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to remember much the severer of pleasures, meaning death, for it was never in plenty but it lessened it, nor in little but it enriched it. > **""Remember much the severer of pleasures, meaning death, for it was never in plenty but it lessened it, nor in little but it enriched it.""**"
"18214","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ as tenth of ten, and a man of the Anṣār asked who is the shrewdest and most resolute of people. He said those who remember death most and prepare most for death before its descent; they are the shrewd, gone with the honour of this world and the nobility of the Hereafter. > **""The shrewdest are those who remember death most and prepare most for death before its descent; they are gone with the honour of this world and the nobility of the Hereafter.""**"
"18215","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh loves every sorrowful heart. > **""Allāh loves every sorrowful heart.""**"
"18216","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to hold to sorrow, for it is the key of the heart. When asked how is sorrow, he said to hunger themselves with hunger and thirst them. > **""Hold to sorrow, for it is the key of the heart. Hunger yourselves with hunger and thirst yourselves.""**"
"18217","Al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when the skin of the servant shudders from fear of Allāh, his sins fall from him as leaves fall from a withered tree. > **""When the skin of the servant shudders from fear of Allāh, his sins fall from him as leaves fall from a withered tree.""**"
"18218","Al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه also reported that they were sitting with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ beneath a tree when the wind stirred and the decayed leaves fell while the green remained. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked what is the likeness of this tree. They said Allāh and His Messenger know best. He said its likeness is the believer: when he shudders from fear of Allāh his sins fall from him and his good deeds remain. > **""Its likeness is the believer: when he shudders from fear of Allāh his sins fall from him and his good deeds remain.""**"
"18219","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came one morning saying they are ruined, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah. When asked why, they said hypocrisy, hypocrisy. He asked do they not testify that there is no god but Allāh alone without partner and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. They said yes, and he said that is not hypocrisy. They returned a second and a third time with the same, and each time he answered likewise. Then they said when they are with him they are in one state, but when they leave him the world and their families preoccupy them. He said had they remained upon leaving him as they are with him, the angels would shake their hands in the roads of Madīnah. > **""That is not hypocrisy. Had you remained upon leaving me as you are with me, the angels would shake your hands in the roads of Madīnah.""**"
"18220","Jarīr رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever takes provision from this world, it benefits him in the Hereafter. > **""Whoever takes provision from this world, it benefits him in the Hereafter.""**"
"18221","Ibn ʿUmar رضi الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that their term compared to past nations is like between the ʿAṣr prayer and sunset. In a version they were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ with the sun upon Quʿayqiʿān after ʿAṣr, and he said their lives compared to those who passed are like what remains of this day compared to what has passed of it. > **""Your term compared to past nations is like between the ʿAṣr prayer and sunset. Your lives compared to those who passed are like what remains of this day compared to what has passed of it.""**"
"18222","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ addressed his Companions one day when the sun was about to set with only a small edge remaining. He swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that what remains of this world compared to what has passed is like what remains of this day compared to what has passed of it, while they saw only a little of the sun. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, what remains of this world compared to what has passed is like what remains of this day compared to what has passed of it.""**"
"18223","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ looked at the sun at its setting upon the edges of palm fronds and said what remains of this world compared to what has passed is like what remains of this day compared to what has passed of it. > **""What remains of this world compared to what has passed is like what remains of this day compared to what has passed of it.""**"
"18224","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the Hour has drawn near, yet they only grow more distant from it. > **""The Hour has drawn near, yet they only grow more distant from it.""**"
"18225","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say he and the Hour were sent together, and it almost preceded him. In a version he said he and the Hour were sent like these two, joining his forefinger and middle finger. > **""I and the Hour were sent together, and it almost preceded me. I and the Hour were sent like these two.""**"
"18226","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that he saw the Prophet ﷺ gesturing with his two fingers saying he and the Hour were sent like this from this. > **""I and the Hour were sent like this from this.""**"
"18227","Wahb al-Suwāʾī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he and the Hour were sent like this from this, and it almost preceded it, or it almost preceded him. > **""I and the Hour were sent like this from this, and it almost preceded me.""**"
"18228","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he and the Hour were sent like these two. > **""I and the Hour were sent like these two.""**"
"18229","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he and the Hour were sent like these two. > **""I and the Hour were sent like these two.""**"
"18230","Abū Jubayrah ibn al-Ḍaḥḥāk al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said he and the Hour were sent thus, joining the forefinger and middle finger, and he preceded it as this precedes this. It is also narrated from elders of the Anṣār likewise. > **""I and the Hour were sent thus, and I preceded it as this precedes this.""**"
"18231","Al-Bazzār narrated thereof only that he was sent in the breath of the Hour. > **""I was sent in the breath of the Hour.""**"
"18232","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was sitting beneath a tree when it moved, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ rose startled. When asked, he said he thought it the Resurrection. > **""I thought it the Resurrection.""**"
"18233","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها handed the Prophet ﷺ a piece of barley bread, and he said this is the first food his father has eaten in three days. In an addition he asked what is this, and she said a disc she baked and her soul was not content till she brought him this piece. > **""This is the first food your father has eaten in three days.""**"
"18234","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها swore by Him Who sent Muḥammad with the truth that he never saw a sieve nor ate sifted bread since Allāh sent him till he was taken. When asked how they ate barley, she said they would blow, blow. > **""He never saw a sieve nor ate sifted bread since Allāh sent him till he was taken.""**"
"18235","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that flour was never sifted for the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, nor had he but a single shirt. > **""Flour was never sifted for the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, nor had he but a single shirt.""**"
"18236","Umm Salamah رضi الله عنها reported that flour was never sifted for the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at all. > **""Flour was never sifted for the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at all.""**"
"18237","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ left this world without he or his family ever satiating with barley bread. > **""He left this world without he or his family ever satiating with barley bread.""**"
"18238","ʿĀʾishah رضi الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ died while empty-bellied. > **""He died while empty-bellied.""**"
"18239","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ never satiated twice in a day till he left this world. > **""He never satiated twice in a day till he left this world.""**"
"18240","ʿĀʾishah رضi الله عنها reported that nothing used to remain upon the table of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ of barley bread, neither little nor much. > **""Nothing used to remain upon his table of barley bread, neither little nor much.""**"
"18241","It is reported in a narration that the table of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was never lifted from before him while any leftover food remained upon it. > **""The table of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was never lifted from before him while any leftover food remained upon it.""**"
"18242","This report is a fragment noting that al-Bazzār narrated part of the preceding narration concerning the table of the Prophet ﷺ."
"18243","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه swore by Allāh that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ never satiated from morning and evening meals until he met Allāh the Mighty and Majestic. > **""He never satiated from morning and evening meals until he met Allāh.""**"
"18244","ʿUthmah al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came out to them one day and a man of the Anṣār met him saying it grieves him to see his face changed and asking the cause. The Prophet ﷺ looked at the man a while then said hunger. The man hurried home, found no food, went to Banū Qurayẓah and hired himself for a date per bucket drawn until he gathered a handful of dates. He returned to find the Prophet ﷺ still in his gathering, placed them before him and said eat, O Messenger of Allāh. The Prophet ﷺ asked whence the dates came, and when told said he deemed him to love Allāh and His Messenger. The man swore by Him Who sent him with the truth that he is dearer to him than himself, his child, his family and his wealth. He then told him to endure poverty with patience and prepare armour against trial, for by Him Who sent him with the truth, they hasten to whoever loves him faster than water descends from a mountaintop to its foot. > **""Endure poverty with patience and prepare armour against trial, for by Him Who sent me with the truth, they hasten to whoever loves me faster than water descends from a mountaintop to its foot.""**"
"18245","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and saw him changed, asking why he appeared changed. He said nothing that enters the belly of a living creature had entered his belly for three days. He went and found a Jew watering his camels, drew water for a date per bucket until he gathered dates, and brought them to the Prophet ﷺ. When asked whence they came and told, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he loves him, and he said yes at the cost of his father. He told him poverty hastens to whoever loves him faster than a flood to its course, and trial shall befall him so let him prepare armour. Later the Prophet ﷺ missed him, was told he was ill, walked to visit him and said rejoice, O Kaʿb. His mother said congratulations, Paradise, O Kaʿb, so the Prophet ﷺ asked who is this woman swearing upon Allāh. When told she is his mother, he asked what makes her know, O mother of Kaʿb, for perhaps Kaʿb said what does not benefit him and withheld what would not suffice him. > **""Poverty hastens to whoever loves me faster than a flood to its course, and trial shall befall you so prepare armour for it.""**"
"18246","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that he went out on a cold hungry morning, chilled by cold, wrapped a woollen garment he had about his neck and chest for warmth. By Allāh there was nothing in his house to eat nor in the house of the Prophet ﷺ to sustain him. He went to the outskirts of Madīnah to a Jew in a garden, peered through a breach, and was offered a date per bucket. He agreed, entered, drew buckets for dates till his palms filled, said enough, ate them, drank water, then came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ in the mosque among his Companions. Then Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr appeared in a patched cloak, though he had been the most pampered youth of Makkah in comfort. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him, he recalled his former bliss and beheld his present state, and his eyes overflowed with tears. Then he asked whether they are better today or when each is served a handful of bread and meat morning and evening, robed morning and evening, and their houses veiled as the Kaʿbah is veiled. They said they would be better then, free for worship. He said rather they are better today. > **""Are you better today, or when each of you is served bread and meat morning and evening, robed morning and evening, and your houses are veiled as the Kaʿbah is veiled? Rather you today are better.""**"
"18247","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that on the day of the Trench he looked at the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and found him with a stone placed between himself and his waist-wrapper, holding his back upright from hunger. > **""He had placed a stone between himself and his waist-wrapper, holding his back upright from hunger.""**"
"18248","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that he went out to a garden where he was offered a date per bucket. He drew till his palms filled, drank sweet water, then came to the Prophet ﷺ, feeding him half and eating half himself. > **""He drew till his palms filled, then fed the Prophet ﷺ half and ate half himself.""**"
"18249","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the family of Muḥammad ﷺ never satiated with seasoned wheat bread till he passed on his way ﷺ. > **""The family of Muḥammad ﷺ never satiated with seasoned wheat bread till he passed on his way.""**"
"18250","ʿUlayy ibn Rabāḥ reported that he was at Alexandria with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ when they mentioned their livelihood, and a man of the Companions said the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ passed away while his family never satiated with ghalīth bread, meaning barley and sult mixed together. > **""He passed away while his family never satiated with ghalīth bread.""**"
"18251","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that crescent after crescent would pass over the family of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with no fire kindled in any of their houses, neither for bread nor cooking. When asked how they lived, he said by the two black ones, dates and water. They had neighbours of the Anṣār, may Allāh reward them well, with milch animals who would send them some milk. > **""Crescent after crescent would pass with no fire kindled in any of their houses. They lived by the two black ones, dates and water.""**"
"18252","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was one day with Jibrīl عليه السلام upon al-Ṣafā, and told him by Him Who sent him with the truth that the family of Muḥammad had neither a handful of flour nor a handful of sawīq by evening. No sooner had he spoken than he heard a rumble from heaven that startled him, and asked whether Allāh had commanded the Resurrection to rise. Jibrīl said no, but Allāh commanded Isrāfīl who descended upon hearing his words. Isrāfīl came and said Allāh heard what he mentioned and sent him with the keys of the treasuries of the earth, commanding him to offer to drive the mountains of Tihāmah with him as emerald, ruby, gold and silver if he wished, whether as a king-prophet or a servant-prophet. Jibrīl gestured to be humble, so he said a servant-prophet, thrice. > **""Rather a servant-prophet.""**"
"18253","ʿUlayy ibn Rabāḥ reported that he heard ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ say they morning and evening desire what the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ renounced, desiring this world while he renounced it. By Allāh no night of his life passed but what he owed exceeded what he owned. Some Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said they saw him borrow. In another wording, no three days passed but what he owed exceeded what he owned. > **""You morning and evening desire what the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ renounced. No night passed but what he owed exceeded what he owned.""**"
"18254","In a narration from ʿAmr رضي الله عنه, he said how far is their guidance from the guidance of their Prophet, for he was the most renunciant of people in this world while they are the most desirous of it. > **""How far is your guidance from the guidance of your Prophet? He was the most renunciant of people in this world, while you are the most desirous of it.""**"
"18255","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that three things of this world pleased the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ: food, women and perfume. He obtained two and did not obtain one; he obtained women and perfume and did not obtain food. > **""He obtained women and perfume and did not obtain food.""**"
"18256","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said they obtained nothing of this world of theirs except their women. > **""We obtained nothing of this world of yours except your women.""**"
"18257","Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came to her one day asking where are my two sons, meaning Ḥasan and Ḥusayn. She said they woke with nothing in the house for any taster, so ʿAlī said he would take them fearing they weep before her with nothing at hand, and went to a certain Jew. The Prophet ﷺ set out to him and found them playing in a heap with surplus dates before them. He said O ʿAlī, will you not bring back my two sons before the heat intensifies upon them. ʿAlī said they woke with nothing, so had he sat a while he would gather some dates for Fāṭimah. The Prophet ﷺ sat till some dates were gathered for Fāṭimah, placed in his bundle, then the Prophet ﷺ carried one and ʿAlī the other till they brought them back. > **""O ʿAlī, will you not bring back my two sons before the heat intensifies upon them?""**"
"18258","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Bilāl was late for dawn prayer, and the Prophet ﷺ asked what detained him. He said he passed by Fāṭimah grinding while the child wept, offered to suffice her the millstone if she sufficed him the child or vice versa. She said she is gentler with her son than him, and that detained him. He said may Allāh have mercy upon you for having mercy upon her. > **""You had mercy upon her, may Allāh have mercy upon you.""**"
"18259","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard ʿUmar say the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ went out one midday and found Abū Bakr sitting in the mosque. He asked what brought him at this hour, and he replied what brought thee. Then ʿUmar came and was asked the same, replying what brought them both. He sat with them and conversed, then asked whether they had strength to go to those palms for food and drink. They said yes, and set out to the house of Mālik ibn al-Tayyahān, Abū al-Haytham al-Anṣārī. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ went ahead and sought permission, while the mother of Abū al-Haytham heard the greeting and wished him to increase it. When he turned to leave she hastened out saying she heard his greeting but wished more of it. He asked where is Abū al-Haytham, and she said he went to fetch sweet water; enter and he shall come. She spread a mat beneath a tree till Abū al-Haytham came with his donkey bearing two waterskins. He rejoiced, greeted, climbed a palm and cut clusters. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said enough, O Abā al-Haytham, but he said eat of its unripe, ripe and dried dates. Then he brought water and they drank. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said this is of the bliss they shall be asked about. Then Abū al-Haytham rose to slaughter a ewe, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ warned against the milch one. He kneaded for them, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar laid their heads and slept till the food was ready, placed before them, and they ate to satiety. Abū al-Haytham brought the rest of the clusters and they partook. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ greeted, supplicated good for them, then told Abū al-Haytham when captives come to approach him. > **""This is of the bliss you shall be asked about. Beware the milch one.""**"
"18260","In a narration, the mother of Abū al-Haytham said had he but supplicated for them. He said fasters have broken fast with you, the righteous have eaten your food, and the angels have prayed upon you. > **""Fasters have broken fast with you, the righteous have eaten your food, and the angels have prayed upon you.""**"
"18261","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that Abū Bakr went out at midday, ʿUmar heard and went out to him asking what brought him. He swore it was the pangs of hunger in his belly, and ʿUmar swore likewise. While thus the Prophet ﷺ came out asking what brought them, and they swore it was hunger. The Prophet ﷺ swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that only that brought him. They rose to the door of Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, who had mentioned food or milk to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ but delayed that day, so his family fed it and he went to his palms to work. His wife welcomed the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and those with him, and told them Abū Ayyūb would come presently. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ turned back, Abū Ayyūb saw him working his palms, hastened till he met him, welcomed them, and said it was not the hour he used to come, then returned. He cut a cluster, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked why this. He wished them to eat of its unripe, ripe, dates and dried fruit, and to slaughter besides. He was told if slaughtering then not a milch one. He took a kid, slaughtered it, told his wife to bake while he cooked, being more skilled in bread. He cooked half the kid and grilled half, and when ready placed it before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and his Companions. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ took of the kid upon a loaf and said O Abā Ayyūb, convey this to Fāṭimah, for she has not had the like for days. When they ate to satiety the Prophet ﷺ said bread, meat, unripe dates, dates and ripe dates, and his eyes filled with tears. Then he said this is of the bliss they shall be asked about on the Day of Resurrection. That weighed upon his Companions, so he said when they obtain the like and strike with their hands, let them say in the name of Allāh, and when satiated let them say praise belongs to Allāh Who satiated them. > **""O Abā Ayyūb, convey this to Fāṭimah, for she has not had the like for days. This is of the bliss you shall be asked about on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"18262","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Quḥāfah told him that dinner escaped him one night and he tossed sleepless, resolving to go to the mosque and pray what was decreed. He leaned in a corner, ʿUmar entered, recognised him and asked what brought him. He said hunger, and ʿUmar said what brought him is what brought thee. Soon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ entered, saw their figures and asked who are these two. ʿUmar hastened saying Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. He asked what brought them, and they told him. He said only what brought them brought him, and to set out with him to al-Wāqifī. They sought permission, the woman said he went to fetch sweet water from the enclosure of Banū Ḥārithah, opened the door and they entered. Soon he came bearing a waterskin upon his back hung upon a palm stump, welcomed them warmly saying no visitors better ever visited him that night. He brought unripe dates which they picked in moonlight and ate, then took the blade and roamed the flock. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ warned against the milch or possessor of milk. He slaughtered a ewe, skinned, cut into the pot, ordered the wife to knead and bake, then brought tharīd and meat and they ate. Then he went to the waterskin cooled by wind and gave them drink. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said praise belongs to Allāh Who brought them out with only hunger and they returned not till they obtained this; this is of the bliss they shall be asked about on the Day of Resurrection. Then he asked al-Wāqifī whether he has no servant to suffice him, and he said no. He told him when first captives come to approach him for a servant. Soon captives came and he asked what brought him. He cited his promise, was told to choose among them. He asked the Prophet ﷺ to choose for him, so he said take this lad and do good to him. He told his wife what the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said, and she said he commanded good so do good. When asked what is good, she said to free him. So he is free for the sake of Allāh. > **""Praise belongs to Allāh Who brought us out with only hunger and we returned not till we obtained this; this is of the bliss you shall be asked about on the Day of Resurrection. Take this lad and do good to him.""**"
"18263","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that Abū Bakr went out with only hunger bringing him, ʿUmar went out with only hunger bringing him, and the Prophet ﷺ came out to them and they told him only hunger brought them. He said set out with him to the house of Abū al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyahān, a man of the Anṣār. He was away fetching water, his wife welcomed the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and his two companions, spread something and they sat. He asked whither Abū al-Haytham went, and she said to fetch sweet water. Soon he came with a waterskin, hung it, intended to slaughter a ewe, and the Prophet ﷺ seemed to dislike it, so he slaughtered a kid. Then he brought clusters of palms, and they ate of that meat, unripe dates and ripe dates, and drank water. One of them, either Abū Bakr or ʿUmar, asked is this of the bliss they shall be asked about. The Prophet ﷺ said the believer is not reproached for what he obtains in this world; reproach is only upon the disbeliever. > **""Set out with me to the house of Abū al-Haytham. The believer is not reproached for what he obtains in this world; reproach is only upon the disbeliever.""**"
"18264","ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah رضي الله عنه, who was at Badr, told his son that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to send them on detachments with no provision but a sack of dates, which they divided handful by handful till it came to date by date. When asked what a single date availed them, he said do not say that, O my son, for after they lost it they missed it sorely. > **""We had no provision but a sack of dates, divided handful by handful till it came to date by date; after we lost it we missed it sorely.""**"
"18265","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ went out at an hour he did not usually go out, then Abū Bakr went out and was asked what brought him. He said hunger, and he said what brought thee brought me. Then ʿUmar went out and swore by Him Who sent him with the truth that hunger brought him. Then some of his Companions walked to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, who said set out with him to the house of Abū al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyahān. When they reached it his wife said he went to fetch sweet water; go round to the garden, and she opened its gate. Abū al-Haytham came, his wife asked whether he knows who is with him, and told him the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and his Companions. He entered, hung his waterskin upon a palm, greeted, welcomed, brought fresh ripe dates and poured them before them. Then he turned to his small flock to slaughter a ewe, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said not a milch one. He took a ewe, slaughtered, skinned, jointed, cooked with water and salt, asked his wife whether she has aught, and she said some barley she had stored. They ground it together, she kneaded and baked, Abū al-Haytham broke the bread and poured the cooked meat over it, and brought it to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and his Companions and they ate. Then the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked O Abā al-Haytham, hast thou no servant. He swore by Him Who sent him with the truth they have none. He told him when captives come to approach him for a servant. Captives came, Abū al-Haytham approached with two lads before him, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told him to choose between them. Abū al-Haytham asked him to choose for him. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ bared his forearms in care and said the consulted is entrusted; O Abā al-Haytham, take this one. As he turned away with him he said O Abā al-Haytham, do good to him for he was seen praying. He agreed to feed him of what they eat, clothe him of what they wear, and not burden him beyond capacity. Abū al-Haytham went to his family who rejoiced greatly at a servant to aid their estate. He told them the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ counselled him concerning him, his wife agreed to the same terms, then remarked glory be to Allāh, shall they be deprived of a servant Allāh and His Messenger granted them. So Abū al-Haytham told the lad he is free for the sake of Allāh; if he wishes to stay they feed and clothe him and burden him only what he can, and if he wishes to go whither he wills. > **""Not a milch one. The consulted is entrusted; O Abā al-Haytham, take this one. O Abā al-Haytham, do good to him, for I saw him praying.""**"
"18266","ʿAbdullāh ibn Shafīq reported that he stayed with Abū Hurayrah in Madīnah a year, and one day by the room of ʿĀʾishah he told him he had seen them with no garments but worn cloaks, and days would pass upon them finding no food to hold their backs upright, till one would take a stone and bind it upon his belly with his garment to hold his back. > **""We had no garments but worn cloaks, and one would bind a stone upon his belly to hold his back upright.""**"
"18267","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said their food with the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ was dates and water. By Allāh they never saw this wheat of theirs nor knew what it was. Their clothing with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was striped cloaks, meaning the cloaks of the bedouins. > **""Our food with the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ was dates and water, and our clothing was striped cloaks.""**"
"18268","Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah reported from his father that they lived long with their Prophet ﷺ with no food but the two black ones. When asked whether they know what the two black ones are, he said dates and water. > **""We had no food but the two black ones: dates and water.""**"
"18269","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that two men with one need came to the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ, and one spoke. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ perceived hunger-breath from his mouth and asked will he not use the tooth-stick. He said he does, but he has tasted no food for three days. So he commanded a man concerning him, who sheltered him and fulfilled his need. > **""Will you not use the tooth-stick?""**"
"18270","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he entered with the Prophet ﷺ a garden of Madīnah, and he began eating green unripe dates, saying eat, O son of ʿUmar. When he said he does not desire them, he replied thou desirest them not, yet it is the first food the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ has eaten in four days. > **""Eat, O son of ʿUmar. It is the first food the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ has eaten in four days.""**"
"18271","In a narration the wording is in three days, concerning the first food the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had eaten. > **""It is the first food he has eaten in three days.""**"
"18272","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the family of Abū Bakr sent them a goat's trotters by night, and she held while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ cut, or she said he held and she cut. She told her narrator this was without a lamp. When asked about a lamp, she said had they oil for a lamp they would have eaten it. > **""Had we oil for a lamp we would have eaten it.""**"
"18273","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that three birds were gifted to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so he fed his servant a bird. The next day she brought it to him, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether he had not forbidden her to store aught for tomorrow, for Allāh brings the provision of every morrow. > **""Did I not forbid you to store aught for tomorrow? For Allāh brings the provision of every morrow.""**"
"18274","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that seven among the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would suck a single date, and ate tree-leaves till their jaws swelled. > **""Seven among the Companions would suck a single date, and ate tree-leaves till their jaws swelled.""**"
"18275","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sent them with Abū ʿUbaydah, six hundred and a few more, to meet the caravan of Quraysh. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ found for them no provision but a sack of dates; he would give them date by date each day, which they sucked then drank water upon, missing them sorely when exhausted. Then they turned to tree-leaves, beating them with sticks and drinking water upon them. Then he narrated the ḥadīth, saying they came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ who asked whether they have aught thereof, meaning whale flesh. They said yes, he said then feed them thereof, so they sent him dried strips and he ate it. > **""Have you aught thereof? Then feed us thereof.""**"
"18276","Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUmar reported that when a man came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with no acquaintance in Madīnah to lodge with, he lodged with the people of al-Ṣuffah, among whom he had companions. There would come to them from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ every two days two mudd of dates. While the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was in a prayer, a caller of his Companions called O Messenger of Allāh, dates have burnt their bellies and coarse cloaks worn them out. When he finished prayer he stood, praised Allāh and extolled Him, then mentioned the hardship he met from his people, saying he and his companion spent above ten days with no food but wild lote fruit till they came to their brothers of the Anṣār who consoled them with food, mostly dates and milk. By Him besides Whom there is no god, had he bread and meat for them he would feed them, and a time shall come when they wear like the veils of the Kaʿbah and are served bowls morning and evening. > **""We spent above ten days with no food but wild lote fruit. Had I bread and meat for you I would feed you, and a time shall come when bowls are brought to you morning and evening.""**"
"18277","Faḍālah al-Laythī رضي الله عنه reported that they came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, whoever had acquaintance lodged with him and whoever had none lodged at al-Ṣuffah. Having none, he lodged at al-Ṣuffah. On Friday a man called O Messenger of Allāh, dates have burnt their bellies, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said those of them who live shall soon be served bowls morning and evening and be clothed as the Kaʿbah is veiled. > **""Those of you who live shall soon be served bowls morning and evening and be clothed as the Kaʿbah is veiled.""**"
"18278","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw hunger in the faces of his Companions and said rejoice, for a time shall come upon them when each is served a bowl of tharīd morning and evening. When asked whether they are better then, he said rather they today are better than they then. > **""Rejoice, for a time shall come when each of you is served a bowl of tharīd morning and evening. Rather you today are better than you then.""**"
"18279","ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Khaṭmī reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked whether they are better today or when each is served a dish morning and another evening, robed morning and evening, and their houses clothed as the Kaʿbah is clothed. A man said they would be better then, but he said rather they today are better. > **""Are you better today, or when each is served a dish morning and evening and robed morning and evening? Rather you today are better.""**"
"18280","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said this world shall be opened upon them till they furnish their houses as the Kaʿbah is furnished. When asked while upon their religion today, he said while upon their religion today. When asked whether they are better then or today, he said rather they today are better. > **""This world shall be opened upon you till you furnish your houses as the Kaʿbah is furnished. Rather you today are better.""**"
"18281","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reported that he ate tharīd and came to the Prophet ﷺ belching before him. He said O Abā Juḥayfah, the longest in hunger on the Day of Resurrection are the most satiated in this world. > **""The longest in hunger on the Day of Resurrection are the most satiated in this world.""**"
"18282","ʿAlī ibn al-Aqmar reported from his father that he saw ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib عرض a sword of his in the square of Kūfah saying who buys this sword of mine. By Allāh he had dispelled therewith many a distress from the face of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and had he the price of a waist-wrapper he would not sell it. > **""Who buys this sword of mine? By Allāh I dispelled therewith many a distress from the face of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ.""**"
"18283","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that they were on a raid of theirs when they met some polytheists and drove them from a bread of theirs, falling upon it and eating. They used to hear in Jāhiliyyah that whoever ate bread fattens, so when they ate that bread each began looking at his sides whether he had fattened. > **""They drove them from a bread and ate, each looking at his sides whether he had fattened.""**"
"18284","In a narration they were at Khaybar with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when they drove them from a loaf of theirs of fine flour. > **""They drove them from a loaf of fine flour at Khaybar.""**"
"18285","A man of ʿAbs reported that he was travelling with Salmān upon the bank of the Tigris, who told him O brother of Banū ʿAbs, dismount and drink. When he drank he asked how much his drink diminished the Tigris. When he said what could it diminish, he said so is knowledge, taken without diminishing. Then he said mount, and they passed heaps of wheat and barley. He asked whether he sees this opened for them while straitened upon the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, for good for them and evil for them, or words to that effect. > **""Knowledge is taken without diminishing, as your drink does not diminish the Tigris.""**"
"18286","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها reported that she was in one of the rooms of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ while he was there, when a man came complaining of need. He said be patient, for by Allāh there is nothing in the family of Muḥammad for seven nights, nor has fire been kindled beneath their pot for three. By Allāh had he asked Allāh to make the mountains of Tihāmah all gold He would have done so. > **""Be patient, for by Allāh there is nothing in the family of Muḥammad for seven nights, nor has fire been kindled beneath their pot for three.""**"
"18287","Al-Shifāʾ bint ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنها reported that she came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asking, and he kept excusing himself while she reproached him. Prayer came, so she went out to her daughter under Shuraḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah, finding Shuraḥbīl at home. When she reproached him for staying home at prayer, he said O aunt, blame me not, for he had a garment which the Prophet ﷺ borrowed. > **""I had a garment which the Prophet ﷺ borrowed.""**"
"18288","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that she knows best the most wealth that came to the Prophet ﷺ till Allāh took him: there came in the night a purse with eight hundred dirhams and a sheet, which he sent to her as it was her night. After the last ʿishāʾ he returned, prayed in his prayer place in the room while she prepared bedding for him and herself, waiting long. He kept going out and returning likewise till dawn call or summons, prayed and returned asking where is the purse that kept him in trial last night. He summoned it and divided it. When asked why he did what he never did, he said it came while praying; he finished, saw it, then resumed praying. > **""Where is the purse that kept me in trial last night?""**"
"18289","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say O people, fear Allāh, for if you fear Allāh He shall satiate you with the oil of Syria and the wheat of Syria. > **""O people, fear Allāh, for if you fear Allāh He shall satiate you with the oil of Syria and the wheat of Syria.""**"
"18290","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, said they pray more and strive more than the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, yet they were better than them. When asked why, O Abā ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, he said they were more renunciant in this world and more desirous of the Hereafter. > **""They were more renunciant in this world and more desirous of the Hereafter.""**"
"18291","ʿAlī ibn Badhīmah reported that the goods of Salmān were sold, reaching fourteen dirhams. > **""The goods of Salmān were sold, reaching fourteen dirhams.""**"
"18292","Salmā, wife of Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنها, reported that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās came to her asking her to make food of what the Prophet ﷺ liked to eat. She said O my sons, ye shall not desire it today. She rose, took barley, ground, winnowed, made a loaf with oil as condiment, sprinkled pepper upon it, served them and said the Prophet ﷺ loved this. > **""The Prophet ﷺ loved this.""**"
"18293","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه said had they seen them with their Prophet ﷺ they would deem their scent the scent of sheep, for their clothing was wool and their food the two black ones, dates and water. > **""Our clothing was wool and our food the two black ones, dates and water.""**"
"18294","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was brought a cup with milk and honey, and said two drinks in one drink and two condiments in one cup, he has no need of it. He does not deem it unlawful, but dislikes that Allāh ask him about the luxuries of this world on the Day of Resurrection. He humbles himself to Allāh; whoever humbles himself to Allāh, Allāh raises him; whoever is proud, Allāh abases him; whoever is moderate, Allāh enriches him; and whoever remembers death much, Allāh loves him. > **""Two drinks in one drink and two condiments in one cup, I have no need of it. I humble myself to Allāh; whoever humbles himself to Allāh, Allāh raises him.""**"
"18295","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that new moons would pass over the family of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with no lamp lit in a single house of them nor fire kindled. If they found oil they anointed therewith, and if they found fat they ate it. > **""New moons would pass with no lamp lit nor fire kindled; if they found oil they anointed therewith, and if they found fat they ate it.""**"
"18296","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say the dearest to him and nearest from him is he who joins him upon what he covenanted him. > **""The dearest to me and nearest from me is he who joins me upon what I covenanted him.""**"
"18297","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said it would not please him that Uḥud be gold for him spent in the path of Allāh, dying the day he dies leaving a dīnār thereof except a dīnār prepared for a creditor if any. So the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ died leaving neither dīnār nor dirham nor slave nor bondmaid, leaving his armour pawned for thirty ṣāʿ of barley. > **""It would not please me that Uḥud be gold for me spent in the path of Allāh, dying leaving a dīnār thereof except a dīnār prepared for a creditor.""**"
"18298","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that he entered upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ lying upon a bed laced with cord, beneath his head a pillow of hide stuffed with fibre. Some of his Companions entered, and ʿUmar entered. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ shifted, and ʿUmar saw no garment between his side and the cord, its mark upon his side, so he wept. The Prophet ﷺ asked what makes thee weep, O ʿUmar. He said by Allāh he weeps not unaware that he is nobler to Allāh than Kisrā and Caesar living in luxury while he, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, lies thus. The Prophet ﷺ asked is he not pleased that theirs be this world and ours the Hereafter. ʿUmar said yes, and he said so it is. > **""Are you not pleased that theirs be this world and ours the Hereafter? So it is.""**"
"18299","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that ʿUmar entered upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ while he was upon a mat that marked his body, suggesting he take a softer bed. He said what has he to do with this world. By Him in Whose Hand is his soul, his likeness and the likeness of this world is but as a rider journeying on a hot day, shading beneath a tree a while, then departing and leaving it. > **""What have I to do with this world? My likeness and the likeness of this world is but as a rider shading beneath a tree a while, then departing and leaving it.""**"
"18300","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ in an upper room as though a bathhouse, sleeping upon a mat that marked his side, so he wept. He asked what makes thee weep, O ʿAbdullāh. He said O Messenger of Allāh, Kisrā and Caesar recline upon brocade, dībāj and silk while thou sleepest upon this mat that marked thy side. He said weep not, O ʿAbdullāh, for theirs is this world and ours the Hereafter. What has he to do with this world; his likeness and the likeness of this world is but as a rider alighting beneath a tree, then journeying and leaving it. > **""Weep not, O ʿAbdullāh, for theirs is this world and ours the Hereafter.""**"
"18301","Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that a tree struck the finger of the Prophet ﷺ and it bled, so he said art thou but a finger that bled and suffered in the path of Allāh. He was carried and laid upon a bed laced with palm fibre or cord, beneath his head a cushion of hide stuffed with fibre, and the cord marked his side. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came and wept. When asked what makes thee weep, he said O Messenger of Allāh, Kisrā and Caesar sit upon golden couches wearing dībāj and istabraq. He asked are they not pleased that theirs be this world and theirs the Hereafter. > **""Art thou but a finger that bled and suffered in the path of Allāh? Are you not pleased that theirs be this world and yours the Hereafter?""**"
"18302","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had a bed latticed with palm stalks, over it a black cloak. They seated him upon the stalks, then Abū Bakr and ʿUmar entered with the Prophet ﷺ sitting thereon, seeing the bed's mark upon his side, so they wept. When the Prophet ﷺ asked what makes them weep, they said its roughness marked his side while Kisrā and Caesar lie upon silk and dībāj. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the end of Kisrā and Caesar is to the Fire, and the end of this bed of his is to Paradise. > **""The end of Kisrā and Caesar is to the Fire, and the end of this bed of mine is to Paradise.""**"
"18303","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ kept away from his wives for a month. ʿUmar came to him upon a mat that marked his side, saying Kisrā and Caesar drink in gold and silver while thou art thus. The Prophet ﷺ said their good things were hastened to them in their worldly life. The Prophet ﷺ also said the month is twenty-nine, thus and thus and thus, folding the thumb on the third. > **""Their good things were hastened to them in their worldly life. The month is twenty-nine, thus and thus and thus.""**"
"18304","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that he came to Fāṭimah complaining of his chest from drawing with the bucket, and she swore by Allāh she complains of her hand from grinding with the mill. ʿAlī told her to approach the Prophet ﷺ and ask him for a servant. She went, greeted him, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked what brought her. She said she came to greet him, and returning to ʿAlī said by Allāh awe of him kept her from speaking. They went together, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ asked what brought them, sure they had a need. ʿAlī said yes, complaining of drawing and grinding, asking for a servant of what Allāh granted him. He said no, but he spends upon the people of al-Ṣuffah whose livers are folded with hunger, finding nothing to feed them. When they returned and lay at night in the coverlet he had furnished her with a pillow stuffed with idhkhir, and the refusal weighed upon them, the Prophet ﷺ came upon hearing his footfall. When they rose he said stay in place, sat upon the edge of the coverlet, and said they came for a servant but he shall guide them to what is better than a servant: to praise Allāh ten times, glorify ten, magnify ten after every prayer, and when lying at night to glorify three and thirty, praise three and thirty, and magnify four and thirty, making a hundred. > **""Stay in your place. I shall guide you to what is better for you than a servant: praise Allāh ten times, glorify ten, and magnify ten after every prayer, and thirty-three and thirty-three and thirty-four when you lie at night.""**"
